OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026", "title": "Strong diffeomorphic approximation in three dimensions for p>2", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Strong-diffeomorphic-approximation-in-three-dimensions-for-p-gt-2-September-24-2026/main.pdf", "family": "368", "family_title": "The three-dimensional Ball–Evans approximation problem", "abstract_html": "We resolve the three-dimensional Ball–Evans approximation problem for every finite <i>p</i> &amp;gt; 2. Every \\(W^{1,p}\\) homeomorphism between arbitrary bounded domains in ℝ<sup>3</sup> can be approximated strongly in \\(W^{1,p}\\) by smooth diffeomorphisms onto the same target.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist \\(C^\\infty\\) diffeomorphisms \\(f_j:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\), each belonging to \\(W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{3})\\), such that \\( \\int_\\Omega\\bigl(|f_j-f|^p+|Df_j-Df|^p\\bigr)\\,dx\\longrightarrow0. \\) No boundary …", "statement": "<p>**Strong diffeomorphic approximation in dimension three — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Omega,\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R^{3}\\) are bounded domains, where a domain is a nonempty connected open set.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(2&lt;p&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(f:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) is a homeomorphism belonging to \\(W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{3})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A diffeomorphism and its inverse are required to be smooth on their open domains; no boundary extension is implicit.</p><p class=\"li\">Matrix norms denoted by \\(|\\cdot|\\) are Frobenius norms.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(C^\\infty\\) diffeomorphisms \\(f_j:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\), each belonging to \\(W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{3})\\), such that\n\\[\n\\int_\\Omega\\bigl(|f_j-f|^p+|Df_j-Df|^p\\bigr)\\,dx\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\nNo boundary regularity, boundary extension of \\(f\\), inverse Sobolev regularity, or nonvanishing Jacobian is assumed. The conclusion concerns forward Sobolev convergence, not Sobolev convergence of the inverses or convergence of energies with singular determinant penalties.</p>", "proof": "<p>Reflect the target if necessary to arrange positive topological orientation. Fix a desired positive error and carry out Proposition 20's ordered selections with sufficient room. Proposition 21 provides actual pre-stacks, so Proposition 14 gives \\(h\\), occupied intervals, and fine label pinning. Put\n\\[\nu=P_0Th.\n\\]</p><p>On saturation interiors, Proposition 22 gives the sharp derivative-error bound. On the exterior residual, full-rank shells, and weighted marked collars, Lemma 20.2 gives arbitrarily small derivative energy. The original \\(|Df|^p\\)-cost there is also small: saturation patches capture the positive deficient ranks, reserved inner cubes capture full rank up to the chosen tail, rank-zero derivatives vanish, and enlarged marked cubes and correction bands were paid initially.</p><p>Use Proposition 15 and the strict quantizers of Proposition 10 to choose\n\\[\nk=P^\\epsilon T_\\delta h\n\\]\nwith summably small local derivative and value errors on every test, including finite full-rank tests and weighted marked collars. It is a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism onto \\(\\Lambda\\). No global energy bound is yet required on exempt marked interiors.</p><p>For \\(2&lt;p&lt;3\\), apply Proposition 8 using the transferred weighted collar budgets. Select slightly outer cube boundaries enclosing all exempt interiors. After \\(k\\) is fixed, choose each inner compression parameter \\(\\alpha\\) so that its finite interior Lipschitz constant is absorbed by \\(\\alpha^{3-p}\\). Then choose \\(\\rho\\downarrow0\\) at the selected radial Lebesgue boundary. The outer energy is controlled by the facet term times\n\\[\n\\int_\\alpha^1t^{2-p}\\,dt\\le(3-p)^{-1},\n\\]\nand slice selection controls the facet term by weighted collar volume energy. Use summable choices, including correction-shell weights. Changes avoid saturation patches and preserve fine values through small original cube oscillations. Call the result \\(k_1\\). For \\(p\\ge3\\), there are no marked cubes and set \\(k_1=k\\).</p><p>Now \\(k_1\\in W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)\\): residual, saturation, shell, and marked-interior costs are summably bounded; unpaid full-rank interiors lie in a finite compact set, where local bi-Lipschitzness gives finite energy. Bounded target values and finite source measure give the value integrability.</p><p>Smooth \\(k_1\\) by Proposition 3, preserving strict full-rank value and band-energy tests. Apply Proposition 7's finite smooth cubical jet corrections. Their exact inner cubes have derivative error fixed by (7.3), and modified shells have arbitrarily small error from the buffered budgets and finite jet factors. Images are preserved, and supports avoid saturation patches. Smooth once more by Proposition 3 with arbitrarily small additional strong error.</p><p>Every operation preserves the fixed target: strict target maps are self-homeomorphisms of \\(\\Lambda\\); wall realization is onto; marked compression is source precomposition; cubical replacements preserve old local images; smoothing preserves source and target. The final smooth bijection has everywhere invertible derivative, hence a smooth inverse.</p><p>For the value integral, choose a compact subset of \\(\\Omega\\) with small-measure complement, impose small uniform error there, and bound the complement by the fixed diameter of \\(\\Lambda\\). Full-rank and marked supports were chosen with the necessary small oscillations.</p><p>The final derivative error is\n\\[\nC_p(\\eta+\\gamma)(1+E)+o(1),\n\\]\nwhere later errors are prescribed only after their multipliers are fixed. Choose \\(\\eta,\\gamma\\) first, then tails against \\(C_w\\), then all freely small errors in the recorded order. Allocate the remaining shares to values and smoothing. This gives any desired positive bound in (1.1). Repeat with error \\(2^{-j}\\) and undo the reflection.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a \\(C^\\infty\\) diffeomorphism \\(g:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) such that \\( \\|g-h\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}<\\varepsilon, \\qquad |g(x)-h(x)|<\\xi(x)\\quad(x\\in\\Omega). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Strong smoothing of a locally finite PL homeomorphism — original Proposition A.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Omega,\\Omega'\\subset\\mathbb R^{3}\\) are bounded domains.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(1\\le p&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) is a locally finite PL homeomorphism in \\(W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^{3})\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and \\(\\xi:\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)\\) is continuous.</p><p class=\"li\">Local finiteness and compactness are relative to the open domains.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a \\(C^\\infty\\) diffeomorphism \\(g:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|g-h\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}&lt;\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n|g(x)-h(x)|&lt;\\xi(x)\\quad(x\\in\\Omega).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemmas 1.3 and 1.4 with total Sobolev error below \\(\\varepsilon\\). At each operation impose value tolerances whose sum is less than\n\\[\n\\min\\left\\{\\xi(x),\\frac12\\operatorname{dist}(h(x),\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\Omega')\\right\\}.\n\\]\nLemma 1.1 permits these simultaneous local choices. The resulting smooth map has positive determinant, and Lemma 1.2 makes it a diffeomorphism onto the original target. Undo the initial reflection if required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a positive \\(L\\)-Lipschitz function \\(d\\) on \\(U\\) satisfying \\( d(x)\\le\\min\\{a(x),1,L\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\mathbb R^n\\setminus U)\\}. \\) There are positive smooth minorants satisfying prescribed positive …", "statement": "<p>**Local tolerances — original Lemma 1.2 and Remark 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(U\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) is open.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(a:U\\to(0,\\infty)\\) is continuous.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(L&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a positive \\(L\\)-Lipschitz function \\(d\\) on \\(U\\) satisfying\n\\[\nd(x)\\le\\min\\{a(x),1,L\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\mathbb R^n\\setminus U)\\}.\n\\]\nThere are positive smooth minorants satisfying prescribed positive continuous upper bounds on their values and first derivatives. Positive constant upper bounds may also be imposed on a locally finite family of compact regional tests. Finite collections of requirements can be combined, and error budgets on a countable compact exhaustion can be made summable.</p>", "proof": "<p>Extend\n\\[\ne(x)=\\min\\{a(x),1,L\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\mathbb R^n\\setminus U)\\}\n\\]\nby zero outside \\(U\\), and put\n\\[\nd(x)=\\inf_{y\\in\\mathbb R^n}\\{e(y)+L|x-y|\\}.\n\\]\nThis is \\(L\\)-Lipschitz and bounded above by \\(e\\). At an interior point \\(x\\), \\(e\\) has a positive minimum on a small closed ball, while outside that ball the distance term has a positive lower bound. Thus \\(d(x)&gt;0\\).</p><p>For a smooth minorant, take a smooth locally finite partition of unity \\(\\{\\chi_i\\}\\), with compact supports in \\(U\\), and set \\(b=\\sum_i c_i\\chi_i\\). Given positive continuous value and derivative bounds \\(v,w\\), choose\n\\[\nc_i\\le2^{-i-2}\\min\\left\\{\n\\inf_{\\operatorname{supp}\\chi_i}v,\\,\n\\frac{\\inf_{\\operatorname{supp}\\chi_i}w}{1+\\|D\\chi_i\\|_\\infty}\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThen \\(b&gt;0\\), \\(b\\le v\\), and \\(|Db|\\le w\\). Take minima for finitely many requirements. For regional tests use a locally finite compact cover with compact enlargements and allocate errors by a convergent positive series.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(g\\) is a diffeomorphism of \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Omega'\\).", "statement": "<p>**Fine control, properness, and the target — original Lemma A.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(h:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) is an orientation-preserving homeomorphism between bounded domains.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(g:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3\\) is smooth and \\(\\det Dg&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For every \\(x\\in\\Omega\\),\n\\[\n|g(x)-h(x)|&lt;\\frac12\\operatorname{dist}(h(x),\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\Omega').\n\\tag{A.4}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(g\\) is a diffeomorphism of \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Omega'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(d(y)=\\operatorname{dist}(y,\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\Omega')\\) and \\(g_t=(1-t)h+tg\\). The entire segment lies in \\(\\Omega'\\). Since \\(d\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz,\n\\[\nd(g_t(x))\\le\\frac32d(h(x)).\n\\]\nIf \\(K\\Subset\\Omega'\\) and \\(m=\\min_K d&gt;0\\), the inverse image of \\(K\\) under the homotopy lies in\n\\[\nh^{-1}\\bigl(\\{y\\in\\Omega':d(y)\\ge2m/3\\}\\bigr)\\times[0,1].\n\\]\nThis is compact because \\(\\Omega'\\) is bounded. Hence \\(g_t\\) is a proper homotopy and \\(\\deg g=\\deg h=1\\). A proper local diffeomorphism has finitely many preimages of each point, all with positive local degree. Consequently every point has exactly one preimage. The local smooth inverses patch together.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One can modify \\(h\\) in mutually disjoint open-edge tubes, with arbitrarily small summable value and \\(W^{1,p}\\) errors, to a continuous locally Lipschitz map \\(h_1\\) that:", "statement": "<p>**Edge smoothing — the open-edge argument in the proof of original Proposition A.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">After reflecting the target if necessary, \\(h\\) is orientation-preserving.</p><p class=\"li\">A locally finite affine triangulation for \\(h\\) is fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Around an open edge, \\(t\\in(0,l)\\) denotes axial position and \\((r,\\theta)\\) are transverse polar coordinates.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can modify \\(h\\) in mutually disjoint open-edge tubes, with arbitrarily small summable value and \\(W^{1,p}\\) errors, to a continuous locally Lipschitz map \\(h_1\\) that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. is nonsingular and affine near every open edge;</p><p class=\"li num\">2. has positive one-sided determinants on its remaining sector pieces;</p><p class=\"li num\">3. fixes the vertices;</p><p class=\"li num\">4. is homogeneous of degree one relative to \\(h_1(v)\\) on a sufficiently small ball about each vertex \\(v\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose tube radii \\(d(t)\\) bounded by a small multiple of \\(\\min(t,l-t)\\), exactly linear near the endpoints, with \\(\\|d'\\|_\\infty\\) small. In positively oriented orthogonal frames the original map is\n\\[\n(t,r,\\theta)\\longmapsto\n\\bigl(at+rb(\\theta),\\,rs(\\theta)e_{\\varphi(\\theta)}\\bigr),\n\\qquad a&gt;0,\\ s&gt;0.\n\\tag{A.5}\n\\]\nIts transverse homogeneous map is injective: equality of transverse images at arbitrarily small radius would permit cancellation of the axial difference by changing \\(t\\), contradicting injectivity. Thus its angular map is an orientation-preserving circle homeomorphism. Choose a lift with\n\\(\\varphi(\\theta+2\\pi)=\\varphi(\\theta)+2\\pi\\). Positive sector determinants give positive lower bounds for \\(s\\) and the one-sided derivatives \\(\\varphi'\\).</p><p>For \\(0\\le\\tau\\le1\\), set\n\\[\nb_\\tau=(1-\\tau)b,\\qquad\ns_\\tau=(1-\\tau)s+\\tau c,\\qquad\n\\varphi_\\tau=(1-\\tau)\\varphi+\\tau(\\theta+c'),\n\\]\nwhere \\(c&gt;0\\). For fixed \\(\\tau\\), the determinant in the frame\n\\((\\partial_t,\\partial_r,r^{-1}\\partial_\\theta)\\) is\n\\[\nas_\\tau^2\\varphi_\\tau'&gt;0.\n\\tag{A.6}\n\\]\nSubstitute\n\\[\n\\tau(t,r)=\\chi\\!\\left(\\frac{\\log(r/d(t))}{N}\\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\chi=1\\) on \\((-\\infty,-1]\\) and \\(\\chi=0\\) on \\([-1/2,\\infty)\\). The additional derivative columns are bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac C N(1+\\|d'\\|_\\infty),\n\\tag{A.7}\n\\]\nbecause \\(|r\\partial_r\\tau|\\le C/N\\) and\n\\(|r\\partial_t\\tau|\\le C(r/d)|d'|/N\\). Large \\(N\\) preserves positive determinants. Near the axis the map is\n\\((t,r,\\theta)\\mapsto(at,cr e_{\\theta+c'})\\), and near the tube boundary it is unchanged.</p><p>The derivative bound is independent of a further common decrease of \\(d\\). Shrinking the tubes therefore makes their derivative and value costs summably small. Exact linearity of their widths near endpoints gives the asserted vertex homogeneity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a smooth map \\(g:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3\\) with \\(\\det Dg>0\\), arbitrarily close to \\(h_1\\) in the prescribed total \\(W^{1,p}\\) and fine value errors.", "statement": "<p>**Face smoothing and homogeneous vertex completion — the remaining local arguments in original Proposition A.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(h_1\\) is the map supplied by Lemma 1.3.</p><p class=\"li\">Summable local error budgets and fine value tolerances are prescribed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth map \\(g:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3\\) with \\(\\det Dg&gt;0\\), arbitrarily close to \\(h_1\\) in the prescribed total \\(W^{1,p}\\) and fine value errors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Away from vertices, the closed convex hull of nearby almost-everywhere gradients lies in \\(GL^+(3)\\). At a remaining face, the two traces \\(A_-,A_+\\) agree on the tangent plane, so their difference has rank at most one and\n\\[\n\\det((1-t)A_-+tA_+)\n=(1-t)\\det A_-+t\\det A_+&gt;0.\n\\tag{A.8}\n\\]\nContinuity on the two sides gives the same assertion for nearby gradients. Homogeneity makes this valid uniformly at relative scale on punctured vertex balls.</p><p>For a smooth probability kernel \\(\\rho\\), define away from vertices\n\\[\nh_2(x)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}h_1(x+w(x)z)\\rho(z)\\,dz.\n\\tag{A.9}\n\\]\nIts derivative is\n\\[\nDh_2=\n\\int Dh_1(x+wz)\\rho(z)\\,dz+\n\\int (Dh_1(x+wz)z)\\otimes Dw(x)\\rho(z)\\,dz.\n\\tag{A.10}\n\\]\nChoose \\(w&gt;0\\) and \\(|Dw|\\) sufficiently small locally. The first integral belongs to the positive-determinant convex hull, and the second is a sufficiently small perturbation. Near a vertex \\(v\\), take \\(w(x)=c_v|x-v|\\); blend this with the other radius choices on disjoint annuli. The resulting \\(h_2-h_1(v)\\) is homogeneous on a smaller ball.</p><p>Choose vertex balls with summably small volume times the fixed local gradient bounds. Outside them, dominated convergence as \\(w,|Dw|\\to0\\), on a locally finite compact cover, gives the required derivative error. Local Lipschitz continuity gives the value error. This includes \\(p=1\\).</p><p>Center a homogeneous vertex ball at zero and subtract \\(h_1(v)\\), writing the map as \\(F\\). Euler's identity and invertibility give \\(F(x)\\ne0\\) for \\(x\\ne0\\). Hence\n\\[\nF(r\\theta)=rR(\\theta)\\Phi(\\theta),\\qquad R&gt;0,\\quad \\theta\\in S^2.\n\\tag{A.11}\n\\]\nPositive determinant makes \\(\\Phi\\) an orientation-preserving local diffeomorphism of \\(S^2\\), hence a one-sheeted covering. Smale's theorem provides a jointly smooth isotopy \\(\\Phi_\\tau\\) to a rotation. Interpolate \\(R\\) through positive functions to a positive constant. The homogeneous family has determinant\n\\[\nR_\\tau(\\theta)^3J_{S^2}\\Phi_\\tau(\\theta)&gt;0,\n\\]\nbounded first derivatives, and a positive determinant minimum. Replace \\(F\\) on a small ball by \\(F_{\\tau(r)}\\), spreading the transition over a sufficiently long interval of \\(\\log r\\) that \\(\\sup_r|r\\tau'(r)|\\) is small. The extra derivative is bounded by this quantity. Near zero the map is a dilation followed by a rotation. Shrinking the support gives arbitrarily small summable errors.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a locally finite PL homeomorphism \\(G:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) such that \\( \\|G-H\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}<\\varepsilon, \\qquad |G(x)-H(x)|<\\xi(x)\\quad(x\\in\\Omega). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Strong PL approximation of a locally bi-Lipschitz map — original Proposition A.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Omega,\\Omega'\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\) are bounded domains.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(1\\le p&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) is a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism in \\(W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and \\(\\xi:\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)\\) is continuous.</p><p class=\"li\">A coarse locally finite Whitney decomposition \\(\\mathcal P\\) has compactly contained enlarged neighborhoods \\(U_P\\). Constants \\(K_P\\ge1\\), enlarged using finitely many neighboring coarse cubes, satisfy\n\\[\nK_P^{-1}|x-y|\\le |H(x)-H(y)|\\le K_P|x-y|\n\\quad(x,y\\in U_P),\n\\tag{A.12}\n\\]\nand also bound \\(|DH|\\) almost everywhere there.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a locally finite PL homeomorphism \\(G:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|G-H\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}&lt;\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad |G(x)-H(x)|&lt;\\xi(x)\\quad(x\\in\\Omega).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use two rounds of \\(n_0\\) color stages, uniformly over all admissible meshes. A new protected patch has radius \\(2\\) in \\(B_Q\\)-coordinates; at each later stage reduce older radii by\n\\[\nd_*=(4n_0+4)^{-1}.\n\\tag{A.17}\n\\]\nFinal radii remain greater than \\(3/2\\). Use half the current decrease for model agreement and the full decrease for the fixed older sets. Choose all value and insertion tolerances backwards using Lemma 2.4.</p><p>Choose \\(\\delta_P&gt;0\\) so that\n\\[\n\\sum_P(C\\delta_P)^p|U_P|&lt;\\varepsilon^p/8,\\qquad\nC\\delta_P&lt;(2K_P)^{-1}.\n\\tag{A.18}\n\\]\nRound every fine-triangulation value \\(H(v)\\) to a target dyadic grid whose spacing is a sufficiently small fixed dyadic multiple of the smallest incident cube size. Interpolate to obtain a continuous piecewise affine map \\(L\\), not asserted globally injective.</p><p>Call \\(Q\\) good if some affine \\(A_Q\\), of bi-Lipschitz constant at most \\(2K_P\\), satisfies\n\\[\n\\sup_{B_Q(20)}|H-A_Q|\\le\\delta_Pl_Q,\\qquad\n\\left(\\frac1{|Q|}\\int_Q|DH-DA_Q|^p\\right)^{1/p}\\le\\delta_P.\n\\tag{A.19}\n\\]\nFinite mesh shapes and rounded vertex errors give\n\\[\n|DL-DA_Q|\\le C\\delta_P\\text{ near }B_Q(3),\\qquad\n\\sup_{B_Q(3)}|L-H|\\le C\\delta_Pl_Q.\n\\tag{A.20}\n\\]\nThe first estimate makes \\(L\\) an embedding there, because \\(L-A_Q\\) has Lipschitz constant below \\((2K_P)^{-1}\\). In the first round insert \\(L\\) only on good cubes. Older overlaps agree because all installed models are restrictions of this one \\(L\\). Lemma 2.4 supplies each insertion.</p><p>For normalized data choose \\(m_Q\\in\\mathbb Z^3\\) nearest to \\(H(c_Q)/l_Q\\), and write\n\\[\n\\widehat H_Q(z)=\\frac{H(c_Q+l_Qz)-l_Qm_Q}{l_Q}.\n\\tag{A.21}\n\\]\nThese maps form uniformly bounded equicontinuous families locally. Normalized rounded vertex values have finitely many possibilities. For interacting cubes,\n\\[\n\\frac{l_Rm_R-l_Qm_Q}{l_Q}\n\\tag{A.22}\n\\]\nis a bounded dyadic vector with denominator from a finite list. The source placements, scale ratios, and protected radii also have finite lists.</p><p>In the second round insert a model on every cube. Before selecting the final mesh, choose finite sup-norm bins for the normalized \\(H\\)-family at each stage. Record the older model labels, normalized placements, translations, and protected radii. In \\(Q\\)-coordinates an older model is\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto\n\\frac{l_R}{l_Q}\\widehat h_R\n\\left(\\frac{c_Q-c_R}{l_R}+\\frac{l_Q}{l_R}z\\right)\n+\\frac{l_Rm_R-l_Qm_Q}{l_Q}.\n\\tag{A.23}\n\\]\nThus there are finitely many combinations of source configuration, normalized \\(H\\)-bin, and exact protected-model records.</p><p>For every occurring combination, choose one representative intermediate embedding \\(G^*\\), close to a representative \\(H^*\\), realizing those records. It is PL near the compact union of half-shrunk overlaps in \\([-3,3]^3\\). Lemma 2.1 approximates \\(G^*\\) by a PL embedding while preserving those overlaps. Store its restriction to a fixed neighborhood of \\([-3,3]^3\\). For any actual tuple in the same combination,\n\\[\n\\|h^*-\\widehat H_Q\\|_\\infty\n\\le\\|h^*-G^*\\|_\\infty+\\|G^*-H^*\\|_\\infty\n+\\|H^*-\\widehat H_Q\\|_\\infty\n&lt;3a_{s,P}/8.\n\\tag{A.25}\n\\]\nLemma 2.4 therefore installs it while preserving older fully-shrunk cores.</p><p>Only the \\(H\\)-family is binned; intermediate maps are existence witnesses. Libraries depend only on strictly earlier stages in finitely many neighboring coarse indices. Their derivative bounds are consequently fixed before the final resolution. Every cube retains its installed model on a neighborhood, so\n\\[\n|DG|\\le M_P&lt;\\infty\\quad\\text{a.e. on }Q,\\quad P=P(Q),\n\\tag{A.26}\n\\]\nwith \\(M_P\\) independent of subsequent refinement. The final map is locally PL and onto \\(\\Omega'\\), since each stage is precomposition by a domain homeomorphism. On good cubes it still equals \\(L\\).</p><p>Almost every point is a differentiability point of \\(H\\) and a Lebesgue point of \\(DH\\), with invertible derivative. Its affine differential passes (A.19) on every sufficiently small cube containing that point. Therefore the union of bad cubes with coarse index \\(P\\) tends to zero in measure, uniformly over admissible meshes. Only now reduce local scales until, for summable \\(b_P&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n(M_P+K_P)^p\n\\left|\\bigcup_{\\substack{Q\\text{ bad}\\\\P(Q)=P}}Q\\right|&lt;b_P,\n\\qquad \\sum_Pb_P&lt;\\varepsilon^p/8.\n\\tag{A.27}\n\\]\nFurther reduce scales to meet \\(\\xi\\) and the value integral budget. On good cubes use (A.19)–(A.20); on bad cubes use (A.26) and (A.12). Summation gives the prescribed derivative error. Local Lipschitzness, globally integrable derivatives, and bounded target values establish global \\(W^{1,p}\\) membership.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a PL homeomorphism \\(f_1:M\\to N\\), agreeing with \\(f\\) on a neighborhood of \\(K\\), such that \\( d_N(f_1(x),f(x))<\\eta(x). \\) Noncompact manifolds are allowed.", "statement": "<p>**Fine relative PL approximation — original Lemma A.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M,N\\) are PL three-manifolds without boundary, with \\(N\\) metrized.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(f:M\\to N\\) is a homeomorphism.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\subset M\\) is closed, and \\(f\\) is PL on a neighborhood of \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\eta:M\\to(0,\\infty)\\) is continuous.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a PL homeomorphism \\(f_1:M\\to N\\), agreeing with \\(f\\) on a neighborhood of \\(K\\), such that\n\\[\nd_N(f_1(x),f(x))&lt;\\eta(x).\n\\]\nNoncompact manifolds are allowed. For manifolds with boundary, the same conclusion holds when \\(K\\) contains \\(\\partial M\\) and \\(f\\) is PL near \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a closed neighborhood \\(K^+\\) of \\(K\\) inside the PL region. Transport the PL structure of \\(N\\) to \\(M\\) by \\(f\\). The identity between the two structures is PL near \\(K^+\\). Hamilton's relative approximation theorem, applied with\n\\[\nd_f(x,y)=d_N(f(x),f(y)),\n\\]\ngives a homeomorphism \\(a\\), PL between those structures, equal to the identity on \\(K^+\\), and satisfying \\(d_f(a(x),x)&lt;\\eta(x)\\). Then \\(f_1=f\\circ a\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\lambda>0\\) there is \\(\\delta>0\\), depending only on these source sets and \\(\\lambda\\), such that every embedding \\(e:O\\to\\mathbb R^3\\) satisfying …", "statement": "<p>**Small local ambient extension — original Lemma A.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(B\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\) is a closed Euclidean cube.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(C,D\\subset B\\) are compact, with \\(C\\subset\\operatorname{int}B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(O\\) is an open neighborhood of \\(C\\cup D\\cup\\partial B\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\) there is \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), depending only on these source sets and \\(\\lambda\\), such that every embedding \\(e:O\\to\\mathbb R^3\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\sup_O|e-\\operatorname{id}|&lt;\\delta,\\qquad e=\\operatorname{id}\\text{ on }D\\cup\\partial B\n\\]\nadmits an ambient homeomorphism \\(A\\) with\n\\[\nA=e\\text{ on }C,\\quad\nA=\\operatorname{id}\\text{ on }D\\cup(\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}B),\n\\quad\n\\sup_{\\mathbb R^3}|A-\\operatorname{id}|&lt;\\lambda.\n\\tag{A.2}\n\\]\nThe tolerance is uniform over a finite list of fixed normalized source configurations; no derivative bound on \\(e\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(E=C\\cup D\\cup\\partial B\\). Edwards–Kirby's deformation theorem at the inclusion deforms sufficiently close embeddings \\(e=e_0\\) to \\(e_1\\), with \\(e_1=\\operatorname{id}\\) on \\(E\\), unchanged outside a fixed compact neighborhood \\(L\\Subset O\\). Continuity at the inclusion permits\n\\[\n|e_t(x)-x|&lt;\\lambda/2,\\qquad |e(x)-x|&lt;\\lambda/2\n\\quad(x\\in L).\n\\]\nThe open images \\(e_t(O)\\) coincide: enclose \\(L\\) in relatively compact subdomains with unchanged boundary collars, and use invariance of domain and degree relative to the common boundary.</p><p>On the common image define \\(A_0=e\\circ e_1^{-1}\\), and extend by the identity elsewhere. This is an ambient homeomorphism, equals \\(e\\) on \\(E\\), and has displacement below \\(\\lambda\\). It fixes \\(\\partial B\\), hence preserves \\(\\operatorname{int}B\\). Restrict to \\(B\\) and extend by the identity. For a finite list take the minimum of the positive tolerances.</p><p>For its later gluing use, if \\(\\eta\\) is defined on \\(U_0\\), \\(O_0\\Subset U_0\\), \\(V\\) contains the protected sets, and \\(\\eta=\\operatorname{id}\\) on \\(U_0\\cap V\\), then sufficiently small displacement ensures \\(\\eta(O_0)\\subset U_0\\). The map equal to \\(\\eta\\) on \\(O_0\\) and the identity on \\(V\\) is an embedding: a collision \\(\\eta(x)=y\\in V\\) gives \\(\\eta(y)=y\\), hence \\(x=y\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is an absolute integer \\(n_0\\) and a locally finite dyadic decomposition \\(\\mathcal Q\\) such that:", "statement": "<p>**Adapted dyadic meshes — original Lemma A.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Arbitrary positive local upper bounds on the fine scale are given.</p><p class=\"li\">For a dyadic cube \\(Q\\), let \\(c_Q,l_Q\\) be its center and side length, and set\n\\[\nB_Q(s)=c_Q+[-sl_Q,sl_Q]^3.\n\\tag{A.13}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute integer \\(n_0\\) and a locally finite dyadic decomposition \\(\\mathcal Q\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(B_Q(100)\\Subset\\Omega\\); cubes meeting \\(B_Q(50)\\) have absolutely comparable side lengths.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Configurations in \\(B_Q(6)\\) belong to a finite normalized list and admit a conforming affine triangulation with finitely many nondegenerate normalized shapes.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The cubes have \\(n_0\\) colors, with the closed \\(B_Q(6)\\) pairwise disjoint in each color.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Assigning \\(P(Q)\\) by \\(c_Q\\in P(Q)\\), the scale bounds can ensure that any fixed finite number of neighboring patch layers stays in a fixed locally finite graph of coarse neighborhoods, independently of subsequent refinement.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a positive size function \\(s\\), with sufficiently small absolute Lipschitz constant, below the prescribed bounds and below \\(10^{-3}\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\Omega^c)\\), using Lemma 1.1. Take maximal dyadic cubes satisfying \\(l_Q\\le\\inf_Qs\\). Failure for the parent gives \\(s(x)\\le(2+C\\operatorname{Lip}s)l_Q\\). Small slope gives local comparability; the dyadic ratios may be confined to \\(\\{1/2,1,2\\}\\). Positivity gives local finiteness.</p><p>Bounded ratios and dyadic positions yield finite normalized configurations. Subdivide contact facets, cone their perimeter segments to their centers, and cone cube-boundary triangles to the cube center. The overlap graph for \\(B_Q(6)\\) has bounded degree, hence an absolute coloring. Initially choosing scales small relative to coarse sizes proves the last assertion. The same finite-position argument applies to older protected boxes in \\(B_Q(20)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each \\(\\lambda>0\\), sufficiently small \\(a>0\\), depending only on \\(\\lambda\\), the normalized source configuration, and the relevant \\(K_P\\), makes \\( \\sup_{B_Q(5)}|G-H|<al_Q,\\qquad \\sup_{B_Q(3)}|h_Q-H|<al_Q \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Insertion with protected cores — original Lemma A.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A patch \\(Q\\) and finitely many older boxes have the normalized configurations of Lemma 2.3.</p><p class=\"li\">Every older box has a half-shrunk and a fully-shrunk version, separated by a fixed positive normalized margin.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(G:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) is a homeomorphism.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h_Q\\) is a PL embedding near \\(B_Q(3)\\), agreeing with \\(G\\) on overlaps with half-shrunk boxes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\), sufficiently small \\(a&gt;0\\), depending only on \\(\\lambda\\), the normalized source configuration, and the relevant \\(K_P\\), makes\n\\[\n\\sup_{B_Q(5)}|G-H|&lt;al_Q,\\qquad\n\\sup_{B_Q(3)}|h_Q-H|&lt;al_Q\n\\tag{A.14}\n\\]\nsufficient for a domain homeomorphism \\(A\\), supported in \\(B_Q(5)\\), satisfying\n\\[\n\\sup|A-\\operatorname{id}|&lt;\\lambda l_Q,\n\\]\nwith \\(G\\circ A=h_Q\\) near \\(B_Q(2)\\) and \\(G\\circ A=G\\) on the fully-shrunk older boxes. No derivatives of \\(G\\) or \\(h_Q\\) enter this tolerance.</p>", "proof": "<p>Normalize \\(l_Q=1\\). The lower bound for \\(H\\) and degree on \\(\\operatorname{int}B_Q(4)\\) show, for small \\(a\\), that \\(h_Q(B_Q(3))\\subset G(\\operatorname{int}B_Q(4))\\). Thus \\(\\eta=G^{-1}h_Q\\) is defined there. If \\(z=\\eta(x)\\),\n\\[\n|z-x|\n\\le K_P|H(z)-H(x)|\n\\le K_P\\bigl(|H(z)-G(z)|+|h_Q(x)-H(x)|\\bigr)\n&lt;2K_Pa.\n\\tag{A.15}\n\\]\nChoose a fixed closed neighborhood \\(C\\) of \\(B_Q(2)\\) inside \\(B_Q(3)\\). Let \\(D\\) consist of the fully-shrunk older boxes in \\(B_Q(5)\\), and include an identity neighborhood of \\(\\partial B_Q(5)\\). The half/full margins permit the gluing observation in Lemma 2.2. Apply that lemma and rescale.</p><p>For repeated insertions,\n\\[\n|G(A(x))-H(x)|\n\\le |G(A(x))-H(A(x))|+K_P|A(x)-x|.\n\\tag{A.16}\n\\]\nFor a fixed number of stages, choose tolerances backwards from the final requirements; at each coarse index take minima over finitely many interacting indices. These choices precede all derivative bounds for the later PL libraries.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a \\(C^\\infty\\) diffeomorphism \\(g:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) such that \\( \\|g-H\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}<\\varepsilon,\\qquad |g(x)-H(x)|<\\xi(x). \\) In particular \\(g\\in W^{1,p}\\), and its target is exactly \\(\\Omega'\\).", "statement": "<p>**Strong smoothing of locally bi-Lipschitz maps — original Theorem A.1, used as an intermediate result.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Omega,\\Omega'\\subset\\mathbb R^3\\) are bounded domains.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(1\\le p&lt;\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) is a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism in \\(W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and \\(\\xi:\\Omega\\to(0,\\infty)\\) is continuous.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a \\(C^\\infty\\) diffeomorphism \\(g:\\Omega\\to\\Omega'\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|g-H\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}&lt;\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n|g(x)-H(x)|&lt;\\xi(x).\n\\tag{A.1}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(g\\in W^{1,p}\\), and its target is exactly \\(\\Omega'\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 2 with Sobolev tolerance \\(\\varepsilon/2\\) and value tolerance \\(\\xi/2\\), obtaining a PL homeomorphism. Apply Proposition 1 with the same tolerances. The triangle inequality proves the assertion, and both stages preserve the target.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a compact \\(K\\subset D\\), with \\(\\mu(D\\setminus K)<\\varepsilon\\), and a Lipschitz map \\(S_0:\\Lambda\\to\\Lambda\\) such that:", "statement": "<p>**Target flattening of retained deficient ranks — original Proposition 2.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(f:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) satisfies the assumptions of Theorem 1 and has positive topological orientation.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(R_f\\) is the regular set of Lemma 4.1 below.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nD=\\{x\\in R_f:1\\le\\operatorname{rank}Df(x)\\le2\\},\\qquad\n\\mu(E)=\\int_{E\\cap D}(1+|Df|^p)\\,dx,\\qquad\n\\nu=f_\\#(1_\\Omega\\mathcal L^3).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1/100\\), and \\(a:\\Lambda\\to(0,\\infty)\\) is continuous.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a compact \\(K\\subset D\\), with \\(\\mu(D\\setminus K)&lt;\\varepsilon\\), and a Lipschitz map \\(S_0:\\Lambda\\to\\Lambda\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(S_0=\\operatorname{id}\\) outside finitely many disjoint compactly contained rectangular boxes, \\(\\operatorname{Lip}S_0\\le C\\), and \\(|S_0(y)-y|&lt;a(y)\\), with absolute \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For \\(0&lt;\\kappa\\le1\\),\n\\[\nS_\\kappa=(1-\\kappa)S_0+\\kappa\\operatorname{id}\n\\]\nis a bi-Lipschitz self-homeomorphism of \\(\\Lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. With \\(F_\\kappa=S_\\kappa f\\),\n\\[\n|DF_\\kappa|\\le C|Df|\\quad\\text{a.e.},\\qquad\n|DF_\\kappa-Df|\\le C\\lambda|Df|\\quad\\text{a.e. on }K.\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nMoreover \\(F_\\kappa\\to F_0\\) strongly in \\(W^{1,p}\\), and \\(\\operatorname{rank}DF_0=\\operatorname{rank}Df\\) almost everywhere on \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. \\(F_0(K)\\) lies on finitely many flat compact plate patches with pairwise disjoint ambient neighborhoods. Each has slope at most \\(C\\lambda\\) in its assigned frame and an assigned rank. Rank-one patches may retain a prescribed small directional grouping of \\(\\operatorname{im}Df\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">5. There is a compact zero-volume set \\(Z\\Subset\\Lambda\\), contained in finitely many piecewise affine graph sheets, such that\n\\[\nS_0:\\Lambda\\setminus S_0^{-1}(Z)\\longrightarrow\\Lambda\\setminus Z\n\\tag{2.8}\n\\]\nis a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism. Its only nonsingleton fibres are collapsed vertical intervals over \\(Z\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">6. For almost every target point outside \\(Z\\), its unique \\(F_0\\)-preimage is either a regular positive full-rank point with the power-mean test below, or lies in a fixed source null set. For \\(p\\ge3\\), the latter alternative occurs only on a target null set.</p>", "proof": "<p>Truncate the finite measure \\(\\mu\\) so that jets and reciprocals of positive singular values are bounded. Partition by rank, rank-one range direction, and the directions of Lemma 4.2. Since \\(f_\\#\\mu\\ll\\nu\\), Lemma 4.3 supplies simultaneous graph-null data. Compact restriction, separated target images, generic box cuts, and removal of small cut neighborhoods reduce the data to finitely many disjoint boxes.</p><p>In one box apply Lemma 4.4. Sort band starts \\(a_1\\le\\cdots\\le a_N\\), and define\n\\[\ns_1=a_1,\\qquad s_j=\\max\\{a_j,s_{j-1}+L\\}.\n\\tag{2.9}\n\\]\nThese remain \\(C\\lambda\\)-Lipschitz. Their bands have disjoint interiors, preserve coverage, and move upward by at most \\(NL\\): advanced intervals attach to the preceding contiguous cluster.</p><p>Choose a horizontal cutoff \\(\\chi=1\\) near retained data and a nondecreasing vertical compensation \\(\\eta\\), transitioning strictly above all bands. Set\n\\[\nq_0(h,v)=v+\\chi(h)\\left(-\\sum_{j=1}^N[v-s_j(h)]_{[0,L]}+NL\\eta(v)\\right),\n\\quad S_0(h,v)=(h,q_0(h,v)).\n\\tag{2.10}\n\\]\nChoose \\(NL\\) so that\n\\[\nNL\\operatorname{Lip}\\chi\\le\\lambda,\\qquad\nNL\\operatorname{Lip}\\eta\\le1,\n\\tag{2.11}\n\\]\nand all motion and chart margins hold.</p><p>The vertical slopes lie in \\([0,2]\\). At most one truncated summand is unsaturated in a piece; saturated summands are constant. Hence, for \\(R=q_0-v\\),\n\\[\n|R(h,v)-R(\\widetilde h,\\widetilde v)|\n\\le C\\lambda|h-\\widetilde h|+C|v-\\widetilde v|.\n\\tag{2.12}\n\\]\nThe map fixes a neighborhood of the box boundary and maps each vertical column onto itself. Patching by the identity gives the absolute Lipschitz bound. Adding \\(\\kappa v\\) makes the column slope at least \\(\\kappa\\), giving a bi-Lipschitz inverse.</p><p>ACL composition proves (2.7). At retained data, Lemma 4.2 controls the vertical derivative, and the horizontal component is exactly \\(\\operatorname{proj}_{e^\\perp}Df\\). Projection is injective on the bundle, so rank does not decrease. The Lipschitz composition and Fréchet differentiation imply \\(\\ker Df\\subset\\ker DF_0\\), so rank cannot increase. The identity \\(F_\\kappa=(1-\\kappa)F_0+\\kappa f\\) gives strong convergence.</p><p>Where \\(\\chi=1\\), band \\(j\\) maps to \\(b_j(h)=s_j(h)-(j-1)L\\). Thus\n\\[\nZ=\\bigcup_{\\mathrm{boxes}}\\bigcup_j\\{(h,b_j(h)):\\chi(h)=1\\}.\n\\tag{2.13}\n\\]\nThese finite graph sheets are compact and volume-null. Their fibres are precisely the contiguous collapsed vertical intervals. Outside them the vertical slope has a positive local lower bound, proving (2.8).</p><p>Refine graph sheets into affine strata, assign each datum measurably to a containing plane, compact-restrict assignments to separated pieces, and use generic rectangular cuts with removed neighborhoods. This yields disjoint plate patches without assuming their intersections have zero assigned measure. Locality of weak derivatives puts each retained differential in its plate tangent plane.</p><p>Apply Lemma 4.1 on the homeomorphic complement. Regular deficient images are null; all remaining nonregular points form one source null set, whose image is null when \\(p\\ge3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a Borel full-measure set \\(R_u\\) such that, for \\(x\\in R_u\\), \\(A=Du(x)\\) is also the Fréchet differential and …", "statement": "<p>**Regular points of a Sobolev homeomorphism — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(u:G\\to G'\\) is a homeomorphism between open subsets of \\(\\mathbb R^3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(u\\in W^{1,p}_{\\mathrm{loc}}(G;\\mathbb R^3)\\), \\(p&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Property \\(N\\) means that source sets of three-dimensional measure zero have zero-volume images.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a Borel full-measure set \\(R_u\\) such that, for \\(x\\in R_u\\), \\(A=Du(x)\\) is also the Fréchet differential and\n\\[\n\\lim_{r\\downarrow0}\\sup_{0&lt;|z-x|\\le r}\n\\frac{|u(z)-u(x)-A(z-x)|}{|z-x|}=0,\n\\qquad\n\\lim_{r\\downarrow0}\\fint_{B_r(x)}|Du-A|^p=0.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nFor positive topological orientation, invertible \\(A\\) has \\(\\det A&gt;0\\). Also\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^3\\bigl(u(\\{x\\in R_u:\\operatorname{rank}Du(x)&lt;3\\})\\bigr)=0.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nIf \\(p\\ge3\\), \\(u\\) has property \\(N\\). Finally, almost every section parallel to any fixed coordinate plane has zero-volume image; this last assertion holds for every continuous \\(W^{1,p}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\) map, without injectivity.</p>", "proof": "<p>At almost every \\(x\\),\n\\[\nw_r(\\xi)=\\frac{u(x+r\\xi)-u(x)}r-A\\xi\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad\\text{in }W^{1,p}(B_2;\\mathbb R^3).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nThe derivative assertion is Lebesgue differentiation. For values, subtract the affine gradient, use Poincaré on concentric balls, and sum dyadic differences of means, bounded by\n\\[\nCt\\left(\\fint_{B_t(x)}|Du-A|^p\\right)^{1/p}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;d&lt;1/8\\). For every \\(z\\in B_1\\), slicing gives \\(\\rho\\in(d,2d)\\) with the sphere \\(W^{1,p}\\) norm bounded by \\(C_d\\|w_r\\|_{W^{1,p}(B_2)}\\). Since \\(p&gt;2\\), sphere Sobolev embedding controls \\(\\sup_{\\partial B_\\rho(z)}|w_r|\\). Every coordinate of the homeomorphism \\((u(x+r\\xi)-u(x))/r\\) attains its extrema on the boundary, by openness. Comparing the affine part yields\n\\[\n\\sup_{B_1}|w_r|\\le C\\|A\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}d+C_{p,d}\\|w_r\\|_{W^{1,p}(B_2)}.\n\\]\nLet \\(r\\downarrow0\\), then \\(d\\downarrow0\\). Uniform affine approximation gives the Fréchet test. For invertible \\(A\\), the small-sphere degree equals \\(\\operatorname{sgn}\\det A\\).</p><p>Fréchet points admit a countable Lipschitz decomposition: restrict to bounded derivatives and a uniform first-order remainder bound, then partition into sufficiently small interior pieces. Apply the area formula to Lipschitz extensions on the rank-deficient portions. Null subsets of these Lipschitz pieces also have null images.</p><p>For property \\(N\\), use \\(p=3\\) locally. Sphere oscillation and slicing on a fixed enlargement of an interior cube give\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{osc}_Q u)^3\\le C\\int_{C_0Q}|Du|^3.\n\\]\nCover a null set by a Whitney decomposition inside an open neighborhood with arbitrarily small \\(\\int|Du|^3\\); bounded overlap proves its image has zero outer volume. For \\(p&gt;3\\), use local \\(L^3\\) integrability.</p><p>For a generic coordinate-plane section, planar Morrey and Hölder on squares of side \\(a\\) give\n\\[\n\\sum_Q\\operatorname{diam}(u(Q))^2\n\\le C\\left(\\sum_Qa^2\\right)^{1-2/p}\n\\left(\\sum_Q\\int_{2Q}|D_\\tau u|^p\\right)^{2/p}.\n\\]\nThe bound is independent of \\(a\\). Uniform continuity gives maximum image diameter tending to zero, hence the sum of cubed diameters tends to zero. Exhaust the section.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{im}Df(x)\\subset V(\\nu,f(x)) \\quad\\text{for almost every }x. \\) On \\(f(D)\\), \\(V(\\nu,y)\\ne\\mathbb R^3\\) for \\(\\nu\\)-almost every \\(y\\).", "statement": "<p>**Forbidden cone for the transported measure — original Lemma 2.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(D,\\nu\\) are as in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(V(\\nu,y)\\) is the Alberti–Marchese decomposability bundle.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{im}Df(x)\\subset V(\\nu,f(x))\n\\quad\\text{for almost every }x.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nOn \\(f(D)\\), \\(V(\\nu,y)\\ne\\mathbb R^3\\) for \\(\\nu\\)-almost every \\(y\\). Up to a \\(\\nu\\)-null set, finitely many measurable pieces have assigned unit vectors \\(e\\) satisfying\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{proj}_{V(\\nu,y)}e|&lt;\\lambda/10.\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nIn coordinates \\((h,v)\\in e^\\perp\\times\\mathbb R\\), no nonzero bundle vector belongs to\n\\[\n\\{(a,b):|a|\\le |b|/\\lambda\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For almost every line parallel to \\(e_i\\), \\(f\\) is absolutely continuous and injective. The one-dimensional area formula identifies its pushforward of \\(|\\partial_if|\\,dt\\) with length on the image curve. Integrating transversely gives\n\\[\nf_\\#(1_{\\mathrm{box}}|\\partial_if|\\,dx)\\ll\\nu.\n\\]\nThe decomposability-bundle property, including weighted restrictions and strong locality, puts \\(\\partial_if\\) in the bundle almost everywhere. Exhaust by boxes and use all three directions.</p><p>By Lemma 4.1, \\(f(D)\\) is volume-null. If \\(A\\subset f(D)\\) had positive \\(\\nu\\)-measure and full bundle, Alberti–Marchese differentiability would make every Lipschitz function ordinarily differentiable \\(\\nu|_A\\)-almost everywhere. De Philippis–Rindler's converse to Rademacher would imply \\(\\nu|_A\\ll\\mathcal L^3\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Choose a sufficiently fine finite sphere net and measurably assign a direction close to a normal to the proper bundle. A nonzero cone vector has vertical-to-total ratio at least \\(\\lambda/\\sqrt{1+\\lambda^2}&gt;\\lambda/2\\), whereas bundle vectors have ratio below \\(\\lambda/10\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a Borel \\(A_0\\subset A\\), of full \\(\\sigma\\)-measure, such that \\( |\\{v:(h(v),v)\\in A_0\\}|=0 \\) for every \\(1/\\lambda\\)-Lipschitz graph on any interval.", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous graph-null set — original Lemma 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Coordinates are \\((h,v)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times\\mathbb R\\), and \\(0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A finite measure \\(\\sigma\\ll\\nu\\) is concentrated on a Borel set \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(\\sigma\\)-almost every point, \\(V(\\nu,\\cdot)\\) contains no nonzero vector in the cone of Lemma 4.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a Borel \\(A_0\\subset A\\), of full \\(\\sigma\\)-measure, such that\n\\[\n|\\{v:(h(v),v)\\in A_0\\}|=0\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nfor every \\(1/\\lambda\\)-Lipschitz graph on any interval.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a compact rectangle \\(X=H\\times I\\), let \\(\\Gamma\\) be the compact family of \\(1/\\lambda\\)-Lipschitz maps \\(I\\to H\\), and\n\\[\n\\rho_h=(v\\mapsto(h(v),v))_\\#(\\mathcal L^1|_I),\\qquad\n\\mathcal C=\\left\\{\\int_\\Gamma\\rho_h\\,dP(h):P\\in\\mathcal P(\\Gamma)\\right\\}.\n\\]\nThis is convex and weakly compact. Every \\(\\rho\\in\\mathcal C\\) is singular to \\(\\sigma|_X\\). Otherwise a nonzero \\(\\sigma_0=a\\rho\\ll\\sigma\\) decomposes as \\(\\int a\\rho_h\\,dP(h)\\). These are weighted rectifiable curve measures with forbidden tangents \\((h'(v),1)\\), while their aggregate is absolutely continuous with respect to \\(\\nu\\), contradicting the bundle property.</p><p>For \\(0\\le\\phi\\le1\\), use\n\\[\nL(\\phi,\\rho)=\\int\\phi\\,d\\rho+\\int(1-\\phi)\\,d\\sigma.\n\\]\nMutual singularity and regularity give \\(\\inf_\\phi L(\\phi,\\rho)=0\\). Sion's minimax theorem gives \\(\\inf_\\phi\\sup_\\rho L=0\\). Choose continuous \\(\\phi_n\\) with\n\\[\n\\int(1-\\phi_n)\\,d\\sigma+\n\\sup_{h\\in\\Gamma}\\int_I\\phi_n(h(v),v)\\,dv\\le2^{-n}.\n\\]\nThen \\(\\phi_n\\to1\\) \\(\\sigma\\)-almost everywhere and \\(\\phi_n(h(v),v)\\to0\\) almost everywhere on each individual graph. The single set \\(\\{\\phi_n\\to1\\}\\) works for every graph.</p><p>Use countably many rectangles with interior margins. Restrict graphs, project their horizontal component to \\(H\\), and extend shorter-interval graphs constantly at endpoints. A countable union of the local graph-null sets, intersected with \\(A\\), proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are finitely many piecewise affine functions \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_N\\) on \\(H\\), each \\(C\\lambda\\)-Lipschitz, and \\(L>0\\), such that \\( NL<\\delta,\\qquad K\\Subset\\bigcup_{j=1}^N\\{(h,v):|v-g_j(h)|\\le L/2\\}. \\) Their values …", "statement": "<p>**Finite thin bands — original Lemma 2.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\) is compact in the interior of a rectangular chart \\(H\\times I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\) satisfies (2.6).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are finitely many piecewise affine functions \\(g_1,\\ldots,g_N\\) on \\(H\\), each \\(C\\lambda\\)-Lipschitz, and \\(L&gt;0\\), such that\n\\[\nNL&lt;\\delta,\\qquad\nK\\Subset\\bigcup_{j=1}^N\\{(h,v):|v-g_j(h)|\\le L/2\\}.\n\\]\nTheir values and bands remain in a fixed compact subinterval of \\(\\operatorname{int}I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a grid of spacing \\(w\\), let \\(P_w\\) be the centers of boxes meeting \\(K\\), ordered by\n\\[\n(h,v)\\prec(h',v')\\iff v'-v\\ge\\lambda|h'-h|.\n\\]\nSuccessive elements of a chain have vertical separation at least \\(w\\). Interpolate a chain as an allowed graph. In a compact graph family define\n\\[\n\\omega(r)=\\sup_h|\\{v:\\operatorname{dist}((h(v),v),K)\\le r\\}|.\n\\]\nCompactness, reverse Fatou, and (2.6) give \\(\\omega(r)\\to0\\). Disjoint vertical intervals of radius \\(w/4\\) around chain nodes imply\n\\[\n\\tfrac12n_ww\\le\\omega(C_\\lambda w),\\qquad n_ww\\to0,\n\\]\nwhere \\(n_w\\) is the longest chain.</p><p>Assign each center its longest-chain rank. Each rank is an antichain, so its heights are \\(\\lambda\\)-Lipschitz in the horizontal coordinates. Extend, clip to the interior vertical interval, and approximate by piecewise affine functions with error \\(o(w)\\) and Lipschitz bound \\(C\\lambda\\). Bands of length \\(L=C_2w\\) contain the assigned boxes with strict margin, and \\(NL\\le C_2n_ww\\to0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One can replace \\(h\\) inside \\(B_{2s}\\), keeping a neighborhood of its boundary unchanged, by a continuous Sobolev map \\(F\\) such that \\( F=\\operatorname{id}\\text{ on }B,\\qquad F(B_{2s})=h(B_{2s}), \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Cubical cutoff by collapse — original Lemma 2.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nB=[-1,1]^3,\\qquad t(x)=\\max_j|x_j|-1,\\qquad B_r=\\{t&lt;r\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(1\\le p&lt;\\infty\\), and \\(s&gt;0\\) is sufficiently small and dyadic.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\) is a homeomorphism on a neighborhood of \\(B_{Cs}\\), onto its image, with \\(h\\in W^{1,p}_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For universal \\(C&gt;2,c&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n|h(x)-x|\\le cs\\quad\\text{when }|t(x)|\\le Cs.\n\\tag{2.19}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can replace \\(h\\) inside \\(B_{2s}\\), keeping a neighborhood of its boundary unchanged, by a continuous Sobolev map \\(F\\) such that\n\\[\nF=\\operatorname{id}\\text{ on }B,\\qquad F(B_{2s})=h(B_{2s}),\n\\tag{2.20}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\int_{0&lt;t&lt;2s}|DF|^p\\le C_p\\int_{|t|\\le Cs}|Dh|^p.\n\\tag{2.21}\n\\]\nIts only nonsingleton fibres are tame closed balls over a countable set of values, accumulating only on \\(\\partial B\\), with source diameters tending to zero at accumulations. They can be opened simultaneously over arbitrarily small mutually disjoint target neighborhoods to produce a homeomorphism. This opening is topological and carries no asserted energy bound. Property \\(N\\) of \\(h\\), if present, is retained by \\(F\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(\\rho(x)=\\min\\{s,t(x)\\}\\). On a smaller skeleton neighborhood define the near-\\(x\\) lift\n\\[\ni(H(x))=h(i(x)).\n\\tag{2.22}\n\\]\nUniform inverse charts give\n\\[\n|H(x)-x|\\le Ac\\rho(x).\n\\tag{2.23}\n\\]\nSmall \\(c\\) makes branches agree. If \\(H(x)=H(x')\\), the displacement estimate and Lipschitzness of \\(\\rho\\) place \\(x,x'\\) and their common image in one injective chart. Injectivity of \\(h\\) and that chart give \\(x=x'\\). On the outer band \\(H=h\\).</p><p>The chain rule gives\n\\[\nDH(x)=Di(H(x))^{-1}Dh(i(x))Di(x),\n\\]\nand the scale factors cancel. Change variables separately in boundedly many charts per simplex:\n\\[\n\\int_{D\\cap\\operatorname{dom}H}|DH|^p\n\\le A_p(\\ell_D/s)^3\n\\int_{B(x_D,As)\\cap\\{|t|\\le Cs\\}}|Dh|^p.\n\\tag{2.24}\n\\]\nAt depth \\(\\ell_D\\asymp\\lambda\\), at most \\(A(s/\\lambda)^2\\) sampling balls cover a target point: the relevant cubical layer has volume \\(O(s^2\\lambda)\\). Summing gives\n\\[\n\\sum_D\\int_{D\\cap\\operatorname{dom}H}|DH|^p\n\\le A_p\\sum_{k\\ge0}2^{-3k}2^{2k}\\int_{|t|\\le Cs}|Dh|^p\n\\le A_p\\int_{|t|\\le Cs}|Dh|^p.\n\\tag{2.25}\n\\]</p><p>Choose homothetic smooth convex balls\n\\[\nV_D\\Subset V_D^+\\Subset U_D\\Subset D\n\\]\nwith the complement of \\(V_D\\) and a two-sided boundary collar in the lift region. Small \\(c\\) gives \\(H(\\partial V_D)\\Subset V_D^+\\). Locally flat Schoenflies gives its tame bounded ball \\(K_D\\subset V_D^+\\); fill \\(V_D\\to K_D\\) homeomorphically. The connected lifted complement lies on the exterior side of each sphere, and different \\(K_D\\) are disjoint. Together with the identity on \\(B\\), this gives a homeomorphism \\(G\\) onto \\(h(B_{2s})\\). Continuity at \\(\\partial B\\) follows from \\(O(t)\\) displacement in each Whitney layer.</p><p>Inside \\(U_D\\), use a uniformly Lipschitz radial squeeze \\(Q_D\\), constant on \\(V_D^+\\), identity near \\(\\partial U_D\\), and a homeomorphism off \\(V_D^+\\) onto the punctured output region. Their disjoint supports give a global Lipschitz \\(Q\\), continuous at the inner seam. Set \\(F=QG\\). It is constant near every uncontrolled filling and otherwise satisfies \\(|DF|\\le\\operatorname{Lip}(Q)|DH|\\). Formula (2.25) proves the energy estimate. ACL on almost every coordinate line, integrable derivatives, and continuous endpoint limits give Sobolev gluing at \\(\\partial B\\).</p><p>The collapsed fibres are \\(G^{-1}(V_D^+)\\), tame balls of diameter \\(O(\\ell_D)\\); all other fibres are singletons. Open each squeeze only over a small target ball about its center, leaving the map unchanged elsewhere. Null source diameters ensure continuity of the openings and inverses at the inner seam.</p><p>If \\(h\\) has property \\(N\\), its smooth-chart conjugates on the lift region and their Lipschitz postcompositions have \\(N\\); filled neighborhoods map to countably many points, and the inner boundary maps to itself. Thus \\(F\\) has \\(N\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The shell \\(\\{0<t<2s\\}\\) has a locally finite conforming triangulation \\(\\mathcal T_s\\), with uniformly controlled shapes and finitely many normalized local configurations.", "statement": "<p>**Immersed cubical skeleton — original Lemma 2.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(B,t,s\\) are as in Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The shell \\(\\{0&lt;t&lt;2s\\}\\) has a locally finite conforming triangulation \\(\\mathcal T_s\\), with uniformly controlled shapes and finitely many normalized local configurations. If \\(D\\) has scale \\(\\ell_D\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diam}D\\asymp\\ell_D\\asymp\\min\\{s,t(x)\\}\\quad(x\\in D).\n\\]\nThere is a smooth positive local diffeomorphism \\(i\\) near the two-skeleton, equal to the identity near \\(t=2s\\), with\n\\[\n|i(x)-x|\\le C_0s,\\qquad\n|Di|\\le C_1s/\\ell_D,\\quad\n|(Di)^{-1}|\\le C_1\\ell_D/s,\\quad\n|D^2i|\\le C_1s/\\ell_D^2.\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\nThe neighborhood width is at least \\(b\\ell_D\\), and uniform inverse charts contain source balls comparable to \\(\\ell_D\\) and image balls comparable to \\(s\\). After bounded chart splitting,\n\\[\ni(x_*+\\ell\\xi)=y_*+sI(\\xi),\n\\tag{2.18}\n\\]\nwhere \\(I\\) belongs to a fixed finite list and \\(y_*\\) lies on a fixed rational subdivision of the \\(s\\)-grid. For fixed \\(s\\), only finitely many anchors occur. Global injectivity of \\(i\\) is not asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Tile dyadic layers \\(\\{2^{-k}s&lt;t&lt;2^{1-k}s\\}\\) by comparable axis-aligned cubes. Match neighboring dyadic faces and cone their subdivisions to face and cube centers. Bounded neighbor ratios give finite shapes. Reserve an outer identity band.</p><p>At each other vertex \\(v\\), choose a nearby \\(s\\)-grid anchor \\(y_v\\) and prescribe \\(i(x)=y_v+(s/\\ell_v)(x-v)\\). Join edge germs by regular immersed arcs inside \\(Cs\\)-balls. Choose positive transverse framings; a full transverse rotation changes the class in \\(\\pi_1(GL^+(3))\\cong\\mathbb Z/2\\), so make the relative frame class trivial. Thicken to positive local diffeomorphisms.</p><p>For each face core, the boundary three-frame loop is null-homotopic. Extend the base map in a target ball and the formal tangent two-frame across the disk. Apply the relative Smale–Hirsch theorem in dimensions \\(2\\to3\\). Extend a positive transverse column, use \\(\\phi(u)+r\\nu(u)\\), and blend with the old boundary formula; their difference is \\(O(r^2)\\), with derivative \\(O(|r|)\\). Small width preserves nonsingularity.</p><p>Normalize source by \\(\\ell\\) and target by \\(s\\). There are finitely many source configurations, incident scale ratios, anchor differences, and identity-band prescriptions. Choose one edge extension and then one face extension for each finite datum. Compact subneighborhoods give uniform derivative bounds, positive least singular values, and inverse-chart radii. Scaling yields all estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After total \\(\\mu\\)-loss below \\(\\varepsilon\\), the first round gives \\(\\kappa>0\\), retained compact \\(K\\), a carrier \\(F_b^1\\), an exact open target set \\(O_1\\), and compact paired endpoint rectangles strictly inside …", "statement": "<p>**Two-round exact carrier — original Proposition 2.13.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(f,\\mu,D\\) are as in Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">A positive continuous source value tolerance \\(a\\) is fixed before flattening, and the target tolerance satisfies \\(a_\\Lambda(y)\\le a(f^{-1}(y))/4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 4 and the protected wells of Lemma 6.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) and a marked-collar enlargement factor \\(A&gt;1\\), before facet budgets.</p><p class=\"li\">After the first round, fix a locally finite target Whitney tiling \\(\\mathcal Q\\) and arbitrary residual thresholds \\(a_Q&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After total \\(\\mu\\)-loss below \\(\\varepsilon\\), the first round gives \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\), retained compact \\(K\\), a carrier \\(F_b^1\\), an exact open target set \\(O_1\\), and compact paired endpoint rectangles strictly inside \\(O_1\\). These choices precede the \\(a_Q\\). The second round gives \\(F_b\\) and \\(O\\supset O_1\\) such that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(F_b:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) is continuous, proper, onto, locally \\(W^{1,p}\\), and has only the countable tame ball fibres of Lemma 6.2. It is genuinely homeomorphic away from their values and accumulation seams, and has property \\(N\\) for \\(p\\ge3\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(O\\) is a locally finite union of exact affine or PL core images, with exact neighborhoods on compact subsets, and\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^3(Q\\setminus O)&lt;a_Q.\n\\tag{2.29}\n\\]\nEndpoint buffers in \\(O_1\\) remain fixed.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. \\(F_b=F_\\kappa\\) near \\(K\\), meets the fine tolerance relative to \\(f\\), and \\(F_\\kappa(K)\\) stays in the protected central neighborhoods.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For \\(2&lt;p&lt;3\\), marked source cubes \\(U\\), locally finite with disjoint closures and away from \\(K\\), satisfy\n\\[\n\\int_{\\bigcup U}(1+|Df|^p)&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{2.30}\n\\]\nThere are none for \\(p\\ge3\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Widths \\(w_U&gt;0\\) can be chosen after both rounds, with disjoint enlarged cubes, so that\n\\[\n\\sum_U\\frac{\\ell_U}{w_U}\n\\int_{C_U(Aw_U)}|DF_b|^p&lt;\\varepsilon,\n\\tag{2.31}\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_U(w)\\) is the cube-radius collar of physical width \\(w\\). The enlarged cubes still have original \\((1+|Df|^p)\\)-mass below \\(2\\varepsilon\\). A uniformly Lipschitz output composition multiplies the bound by at most its Lipschitz constant to the power \\(p\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">6. For regional tests \\(A_j\\) prescribed before implantation, with two successive positive buffered enlargements \\(A_j^{[1]},A_j^{[2]}\\), implants can be so fine that\n\\[\n\\int_{A_j\\setminus\\bigcup U}|DF_b|^p\n\\le C_p\\int_{A_j^{[2]}}|Df|^p.\n\\tag{2.32}\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of jet condition numbers, residual thresholds, and PL reference constants. Summably weighted tests toward the ends are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>The flattening share gives \\(|F_\\kappa-f|&lt;a/4\\). Choose supports so that the first- and second-round motions are each below \\(a/4\\), using image preservation and local oscillation.</p><p>In the first round, classify both endpoint signs simultaneously. For \\(2&lt;p&lt;3\\), enclose exceptional inverse assignments in finitely many marked cubes by Lemma 6.3, with small original mass and extended-facet budgets, disjoint from central and regular compact data. Impose generic facets for both \\(F_0\\) and \\(f\\). Choose inner cores \\(U^-\\) and compact target neighborhoods \\(W_U\\) of retained endpoints inside \\(F_0(\\operatorname{int}U^-)\\), before \\(\\kappa\\) and reference maps.</p><p>Compact-restrict regular endpoint jets and differentiation radii. Use one shifted horizontal grid for both signs, with phase-independent sufficiently small pitch. Each square has fixed inverse classifications; exceptional rectangles lie inside \\(W_U\\), and regular buffered boxes pass Lemma 6.4's tests. Phase averaging loses arbitrarily little assignment measure. Fix all boxes, representatives, comparisons, and central margins.</p><p>Choose \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\) sufficiently small that regular tests persist, central data remain protected, and \\(F_\\kappa^{-1}(W_U)\\Subset\\operatorname{int}U^-\\). Uniform inverse convergence on compact sets in the homeomorphic complement follows from properness, fine motion, and uniqueness of the \\(F_0\\)-preimage.</p><p>Choose a PL reference homeomorphism \\(h_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\) by Lemma 2.1, sufficiently close that \\(h_{\\mathrm{ref}}^{-1}F_\\kappa\\) passes Proposition 5's identity tests on exceptional cubes and \\(h_{\\mathrm{ref}}^{-1}(W_U)\\Subset U^-\\). Cut off there and postcompose by \\(h_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\); the core becomes exactly PL. Its finite but possibly large energy is confined to marked cubes. On regular signs use Lemma 6.4, charged universally to \\(\\int_{V_b}|DF_0|^p\\le C\\int_{V_b}|Df|^p\\). Call the result \\(F_b^1\\), and take smaller buffered exact core images as \\(O_1\\).</p><p>Only now prescribe \\(a_Q\\). In each Whitney interior, exclude \\(O_1\\), its boundary, fibre values and seams, marked-boundary images, and \\(F_b^1(K)=F_\\kappa(K)\\). Apart from \\(O_1\\), these exclusions have zero volume. On the remaining genuine inverse regions, Lemma 4.1 gives regular full rank or a fixed source-null inverse type. Property \\(N\\) removes the latter for \\(p\\ge3\\). Compact-restrict target assignments with loss below a prescribed fraction of \\(a_Q\\), separating classifications and old marked interiors.</p><p>For new exceptional assignments use Lemma 6.3 with \\(g=1+|Df|^p+|DF_b^1|^p\\), adding summably small marked cubes outside old ones. Inside old marked cubes use smaller reference implants. Obtain reference maps by relative opening and Lemma 2.1. For regular assignments use target grids and Lemma 6.4 with \\(F_b^1\\)-jets; comparison boxes are disjoint and wholly inside or outside old marked cubes. Insets and phase averaging make total uncovered volume below \\(a_Q\\). There are finitely many implants per Whitney cube, hence local finiteness in both source and target.</p><p>Universal regional charging uses two rounds only. First-round regular changes charge disjoint comparisons to \\(|Df|^p\\); second-round changes charge disjoint comparisons to \\(|DF_b^1|^p\\). Supports meeting \\(A_j\\) fit in its first enlargement, and first-round charges fit in the second. This proves (2.32) without reference-map constants outside marked cubes.</p><p>Near each marked boundary the carrier is unchanged: \\(F_\\kappa\\) for first-round cubes and \\(F_b^1\\) for new cubes. Choose widths inside these unchanged neighborhoods. Normal-slice Lebesgue differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\limsup_{w\\downarrow0}\\frac{\\ell_U}{w}\n\\int_{C_U(Aw)}|DF_b|^p\n\\le C_A\\ell_U\\int_{\\partial U}^{\\mathrm{ext}}g_U\\,d\\mathcal H^2.\n\\]\nFacet budgets and summable further errors prove (2.31). Decrease widths for disjoint enlargements and small extra original mass. Image preservation and local finiteness establish continuity, properness, onto-ness, local Sobolev regularity, and the fibre assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each plate has an origin \\(z\\), a unit \\(n\\) arbitrarily close to its normal, a number \\(d>0\\), and nested horizontal rectangles in \\(n^\\perp\\).", "statement": "<p>**Protected wells — original Lemma 2.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Finitely many separated retained \\(F_0\\)-image pieces lie on flat plates with disjoint compactly contained chart neighborhoods.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\delta:\\Lambda\\to(0,\\infty)\\) is a prescribed motion scale.</p><p class=\"li\">Arbitrarily small \\(\\mu\\)-loss is allowed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each plate has an origin \\(z\\), a unit \\(n\\) arbitrarily close to its normal, a number \\(d&gt;0\\), and nested horizontal rectangles in \\(n^\\perp\\). There is \\(b_1\\ge0\\), smooth and compactly supported in \\(\\Lambda\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Delta b_1\\le C\\) absolutely, and \\(b_1\\) is as small as prescribed relative to \\(\\delta^2\\);</p><p class=\"li\">for protected \\(h\\), \\(q=z+h\\), the affine function\n\\[\nL_h(x)=q\\cdot x-\\tfrac12|q|^2+d^2\n\\]\nsupports \\(\\psi_1=|x|^2/2+b_1\\) globally, exactly at \\(q\\pm dn\\), and\n\\[\n\\sup_x(q\\cdot x-\\psi_1(x))=\\tfrac12|q|^2-d^2;\n\\tag{2.14}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">uniformly over compact slope rectangles, the gap is positive outside prescribed endpoint neighborhoods;</p><p class=\"li\">assigned central data \\(X=F_0(x)\\) satisfy \\(|\\langle X-z,n\\rangle|&lt;d/4\\), and almost every assigned datum has both endpoints\n\\[\nY_\\pm(X)=X+n(\\pm d-\\langle X-z,n\\rangle)\n\\tag{2.15}\n\\]\nin genuine inverse neighborhoods of \\(F_0\\), with inverse type regular full rank or belonging to a fixed source null set. The exceptional type is needed only for \\(2&lt;p&lt;3\\).</p><p>Compact restrictions may separate inverse ranges and types. All conclusions persist after shrinking protected rectangles.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a horizontal cutoff \\(\\theta=1\\) beyond the protected rectangle and a nonnegative smooth \\(\\beta\\), supported in \\([-2,2]\\), for which \\(u^2/2+\\beta(u)\\) has minimum \\(1\\) exactly at \\(\\pm1\\). The paper prescribes \\(1+(u^2-1)^2\\) for \\(|u|\\le3/2\\), then cuts off in the remaining region, where \\(u^2/2&gt;1\\). Set\n\\[\nb_z(z+h+vn)=\\theta(h)d^2\\beta(v/d).\n\\tag{2.16}\n\\]\nTake \\(d\\) much smaller than cutoff and chart margins and require \\(d^2\\|\\Delta_h\\theta\\|_\\infty\\le1\\). Disjoint supports give the absolute Laplacian bound and arbitrary value smallness.</p><p>Subtracting \\(L_{h_0}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\tfrac12|h-h_0|^2+\\tfrac12v^2+b_1-d^2.\n\\]\nWhere \\(\\theta=1\\), equality occurs exactly at \\(h=h_0,v=\\pm d\\). Where \\(\\theta\\ne1\\), the horizontal margin gives \\(|h-h_0|^2/2&gt;d^2\\). Compactness and quadratic growth give the uniform gap.</p><p>Choose \\(d\\in[d_-,d_+]\\) and \\(n\\) in a sufficiently small cap. For fixed \\(X\\), the endpoint map is \\(X+rn\\), \\(r=\\pm d-\\langle X-z,n\\rangle\\), with volume Jacobian \\(|r|^2\\ge(3d_-/4)^2\\). Its law is absolutely continuous for target volume. Fubini and Proposition 4 select parameters for which both endpoints have allowed inverse types for almost every assigned datum. Split types simultaneously and compact-restrict inverse charts and ranges, then refine rectangles.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The nonsingleton fibres are tame balls, form a null family on compact localizations, and their accumulating values have singleton fibres.", "statement": "<p>**Relative opening of isolated ball fibres — original Lemma 2.10.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(F:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) is a continuous proper surjection obtained by locally finite image-preserving applications of Proposition 5 in genuine homeomorphic neighborhoods.</p><p class=\"li\">Later operations avoid older fibre values and their accumulation sets.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\) is the countable set of nonsingleton values.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(C\\subset\\Lambda\\) is relatively closed and \\(C\\cap P=\\varnothing\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The nonsingleton fibres are tame balls, form a null family on compact localizations, and their accumulating values have singleton fibres. There is a homeomorphism \\(H:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\), as finely close to \\(F\\) as prescribed, such that\n\\[\nH=F\\quad\\text{on }F^{-1}(C).\n\\tag{2.26}\n\\]\nAgreement may be required on a neighborhood of any compact target test separated from \\(P\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 5 gives the fibre description for one cutoff. Local finiteness and avoidance preserve it, and each \\(v\\in P\\) remains isolated from other exceptional values and seams.</p><p>Choose mutually disjoint target balls \\(B_v\\), individually avoiding \\(C\\), older tests, exceptional values, and seams, with radii tending to zero and meeting fine errors. Properness gives upper semicontinuity of fibres: sufficiently small \\(B_v\\) has \\(F^{-1}(\\overline B_v)\\) in any chosen neighborhood of \\(F^{-1}(v)\\).</p><p>The preimage of \\(\\partial B_v\\) is a locally flat sphere in a genuine inverse neighborhood and bounds a tame ball containing the fibre. Replace \\(F\\) there by a boundary-extending ball homeomorphism, using nested target balls to retain an outer collar. Images are disjoint, and their diameters tend to zero at seams. The resulting bijection is continuous and, by invariance of domain, a homeomorphism onto \\(\\Lambda\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Finitely many cubes \\(U\\), with disjoint closures in the given neighborhoods, may be chosen so that retained data, after \\(\\sigma\\)-loss below \\(a\\), have positive relative interior margins and …", "statement": "<p>**Exceptional inverse data — original Lemma 2.11.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(E\\) is a compact source null set in finitely many genuine homeomorphic neighborhoods of a continuous locally \\(W^{1,p}\\) map \\(F\\), \\(p&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\sigma\\) is finite and supported on \\(E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(g\\ge0\\) is locally integrable.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(a,b&gt;0\\), size bounds, separated closed forbidden sets, and arbitrarily small relative insets are prescribed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Finitely many cubes \\(U\\), with disjoint closures in the given neighborhoods, may be chosen so that retained data, after \\(\\sigma\\)-loss below \\(a\\), have positive relative interior margins and\n\\[\n\\int_{\\bigcup U^+}g&lt;b,\\qquad\n\\sum_U\\ell_U\\int_{\\partial U}^{\\mathrm{ext}}g\\,d\\mathcal H^2&lt;b.\n\\tag{2.27}\n\\]\nFacet positions are \\(L^1\\)-Lebesgue points in the normal variable, and their \\(F\\)-images, including fixed small tangential extensions, have zero volume.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an open \\(V\\) around \\(E\\), compact in the charts, avoiding forbidden sets, with \\(\\int_Vg\\) arbitrarily small. Take a fine grid of pitch \\(r\\) and concentric insets of side \\((1-\\tau)r\\). Average phase over \\([0,r)^3\\). Each fixed point is lost to gaps and further margins on an \\(O(\\tau)\\) fraction of phases, so Fubini controls loss for arbitrary \\(\\sigma\\).</p><p>Fubini in facet-normal coordinates gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sum_U\\ell_U\\int_{\\partial U}^{\\mathrm{ext}}g\\,d\\mathcal H^2\n\\le C\\int_Vg.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\tau\\) and \\(V\\), then use Markov to satisfy both bounds on a positive-measure set of phases. Almost every phase also gives normal Lebesgue points and zero-volume facet images by Lemma 4.1. Compactness leaves finitely many cubes; final compact restriction supplies strict margins. Small \\(r\\) meets source and target size bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One can implant exact positive affine cores with arbitrarily small relative grid margins and disjoint enlarged source comparison boxes.", "statement": "<p>**Regular affine implantation — original Lemma 2.12.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(F\\) is a positively oriented locally \\(W^{1,p}\\) homeomorphism near compact regular full-rank data.</p><p class=\"li\">Jets, inverse jets, and Fréchet and power-mean differentiation radii are uniformly bounded.</p><p class=\"li\">A sufficiently fine target grid is used.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can implant exact positive affine cores with arbitrarily small relative grid margins and disjoint enlarged source comparison boxes. For target side \\(R\\), affine jet \\(b\\), and affine-source comparison box \\(V_b\\),\n\\[\n\\text{new energy in the source support}\n\\le C_p\\frac{|Db|^pR^3}{\\det Db}\n\\le C_p\\int_{V_b}|DF|^p.\n\\tag{2.28}\n\\]\nThe constant does not depend on the condition number of \\(Db\\). Previously protected separated compact sets and cores may be preserved.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a representative \\(x_b\\), and put\n\\(b(x)=F(x_b)+DF(x_b)(x-x_b)\\). Uniform differentiation makes \\(Fb^{-1}\\) arbitrarily close to the identity, in normalized value and derivative mean, on buffered target boxes. Choose accuracy after finite jet bounds. Also require\n\\[\n\\int_{V_b}|DF-Db|^p\\le2^{-p-1}|Db|^p|V_b|.\n\\]\nThe normalized derivative integral is \\(O_p(R^3)\\), while the original energy is bounded below by a fixed multiple of \\(|Db|^pR^3/\\det Db\\). Apply Proposition 5 to \\(Fb^{-1}\\), then precompose by \\(b\\). Multiplication by \\(Db\\) and change of variables yield (2.28).</p><p>Inset every support and comparison box within its outer grid box and make the value error smaller than that margin. Their \\(F\\)-images stay in disjoint target boxes, so source comparisons are disjoint. Compact separation handles other charts and protected data.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are finitely many pairwise disjoint buffered source cubes, avoiding \\(P\\), with centers \\(x_j\\in R\\), radii \\(r_j\\), inner cubes \\(K_j=x_j+r_jB\\), and positive affine jets …", "statement": "<p>**Postponed full-rank correction — original Proposition 2.16.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(f:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) is an orientation-preserving Sobolev homeomorphism as in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(R\\) is its regular rank-three set.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P\\Subset\\Omega\\) is protected and disjoint from \\(R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are finitely many pairwise disjoint buffered source cubes, avoiding \\(P\\), with centers \\(x_j\\in R\\), radii \\(r_j\\), inner cubes \\(K_j=x_j+r_jB\\), and positive affine jets\n\\[\nA_j=Df(x_j),\\qquad b_j(x)=f(x_j)+A_j(x-x_j),\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\int_{R\\setminus\\bigcup_jK_j}|Df|^p&lt;\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n\\sum_j\\int_{K_j}|Df-A_j|^p&lt;\\varepsilon.\n\\tag{2.36}\n\\]\nTheir buffered cutoff bands \\(E_j=\\{x_j+r_j\\xi:|t(\\xi)|\\le Cs\\}\\) have arbitrarily small total \\(f\\)-energy, including all finite affine factors \\(C_p|A_j|^p|A_j^{-1}|^p\\). Normalized \\(f\\) is within \\(cs/4\\) of the identity there.</p><p>After these choices there are \\(v_j&gt;0,\\beta&gt;0\\) such that every smooth diffeomorphism \\(k:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) with\n\\[\n\\sup_{Q_j}|k-f|&lt;v_j,\\qquad\n\\sum_j\\int_{E_j}|Dk|^p&lt;\\beta,\n\\quad Q_j=x_j+r_jB_{(C+1)s},\n\\]\ncan be changed only in \\(x_j+r_jB_{2s}\\) to a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism \\(\\widetilde k:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\), equal to \\(b_j\\) on \\(K_j\\), with total derivative error on corrected cores and shells below \\(\\varepsilon\\), after starting with smaller prescriptions. Fine value tolerances may also be retained. A locally bi-Lipschitz preliminary \\(W^{1,p}\\) map may first be smoothed by Proposition 3.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a compact \\(K\\subset R\\setminus P\\), discarding arbitrarily little full-rank energy, on which \\(|Df|+|Df^{-1}|\\le M\\). Fix \\(M\\) before shell choices. Choose a common small dyadic \\(s\\). At each \\(x\\in K\\), arbitrarily small buffered cubes satisfy\n\\[\n\\sup_Q|A^{-1}(f-b)|&lt;csr/4,\\qquad\n\\int_Q|Df-A|^p&lt;\\eta|Q|.\n\\tag{2.37–2.38}\n\\]\nUse Vitali selection, finite truncation, and slight shrinking to obtain disjoint closed buffered cubes. Their bands and outer-minus-inner portions have volume \\(O(s)|Q_j|\\), hence\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\int_{E_j\\cup(Q_j\\setminus K_j)}|Df|^p\n\\le A_p(M^ps+\\eta)|\\Omega|,\n\\tag{2.39}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\int_{K_j}|Df-A_j|^p\\le\\eta|\\Omega|.\n\\tag{2.40}\n\\]\nChoose \\(s\\), then \\(\\eta\\), after \\(M\\), including the factor \\(C_pM^{2p}\\).</p><p>Require \\(\\sup_{Q_j}|k-f|&lt;csr_j/(4M)\\). The normalized \\(k\\) is within \\(cs/2\\) of the identity on the band. Apply Lemmas 7.1–7.2 in disjoint cubes. They preserve old images and glue to a homeomorphism onto \\(\\Lambda\\). On modified shells \\(S_j\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\int_{S_j}|D\\widetilde k|^p\n\\le C_pM^{2p}\\sum_j\\int_{E_j}|Dk|^p+\\delta.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\int_{S_j}|D\\widetilde k-Df|^p\n\\le2^{p-1}\\left(\nC_pM^{2p}\\sum_j\\int_{E_j}|Dk|^p+\\delta+\n\\sum_j\\int_{S_j}|Df|^p\\right).\n\\tag{2.42}\n\\]\nChoose the band test and additive errors after the finite factors. Small cube oscillations and preliminary value errors retain fine accuracy, since modified values stay in the old cube image. Proposition 3 allows a locally bi-Lipschitz preliminary map, preserving strict finite energy and value tests.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism \\(F\\) onto the same image, equal to the identity on \\(B\\) and to \\(h\\) near and outside \\(\\partial B_{2s}\\), satisfying …", "statement": "<p>**Smooth cubical cutoff — original Lemma 2.14.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The hypotheses and notation of Proposition 5 hold.</p><p class=\"li\">Additionally, \\(h\\) and its inverse are smooth on the neighborhoods used.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism \\(F\\) onto the same image, equal to the identity on \\(B\\) and to \\(h\\) near and outside \\(\\partial B_{2s}\\), satisfying\n\\[\n\\int_{0&lt;t&lt;2s}|DF|^p\n\\le C_p\\int_{|t|\\le Cs}|Dh|^p+\\varepsilon_0.\n\\tag{2.33}\n\\]\nIts local bi-Lipschitz constants may depend on \\(h,s,\\varepsilon_0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Proposition 5 through hole filling. The lift is smooth; smooth Schoenflies makes each lifted sphere's bounded side a smooth ball. A prescribed positive sphere parametrization extends smoothly: identify the ball, isotope the sphere diffeomorphism to a rotation by Smale's theorem, and insert the isotopy in an annulus, matching collars first.</p><p>For fixed \\(h,s\\), normalized local lifts are\n\\[\n\\xi\\longmapsto\nI^{-1}\\left(\\frac{h(y_*+sI(\\xi))-y_*}{s}\\right).\n\\tag{2.34}\n\\]\nThere are finitely many \\(I\\), anchors \\(y_*\\), and normalized hole and buffer data. Choose one smooth filling per datum; their forward and inverse constants have a finite common maximum \\(L(h,s)\\), unchanged under the common source/output rescaling by \\(\\ell_D\\).</p><p>Replace each nonstrict squeeze by a strictly increasing one, a homothety of ratio \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\) on \\(V_D^+\\), interpolated to the identity. Forward bounds are uniform in \\(\\alpha\\), inverse bounds finite for fixed \\(\\alpha\\). Hole energy is at most\n\\[\n\\alpha^pL(h,s)^p|B_{2s}\\setminus B|,\n\\]\nso choose \\(\\alpha\\) to make it below \\(\\varepsilon_0\\). Elsewhere retain (2.25). Uniform fixed-data local bounds for maps and inverses extend across the inner cube seam by decomposing line segments and using continuity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Returning to original coordinates makes the new map equal to \\(b\\) on \\(x_0+rB\\), preserves the old image and outer collar, and, in the smooth case …", "statement": "<p>**Affine-coordinate cutoff estimate — original Remark 2.15.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(b(x)=y_0+A(x-x_0)\\), \\(A\\in GL^+(3)\\), \\(r&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize \\(k\\) by\n\\[\n\\widehat k(\\xi)=r^{-1}A^{-1}(k(x_0+r\\xi)-y_0).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\widehat k\\) satisfies the hypotheses of the appropriate cubical cutoff.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Returning to original coordinates makes the new map equal to \\(b\\) on \\(x_0+rB\\), preserves the old image and outer collar, and, in the smooth case,\n\\[\n\\int_S|Dk_{\\mathrm{new}}|^p\n\\le C_p|A|^p|A^{-1}|^p\\int_{A^{\\mathrm{band}}}|Dk|^p\n+|A|^pr^3\\varepsilon_0,\n\\tag{2.35}\n\\]\nwhere \\(S=\\{x_0+r\\xi:0&lt;t(\\xi)&lt;2s\\}\\) and\n\\(A^{\\mathrm{band}}=\\{x_0+r\\xi:|t(\\xi)|\\le Cs\\}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\(D_\\xi\\widehat k=A^{-1}D_xk\\), the source Jacobian \\(r^3\\), and multiplication by \\(A\\) when returning to original coordinates. The additive error is freely prescribable in original units.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are disjoint slightly enlarged cubes \\(\\widetilde U\\supset U\\), with boundaries in those outer collars, and source self-homeomorphisms supported in their closures, for which the resulting locally bi-Lipschitz …", "statement": "<p>**Subcritical marked-cube compression — original Lemma 2.17.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(2&lt;p&lt;3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(U,w_U\\) are the disjoint marked cubes and collars of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) is locally bi-Lipschitz.</p><p class=\"li\">Thinner collars \\(C_U'(w_U)\\) contain a fixed relative share of the outer half-collar and satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_U\\frac{\\ell_U}{w_U}\\int_{C_U'(w_U)}|Dk|^p\\le b.\n\\tag{2.43}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\varepsilon_0&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are disjoint slightly enlarged cubes \\(\\widetilde U\\supset U\\), with boundaries in those outer collars, and source self-homeomorphisms supported in their closures, for which the resulting locally bi-Lipschitz \\(\\widetilde k\\) agrees with \\(k\\) outside \\(\\bigcup\\widetilde U\\) and\n\\[\n\\sum_U\\int_{\\widetilde U}|D\\widetilde k|^p\\le C_pb+\\varepsilon_0.\n\\tag{2.44}\n\\]\nValue changes occur inside \\(k(\\widetilde U)\\), so sufficiently fine marked cubes retain prescribed compact value accuracy. No convergence to a fixed boundary trace is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Coarea for cube radius selects outer boundaries, also Lebesgue points of the surface integral, with\n\\[\n\\sum_U\\ell_U\\int_{\\partial\\widetilde U}|Dk|^p\\,d\\mathcal H^2\\le Cb.\n\\tag{2.45}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\widetilde U=x_0+a[-1,1]^3\\), use \\(x=x_0+at\\theta\\). Let the radial self-homeomorphism \\(\\Phi\\) have\n\\[\n\\phi(t)=\n\\begin{cases}\n(1-\\rho)t/\\alpha,&amp;0\\le t\\le\\alpha,\\\\\n1-\\rho+\\rho(t-\\alpha)/(1-\\alpha),&amp;\\alpha\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIt fixes the boundary and is bi-Lipschitz for fixed \\(\\alpha,\\rho&gt;0\\). A finite Lipschitz constant \\(L\\) for \\(k\\) gives inner energy at most \\(Ca^3L^p\\alpha^{3-p}\\); choose \\(\\alpha\\) after \\(k\\).</p><p>On the outer region \\(|D\\Phi|\\le C/t\\), and cube-polar integration gives\n\\[\n\\int_{\\alpha&lt;t&lt;1}|D(k\\Phi)|^p\n\\le C_pa^3\\int_\\alpha^1t^{2-p}\n\\int_{\\partial[-1,1]^3}|Dk(x_0+a\\phi(t)\\theta)|^p\\,d\\mathcal H^2\\,dt.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\alpha\\), let \\(\\rho\\downarrow0\\) at the chosen radial Lebesgue boundary. The limit superior is bounded by\n\\[\nC_pa\\int_{\\partial\\widetilde U}|Dk|^p\\,d\\mathcal H^2\n\\int_\\alpha^1t^{2-p}\\,dt\n\\le C_p\\ell_U\\int_{\\partial\\widetilde U}|Dk|^p\\,d\\mathcal H^2.\n\\]\nUse \\(\\int_0^1t^{2-p}\\,dt=(3-p)^{-1}\\), choose summable errors, and sum (2.45). Local finiteness and positive fixed radial slopes preserve local bi-Lipschitzness. Every new value stays in the old image.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Choose locally finitely many sites \\(q_i\\in\\Lambda\\), one contact \\(x_i\\) per ordinary site and both protected contacts per paired site, and include every exterior support.", "statement": "<p>**Proper convex product quantizer — original Proposition 3.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(F_b,O,O_1\\) and protected endpoint boxes are from Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(b_1\\) is from Lemma 6.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\psi=|x|^2/2+b_1+b_2\\), \\(\\varphi\\) its lower convex envelope, and\n\\[\nF(q)=\\varphi^*(q)=\\sup_x(q\\cdot x-\\psi(x)).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(m&gt;0\\) is the absolute dual separation constant of Lemma 9.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;c&lt;m\\), \\(c&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Choose locally finitely many sites \\(q_i\\in\\Lambda\\), one contact \\(x_i\\) per ordinary site and both protected contacts per paired site, and include every exterior support. Set\n\\[\nG(q)=\\sup_i\\left\\{F(q_i)+x_i\\cdot(q-q_i)+\\frac c2|q-q_i|^2\\right\\},\n\\qquad T=(\\partial G)^{-1}.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nThen \\(T:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3\\) is single-valued, \\(c^{-1}\\)-Lipschitz, identity outside \\(\\Lambda\\), and a proper surjection \\(\\Lambda\\to\\Lambda\\), as finely close to the identity as prescribed.</p><p>Inside \\(\\Lambda\\), \\(G-c|q|^2/2\\) is a locally finite affine maximum with compact convex polyhedral cells. Writing \\(p_i=x_i-cq_i\\), over a relatively open face \\(P\\),\n\\[\n\\partial G(q)=cq+\\operatorname{conv}\\{p_i:i\\in I(P)\\},\n\\qquad T(cq+v)=q.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThe two factors have complementary orthogonal direction spaces. Each support owns a genuine full-dimensional output cell \\(C_i\\). The closed input blocks\n\\[\nX_i=cC_i+p_i\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nare compactly contained in \\(O\\), pairwise disjoint, and have locally finite mutually disjoint protective neighborhoods in exact carrier regions. Outside their union, \\(T\\) takes values in the two-skeleton.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a support \\(L_i\\) in (3.4), Lemma 9.2 gives\n\\[\nL_i(q)\\le F(q)-\\frac{m-c}{2}|q-q_i|^2.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nA sufficiently fine locally finite net makes \\(F-e\\le G\\le F\\). Choose\n\\[\n\\tau(q)=\\sqrt{2e(q)/(m-c)}&lt;\\tfrac14\\operatorname{dist}(q,\\Lambda^c).\n\\]\nEvery active site lies within \\(\\tau(q)\\). Exterior and remote supports are uniformly below the maximum, leaving finitely many locally. This proves the local polyhedral description, including boundaries.</p><p>Strong convexity gives a unique minimizer of \\(G(q)-y\\cdot q\\), defining \\(T(y)\\), and\n\\[\n(y-y')\\cdot(T(y)-T(y'))\\ge c|T(y)-T(y')|^2.\n\\]\nExterior \\(G=F=|q|^2/2\\) gives \\(\\partial G(q)=\\{q\\}\\). Interior inputs are convex combinations of\n\\(x_i+c(q-q_i)\\), finely close to \\(q\\); they remain in \\(\\Lambda\\). This gives onto-ness, fine motion, and properness.</p><p>Subtract the common quadratic. Equalities of active affine functions have normals \\(p_i-p_j\\), spanning the normal space of the face. This gives complementary orthogonal products and (3.5). At its own site an ordinary support strictly dominates other-site supports; a protected pair wins on opposite sides of its tie plane. Thus each owns a full cell.</p><p>Strong monotonicity of \\(\\partial F\\) gives\n\\[\n(x_i-x_j)\\cdot(q_i-q_j)\\ge m|q_i-q_j|^2.\n\\]\nConsequently distinct offsets cannot coincide. Intersecting closed blocks would give, by uniqueness of \\(T\\), equal output and equal offsets, a contradiction. Choose \\(\\tau\\) below local contact clearance divided by \\(2c\\), placing blocks in \\(O\\). Activity control prevents their output cells from approaching \\(\\partial\\Lambda\\); boundedness gives compactness. Motion control gives local finiteness and disjoint protective neighborhoods.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "These choices yield \\(b_2\\in C^\\infty(\\Lambda)\\), zero outside \\(\\Lambda\\), such that \\( \\Delta\\psi\\le C\\text{ in }\\Lambda,\\qquad \\Delta\\psi<0\\text{ on }\\Lambda\\setminus O, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Poisson contact exclusion — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\delta&gt;0\\) is \\(1\\)-Lipschitz on \\(\\Lambda\\), zero outside, with \\(\\delta\\le\\operatorname{dist}(\\cdot,\\Lambda^c)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Well sizes are chosen first and residual-volume thresholds in Proposition 6 afterwards.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These choices yield \\(b_2\\in C^\\infty(\\Lambda)\\), zero outside \\(\\Lambda\\), such that\n\\[\n\\Delta\\psi\\le C\\text{ in }\\Lambda,\\qquad\n\\Delta\\psi&lt;0\\text{ on }\\Lambda\\setminus O,\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\\[\n|b_1+b_2|\\le\\epsilon\\delta^2,\\qquad\nb_1+b_2=o(\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\Lambda^c)^2)\\text{ at }\\partial\\Lambda.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nHere \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) is arbitrarily small. The perturbation vanishes near protected endpoint boxes and preserves every protected global affine support.</p>", "proof": "<p>For closed Whitney cubes \\(Q\\) with bounded-overlap enlargements \\(Q^+\\), smooth a neighborhood of \\(E_Q=Q\\setminus O\\) to a density \\(0\\le\\rho_Q\\le1\\), equal to one near \\(E_Q\\), with arbitrarily small normalized support. On a fixed normalized torus solve\n\\[\n\\Delta u_Q=-A\\rho_Q+\\frac A{|\\mathbb T|}\\int_{\\mathbb T}\\rho_Q,\\qquad\n\\int_{\\mathbb T}u_Q=0,\n\\]\nwith \\(A&gt;3+\\sup\\Delta b_1+2\\). Periodic Green-kernel estimates, using \\(q&gt;3\\), give\n\\[\n\\|u_Q\\|_\\infty+\\|Du_Q\\|_\\infty\n\\le C_qA|\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_Q|^{1/q}.\n\\]\nCut off in \\(Q^+\\), rescale by \\(\\ell(Q)^2\\), and obtain \\(v_Q\\) with \\(\\Delta v_Q\\le\\epsilon_Q\\) everywhere and \\(\\Delta v_Q\\le-A+\\epsilon_Q\\) on \\(E_Q\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\chi=0\\) near protected endpoint boxes and \\(\\chi=1\\) outside \\(O_1\\), with transition in \\(O_1\\), before residual thresholds. Set \\(b_2=\\chi\\sum_Qv_Q\\). Cutoff derivative errors involve only the small \\(v_Q,Dv_Q\\); bounded overlap gives (3.1). Local value budgets and boundary-decaying multipliers give (3.2).</p><p>The protected supporting gap is uniformly positive away from endpoint neighborhoods over compact slope sets and grows quadratically at infinity. Make \\(|b_2|\\) below half the minimum of these finitely many positive gap requirements.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For an absolute \\(C_0\\), \\( \\varphi\\in C^{1,1},\\qquad 0\\le D^2\\varphi\\le C_0I \\) distributionally.", "statement": "<p>**Contact curvature and dual separation — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\psi,\\varphi,F\\) are as in Proposition 9.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For an absolute \\(C_0\\),\n\\[\n\\varphi\\in C^{1,1},\\qquad 0\\le D^2\\varphi\\le C_0I\n\\]\ndistributionally. With \\(m=1/C_0\\), reduced if necessary, every maximizing contact \\(x_i\\) at \\(q_i\\) satisfies\n\\[\nF(q)\\ge F(q_i)+x_i\\cdot(q-q_i)+\\frac m2|q-q_i|^2.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nContacts may be made arbitrarily finely close to their slopes. For \\(q\\notin\\Lambda\\), the unique contact is \\(q\\), and \\(F(q)=|q|^2/2\\). For \\(q\\in\\Lambda\\), all contacts lie in \\(O\\); their union over a compact slope set is compactly contained in \\(O\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Quadratic growth gives compact nonempty contact sets and contact combinations of at most four points: minimize averages at fixed barycenter, then use supporting-plane separation and Carathéodory. At an interior contact \\(D^2\\psi\\ge0\\), and the Laplacian bound bounds each eigenvalue above. At boundary or exterior contacts, (3.2) gives the quadratic Taylor expansion with \\(o(|v|^2)\\) remainder.</p><p>Translate all points of a contact combination by \\(\\pm v\\). This gives\n\\[\n\\limsup_{v\\to0}\n\\frac{\\varphi(x+v)+\\varphi(x-v)-2\\varphi(x)}{|v|^2}\\le C_0.\n\\]\nThe chord-minimum argument on lines gives semiconcavity. Convexity then gives \\(C^{1,1}\\) regularity and the global quadratic upper tangent bound. At a contact \\(D\\varphi(x_i)=q_i\\); substitute \\(v=(q-q_i)/C_0\\) in the conjugate definition to get (3.3).</p><p>For a maximizing contact \\(x\\) at \\(q\\), comparison with \\(x=q\\), with \\(d=|x-q|\\), gives\n\\[\n\\tfrac12d^2\\le\\epsilon\\{(\\delta(q)+d)^2+\\delta(q)^2\\},\n\\]\nhence \\(d\\le C\\sqrt\\epsilon\\,\\delta(q)\\). Exterior \\(q\\) gives \\(d=0\\); interior contacts remain inside \\(\\Lambda\\). A contact in \\(\\Lambda\\setminus O\\) would have nonnegative Laplacian, contradicting (3.1). Closedness of the maximizing graph and boundedness over compact slope sets give compact containment.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The sites can be chosen with paired rectangular arrays and a locally bi-Lipschitz self-homeomorphism \\(J\\), identity near every \\(X_i\\), such that \\(TJ\\) has an absolute Lipschitz bound.", "statement": "<p>**Calibration and strict quantizers — original Lemmas 3.4 and 3.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the absolute choice \\(c=\\min\\{m,1\\}/2\\) in Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F_b,F_\\kappa\\) and the retained rank data are from Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The sites can be chosen with paired rectangular arrays and a locally bi-Lipschitz self-homeomorphism \\(J\\), identity near every \\(X_i\\), such that \\(TJ\\) has an absolute Lipschitz bound. Put\n\\[\n\\widehat F=JF_b,\\qquad q_0=T\\widehat F.\n\\]\nUp to arbitrarily small source-weight loss, retained rank-\\(k\\) data, \\(k=1,2\\), map into rectangular true two-face interiors, and\n\\[\nDq_0=a_*^{-1}\\operatorname{proj}_{n^\\perp}DF_\\kappa,\\qquad\n|Dq_0-Df|\\le C(\\lambda+1-a_*)|Df|,\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nwhere \\(c&lt;a_*&lt;1\\) is arbitrarily close to one. Rank is \\(k\\), and \\(\\ker Df\\subset\\ker Dq_0\\). Rank-one rectangles may have arbitrarily small prescribed short-to-long aspect and long axis arbitrarily close to the range line. Further compact sector margins and uniform jet tests are available. Also \\(|Dq_0|\\le C|DF_b|\\).</p><p>The quantizer \\(T\\) admits proper locally bi-Lipschitz self-homeomorphisms \\(T_\\eta:\\Lambda\\to\\Lambda\\) with, for any positive continuous \\(\\epsilon_0\\),\n\\[\n|T_\\eta-T|&lt;\\epsilon_0,\\qquad |DT_\\eta-DT|&lt;\\epsilon_0\\quad\\text{a.e.}\n\\]\nTangential derivatives agree on common strata. If \\(h\\) is locally bi-Lipschitz, the pushforward under \\(Th\\) of volume on each product interior is absolutely continuous with respect to the dimensional measure of its output stratum.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a protected plane take Cartesian sites \\(q_i=z+u_i\\) and contacts \\(x_i^\\pm=z+u_i\\pm d_\\perp n\\), using\n\\[\nF(z+u)=\\tfrac12|z+u|^2-d_\\perp^2.\n\\]\nExclude nonmatching sites in a narrow tube. Choose periods much smaller than the tube and approximation tolerances. Nearest-pair deficits are \\(O(d_1^2+d_2^2)\\), while other-site deficits have a fixed positive gap on a smaller protected region. Dominance persists in a slab. Cartesian bisectors are exact, and signs tie on the central plane. There\n\\[\nT(z+h+vn)=z+(Q_{c,1}(h_1),Q_{c,2}(h_2))_{\\mathrm{horiz}},\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q_c\\) has slope \\(1/c\\) on a fraction \\(c\\) of each period and is constant on complementary half-gaps.</p><p>Choose a cutoff \\(a(h,v)\\in[c,a_*]\\), equal to \\(a_*\\) near data and \\(c\\) off the slab. Let \\(R_a\\) send the middle interval of fraction \\(a\\) onto that of fraction \\(c\\), fixing period endpoints, so \\(Q_cR_a=Q_a\\). Its inverse \\(B_a\\) satisfies \\(|\\partial_aB_a|\\le d/2\\). For\n\\[\nJ(h,v)=(R_{a(h,v),1}(h_1),R_{a(h,v),2}(h_2),v),\n\\]\nthe inverse equations \\(h_j=B_{a(h,v),j}(w_j)\\) are a contraction when\n\\(\\sqrt{d_1^2+d_2^2}\\operatorname{Lip}a&lt;1/2\\). Thus \\(J\\) is bi-Lipschitz. Its support avoids full blocks. Ordinary slopes of \\(Q_a\\) are bounded by \\(1/c\\), and parameter slopes by \\(C_cd\\); hence \\(TJ\\) is uniformly Lipschitz.</p><p>Fix phase-independent nested boxes, excluded tubes, and cutoff margins first. Random independent phases put each datum in both middle fractions with probability \\(a_*^2\\). Conditional normalized output coordinates are uniform in the rectangle. Fubini therefore permits small loss and avoidance of centers, perimeters, sector rays, and small neighborhoods, even for singular projected data. Differentiate in strict middle intervals to obtain (3.8); choose errors below retained positive singular-value bounds to preserve rank. Kernel inclusion prevents rank increase.</p><p>For strict quantizers put, with \\(q=T(y)\\),\n\\[\nT_\\eta(y)=q+\\eta(q)(y-q),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) is smooth and \\(\\|\\eta\\|_\\infty,\\operatorname{Lip}\\eta\\) are small. If \\(M=\\sup|y-T(y)|\\),\n\\[\n(T_\\eta(y_1)-T_\\eta(y_2))\\cdot(q_1-q_2)\n\\ge(1-\\|\\eta\\|_\\infty-M\\operatorname{Lip}\\eta)|q_1-q_2|^2\n\\ge\\tfrac12|q_1-q_2|^2.\n\\]\nThis controls \\(q\\)-differences; the defining formula and positive local lower bounds of \\(\\eta\\) control \\(y\\)-differences. Fine motion gives properness and values in \\(\\Lambda\\). Invariance of domain and relatively closed image give onto-ness. Finally\n\\[\nD(T_\\eta-T)=\\eta(T)(I-DT)+(y-T)\\otimes(D\\eta(T)DT).\n\\]\nProperness transfers local tolerances to output variables, and Lemma 1.1 chooses \\(\\eta,D\\eta\\). Orthogonal product projection proves tangential agreement and, by Fubini and bi-Lipschitz change of variables, dimensional absolute continuity. At vertex levels \\(D(Th)=0\\) almost everywhere.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(M_y\\) is a locally finite PL three-manifold with boundary.", "statement": "<p>**Graph exterior and its two wall colors — original Proposition 3.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the quantizer of Propositions 9–10.</p><p class=\"li\">Its true two-faces have conforming convex polygonal subcells \\(D\\) and centers \\(d_D\\), as in Lemma 11.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\xi_e(s)\\) be arclength on a perimeter subedge \\(e\\), and\n\\[\nR_{D,e}(t,s)=d_D+r(\\xi_e(s)-d_D),\\qquad r=e^{t/L_D}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose small \\(r_{u,D}&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In input target coordinates remove the closed full blocks \\(X_i\\) and open cores of interval prisms over \\(d_D+r_{u,D}(D-d_D)\\), attached at their ends to the full blocks.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(M_y\\) is the closure of the remainder, and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the subedge graph.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(M_y\\) is a locally finite PL three-manifold with boundary. Radial projection defines a continuous \\(\\pi_T:M_y\\to\\Gamma\\). Each component is a possibly noncompact graph handlebody; every compact PL interior sphere bounds a PL ball, and\n\\[\nH_2(M_y;\\mathbb Z)=0.\n\\tag{3.12}\n\\]\nThe radial walls \\(A_D(t)\\) are incompressible annuli; the meridian walls \\(B_e(s)=\\pi_T^{-1}(\\xi_e(s))\\) are disks. Walls within one color are disjoint. An incident pair has two boundary intersections, one on each annular end, and a nonincident pair none. Transverse individually standard walls with this incidence have one spanning arc per incident pair and otherwise only circles, each contractible on both walls.</p>", "proof": "<p>Products over true faces, edges, and vertices are respectively polygon times normal interval, interval times offset polygon, and convex offset polytope. They glue as subdifferentials of the same function. Removing full blocks and small central prisms gives PL half-ball boundary charts. Radial projections agree across subedges and are constant on offset fibres, hence continuous.</p><p>Cut at one meridian per subedge. A chamber around a subdivided graph vertex is a ball. For a vertex inside a true face, the radial strips form a disk fan times interval. Inside a true edge, start from the offset-polygon prism and attach incident face balls along disjoint boundary disks. At a true vertex, start from the convex offset polytope, attach edge products to facets, then face-fan products along boundary disks prescribed by the normal fan. Repeated ball gluing along disks gives a ball, without simplicity assumptions.</p><p>Restoring meridian collars attaches one-handles to zero-handles, giving a graph handlebody and \\(H_2=0\\). A compact sphere lies in a finite graph chunk. Cut its handlebody along meridians and remove innermost sphere intersections; it reduces to a sphere in a ball, which bounds a ball by PL Schoenflies. Restore collars to obtain a ball in \\(M_y\\).</p><p>An annular core projects to a reduced perimeter loop in \\(\\Gamma\\), so its nonzero powers are nontrivial. Hence the annulus is incompressible. A meridian is two spoke rectangles at an artificial edge, or a convex offset polygon with incident spoke rectangles at a true edge; each is a disk. Boundary incidence is explicit. A transverse pair has the prescribed one arc and possible circles. Each circle bounds a disk in the meridian and is null-homotopic in \\(M_y\\); incompressibility makes it null-homotopic, hence disk-bounding, on the annulus.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "True two-faces admit locally finite conforming convex subdivisions with centers \\(d_D\\).", "statement": "<p>**Face subdivision — original Lemma 3.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">True faces form the locally finite polyhedral diagram.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(q_0=T\\widehat F\\) is proper and locally Sobolev.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>True two-faces admit locally finite conforming convex subdivisions with centers \\(d_D\\). Protected rectangles may remain rectangular or be subdivided by rectangular grids. Diameter-to-center-clearance ratios are absolute away from prescribed small original-vertex neighborhoods, and finite face-dependent constants suffice inside them. The latter regions can have arbitrarily small weighted \\(\\int|Dq_0|^p\\). Additional protected-center rays to inserted perimeter vertices can avoid almost every retained datum.</p>", "proof": "<p>Intersect planar Voronoi cells of a maximal separated \\(d\\)-net with the convex face. Maximality bounds diameters by \\(Cd\\). Interpolating generators toward a fixed interior disk gives inscribed disks of radius \\(c_Pd\\). Away from original vertices, sufficiently small neighborhoods meet at most one supporting edge, giving an absolute inscribed-disk fraction.</p><p>Overlay shared-edge subdivisions, inserting collinear vertices. Generic finite nets give transverse edge intersections and positive finite angle margins; small sliding preserves convexity and clearance. Continuous perturbation of an extra perimeter vertex avoids any fixed ray with probability one; Fubini gives avoidance for the retained measure.</p><p>Sobolev derivatives vanish almost everywhere on a fixed level set. Thus \\(Dq_0=0\\) on \\(\\{q_0=v\\}\\) for an original vertex, and\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\rho\\downarrow0}\\int_{\\{|q_0-v|&lt;\\rho\\}}|Dq_0|^p=0.\n\\]\nFix finite face factors first, choose summably affordable vertex neighborhoods, then choose subdivision scales.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On an open sector, \\( |D_qt|+|D_qs|\\le C_D/r,\\qquad |\\partial_tR_{D,e}|+|\\partial_sR_{D,e}|\\le Cr. \\) The inverse constant depends only on clearance ratio; the forward constant is absolute.", "statement": "<p>**Polar estimates — original Lemma 3.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(L_D\\) is comparable to the maximum radius of \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In a rank-one rectangle of half-length \\(L\\), half-width \\(\\gamma L\\), take \\(L_D=L\\) and its midpoint as center.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(v_1\\) is its unit long-axis vector.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On an open sector,\n\\[\n|D_qt|+|D_qs|\\le C_D/r,\\qquad\n|\\partial_tR_{D,e}|+|\\partial_sR_{D,e}|\\le Cr.\n\\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nThe inverse constant depends only on clearance ratio; the forward constant is absolute. In the rectangle,\n\\[\n|D_qt(v_1)|+|D_qs(v_1)|\\le(1+\\gamma)/r,\\qquad\n\\max\\{|\\partial_tR|,|\\partial_sR|\\}\\le\\sqrt{1+\\gamma^2}\\,r.\n\\tag{3.11}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Translate the center to zero. If the supporting line is \\(\\nu\\cdot\\xi=b&gt;0\\), then\n\\[\nr=(\\nu\\cdot q)/b,\\quad Dr=\\nu/b,\\quad Dt=L_D\\nu/(br),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\xi'(s)Ds=\\frac1r\\left(I-\\frac{q\\otimes\\nu}{br}\\right)\n\\]\non the sector plane. Clearance and maximum radius give inverse bounds. Directly,\n\\(\\partial_tR=r\\xi/L_D\\), \\(\\partial_sR=r\\xi'\\).\nOn a short end the long-axis derivatives are \\(1/r\\) and at most \\(\\gamma/r\\); on a long side they are \\(0\\) and \\(1/r\\). Splitting straight sides only changes the additive origin of \\(s\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One may choose \\(0<r_{u,D}\\ll a_D\\), a PL homeomorphism \\(h_*:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\), and a proper continuous locally \\(W^{1,p}\\) map \\(K:\\Lambda\\to\\Lambda\\), with \\( F_*=Kh_*,\\qquad M_x=h_*^{-1}(M_y),\\qquad q_*=TF_*, \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Carrier exact on central tubes — original Lemma 3.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(T\\) and \\(M_y\\) are from Propositions 9–11.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\widehat F:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) is the calibrated proper continuous locally \\(W^{1,p}\\) carrier, with connected fibres, countable nonsingleton-value set \\(E_0\\), and relative opening.</p><p class=\"li\">It is exactly PL over neighborhoods of all \\(X_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Positive activation radii \\(a_D\\) are fixed independently of central radii.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One may choose \\(0&lt;r_{u,D}\\ll a_D\\), a PL homeomorphism \\(h_*:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\), and a proper continuous locally \\(W^{1,p}\\) map \\(K:\\Lambda\\to\\Lambda\\), with\n\\[\nF_*=Kh_*,\\qquad M_x=h_*^{-1}(M_y),\\qquad q_*=TF_*,\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(K=\\operatorname{id}\\) on an open neighborhood of full blocks and removed central prisms. Thus \\(F_*^{-1}(M_y)=M_x\\), and \\(F_*=h_*\\) near \\(\\partial M_x\\), with the prescribed PL data.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. \\(K\\) is a fine limit of homeomorphisms retaining those exact data, has nonempty connected fibres, and only countably many nonsingleton values.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Changes from \\(\\widehat F\\) occur in locally finite compact face neighborhoods whose old and new labels have \\(r&lt;a_D\\), with arbitrary fine motion. Outside them \\(F_*=\\widehat F\\). Labels and radial/perimeter coordinates have finite ambient local Sobolev budgets on compact portions of \\(M_x\\), with no uniform low-region bound asserted.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. A locally finite closed family \\(S\\) of limiting inner cube seams accounts for possible failures of the local fibre formula below. Its nonexact parts avoid a neighborhood of \\(\\partial M_x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First fix enlarged disjoint PL protection balls around \\(X_i\\), central prisms, elongated affine replacement boxes, and sampling margins, before choosing \\(h_*\\). The transverse box lies in \\(r\\ll a_D\\); its inner longitudinal interval contains the nonexact tube portion and its end strips lie in exact regions. Choose shell and transverse widths to meet a properness tolerance \\(\\tau\\), where for a compact exhaustion \\(C_m\\Subset\\operatorname{int}C_{m+1}\\),\n\\[\n\\tau(z)&lt;\\tfrac14\\operatorname{dist}(z,C_m)\\quad\n(z\\notin\\operatorname{int}C_{m+1}).\n\\]\nOpen \\(\\widehat F\\) by Lemma 6.2 and approximate relatively by Lemma 2.1, obtaining \\(h_*\\) so fine that \\(K_0=\\widehat Fh_*^{-1}\\) is close to the identity on every fixed sampling neighborhood and exact on the protections. The prescribed motion bounds give, for resulting maps \\(g\\),\n\\[\ng^{-1}(C_m)\\subset K_0^{-1}(C_{m+1}).\n\\tag{3.13}\n\\]</p><p>In each normalized box use Proposition 5's skeleton immersion. Reserve an identity outer band and put end-strip samplings inside the exact region. On usable lift pieces set\n\\[\nH(x)=(i|_{U_x})^{-1}K_0(i(x)).\n\\tag{3.14}\n\\]\nExistence and branch agreement need only value closeness, not injectivity of \\(K_0\\). The same chain rule and weighted overlap argument as (2.24)–(2.25) gives finite shell energy. On each omitted nonidentity hole, squeeze the buffer to a center \\(v_\\sigma\\), use \\(J_\\sigma H\\) on the lift, and declare the hole constant. These definitions agree on collars. Put the identity on the inner cube and \\(K_0\\) outside. \\(O(\\ell)\\) displacements and strict exterior-side margins give continuity and Sobolev gluing at inner seams.</p><p>Open \\(K_0\\) to homeomorphisms \\(k_n\\), lift them by the same branches, fill holes topologically, and use strict cap squeezes tending to the nonstrict ones. Outputs on holes tend to their declared constants regardless of fillings. Small depth scales have uniformly small displacement, giving compact convergence, then fine diagonal convergence. Uniform inverse compactness from (3.13) proves properness and onto-ness of \\(K\\). Fibres are connected: shrinking target balls have connected compact inverse images under approximants; Hausdorff limits connect any two points of a limiting fibre.</p><p>For a non-cap value \\(y\\), let \\(x'=J_{\\mathrm{cap}}^{-1}(y)\\). Away from inner seams, choose one injective immersion chart \\(U\\), a source ball of radius comparable to \\(\\ell\\), and an image sampling ball of radius comparable to \\(s\\). Degree one and closeness put the old fibre over \\(i(x')\\) inside the sampling ball; connectedness excludes further components. Its pullback is the new local fibre, and connectedness of the new fibre excludes all other preimages. Thus, on a neighborhood of \\(y\\),\n\\[\nK^{-1}(y)=(i|_U)^{-1}\n\\left(K_0^{-1}\\bigl(i(J_{\\mathrm{cap}}^{-1}(y))\\bigr)\\right).\n\\tag{3.15}\n\\]\nA countable chart cover shows countably many non-cap exceptional values, inherited from \\(E_0\\); cap centers are countable. Exact regions and inner seams have singleton fibres by isolation and connectedness. The identity neighborhood of thick cores therefore has no other preimages, proving \\(K^{-1}(M_y)=M_y\\).</p><p>Finally, on compact exterior portions the central radius is bounded away from zero and only finitely many sector/product charts occur. Local Lipschitz coordinate extensions and Sobolev composition give finite ambient budgets.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are homeomorphisms \\(f_n:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\), converging finely to \\(F_*\\), equal to it near \\(\\partial M_x\\) and on other exact regions, such that …", "statement": "<p>**Exact edge matching during opening — original Lemma 3.10 and Remark 3.11.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F_*,K,h_*,M_x,M_y,S\\) from Proposition 12.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal E\\) is a locally finite family of mesh edges in \\(M_x\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal W\\) is a locally finite sampled family of standard walls.</p><p class=\"li\">Its entire union \\(W=\\bigcup\\mathcal W\\) is relatively closed in \\(M_y\\) and avoids every nonsingleton value of \\(K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The actual hit set\n\\[\n\\left(\\bigcup\\mathcal E\\right)\\cap F_*^{-1}(W)\n\\]\nis finite on compact localizations, and no hit outside exact regions lies on \\(S\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are homeomorphisms \\(f_n:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\), converging finely to \\(F_*\\), equal to it near \\(\\partial M_x\\) and on other exact regions, such that\n\\[\nf_n^{-1}(W_0)\\cap e=F_*^{-1}(W_0)\\cap e\n\\quad(W_0\\in\\mathcal W,\\ e\\in\\mathcal E).\n\\]\nThey map \\(M_x\\) onto \\(M_y\\) with the prescribed boundary map. The assertion is for these sampled tests, not arbitrary closed tests accumulating through inner lift scales.</p>", "proof": "<p>At a nonexact hit, the local formula (3.15) holds on a neighborhood. For a compact wall patch \\(P\\) there,\n\\[\nC_P=i(J_{\\mathrm{cap}}^{-1}(P))\n\\tag{3.16}\n\\]\nis compact and disjoint from \\(E_0\\): an old nonsingleton fibre would give a new nonsingleton fibre on the whole wall. At an unchanged hit, singleton fibres and properness put the inverse of a smaller neighborhood entirely in the unchanged region, so use a direct patch.</p><p>Compact-local hit finiteness gives locally finite charts, involving finitely many depth scales per compact. The corresponding old tests \\(C_P\\) are locally finite and relatively closed. Adjoin protected exact data and apply Lemma 6.2 to obtain old openings agreeing over all these tests, including inverse evaluations.</p><p>Lift and fill as in Proposition 12. Open caps only over sufficiently small balls about their centers, so inverse cap maps remain exactly \\(J_{\\mathrm{cap}}^{-1}\\) on all tested patches. Each center has positive individual clearance from the closed relevant tests. Thus inverse images of wall patches are unchanged. Outside source hit neighborhoods, compact edge remainders have a positive gap from \\(W\\); fine tolerances prevent new hits.</p><p>For later applicability, ACL and coarea provide finite hits for almost every sampled scalar level. Generic edge intersections with nonexact seams have parameter measure zero, whose scalar-label images are null by absolute continuity. Countably many exceptional values exclude only countably many radial or intermediate meridian levels; graph vertices are not intermediate meridian values.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are finite unions of separated occupied closed intervals \\(E_j^{\\mathrm{occ}}\\subset(0,w_j)\\), with \\( |E_j^{\\mathrm{occ}}|\\ge(1-\\zeta)w_j, \\) and a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism \\(h:M_x\\to M_y\\), equal to …", "statement": "<p>**Conditional wall realization — original Theorem 4.11, used as an intermediate result.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(p&gt;2\\), Propositions 9–13, and \\(q_*=TF_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(M_x,M_y\\) are irreducible, with \\(H_2(M_x;\\mathbb Z)=0\\), and \\(h_*:M_x\\to M_y\\) is the fixed boundary reference.</p><p class=\"li\">Standard walls are \\(A(D,t)\\) and \\(B(e,s)\\), with \\(t=L_D\\log r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\zeta&lt;1\\), \\(c_*&gt;0\\), local label/value accuracies, and buffered ball or half-ball charts \\(K_a\\Subset U_a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A two-color pre-stack system, as in original Definition 4.2, consists of compact collars\n\\[\nE_j:\\Sigma_j\\times[0,w_j]\\to C_j,\\qquad u_j(E_j(z,\\lambda))=\\lambda,\n\\]\nwith the following complete contract:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Each \\(E_j\\) is bi-Lipschitz. \\(\\Sigma_j\\) is an essential annulus for color \\(A\\), a disk for \\(B\\); levels are proper, and the closed same-color collars are disjoint and locally finite.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. Each root interval has an increasing affine correspondence \\(p_j:[0,w_j]\\to I_j\\) to its true target label interval. Every level has the exact \\(h_*\\)-pulled-back boundary trace and increasing-label coorientation, including both annular ends.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Opposite-color collar intersections are finite disjoint unions of full products\n\\[\nQ\\times[0,w_j]\\times[0,w_k],\\qquad u_j=\\lambda,\\quad u_k=\\mu,\n\\]\nwhere \\(Q\\) is a circle or compact interval; interval endpoints are precisely on \\(\\partial M_x\\). There are no interior parameter terminations or other intersection pieces.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. Each collar's whole subdivision by opposite boxes and complementary and boundary pieces is jointly bi-Lipschitz trivial over its root parameter, preserving every concurrent opposite parameter.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">5. Finite-on-compacts constant or affine parameter cuts admit common ambient locally bi-Lipschitz changed coordinates making the pieces PL simultaneously, preserving boundary and incidences.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">6. Each \\(u_j\\) is Lipschitz on its closed collar. A measurable \\(G\\ge0\\) dominates its point-to-base upper local slope there, with adjacent maxima, both slopes at essential boxes, and actual interface slopes on volume-null exceptional strata.</p><p class=\"li\">Prior capacities, fixed before guards, samples, and final resolution, satisfy\n\\[\n\\int_{U_a}G^p\\le C_a&lt;\\infty.\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Guards, as in original Definition 4.6, are compact connected unions \\(\\kappa_g\\) of two paths in a constant-\\(Th_g\\) fibre from each sufficiently fine source-net point to boundary anchors on distinct full blocks. Here \\(h_g\\) is a sufficiently fine homeomorphic approximation to \\(F_*\\), exact on boundary data. Write \\(q_g=Th_g(g)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Pre-stacks avoid all guards. Sampling intervals obey Lemma 14.1, with ordered disjoint \\(I_j\\) and \\(|I_j|\\asymp c_*w_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Guard spacings and stair accuracies are chosen using only the prior capacities and requested accuracies.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are finite unions of separated occupied closed intervals \\(E_j^{\\mathrm{occ}}\\subset(0,w_j)\\), with\n\\[\n|E_j^{\\mathrm{occ}}|\\ge(1-\\zeta)w_j,\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nand a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism \\(h:M_x\\to M_y\\), equal to \\(h_*\\) on the boundary, sending every occupied routed layer at \\(\\lambda\\) to its standard layer at \\(p_j(\\lambda)\\). The same affine \\(p_j\\) is used on all occupied components. It extends by \\(h_*\\) on removed blocks and tubes, and \\(q_h=Th\\) is as finely close to \\(q_*\\) as prescribed.</p><p>This statement is conditional: existence of pre-stacks satisfying its geometric and prior-energy hypotheses is supplied later.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose occupied intervals using Lemma 14.7. Cut at both endpoints of each occupied \\(B\\)-interval. Target chambers are balls by Proposition 11. A corresponding source boundary patch, capped by new gate disks and pushed through a boundary collar into the interior, is a sphere. Irreducibility gives an inward ball. No extra gate lies inside; the two prescribed balls occupy opposite gate sides. Compatible collar pushes, disjoint spheres, and their distinguished boundary-patch points exclude overlapping interiors. Local finiteness and a compact transverse path exclude leftover components. Thus source and target gate-ball decompositions correspond.</p><p>On gates, endpoint \\(A\\)-arcs correspond by their boundary endpoint pairs. Cutting endpoint annuli along spanning gate arcs leaves disks; boundary slots identify their pieces, including twisting. Those disks lie in matching gate balls. Disjoint proper tame disks cut balls into balls indexed by boundary patches, so endpoint chamber incidence matches.</p><p>Use the common changed PL coordinates to recognize finite disk and ball models. Map both-active regions as arc times two occupied intervals, preserving both parameters and interpolating the arc coordinate between exact endpoint correspondences. In single-active stacks, complementary fibres are disks. Extend their assigned circle maps by\n\\[\n(r\\theta,\\lambda)\\longmapsto(rg_\\lambda(\\theta),\\lambda),\n\\]\njointly bi-Lipschitz because the factor \\(r\\) controls parameter variation at the center. Remaining neither-active cells are balls and extend by coning. Finite-on-compacts compatible pieces glue to a locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphism, extended by \\(h_*\\) on removed regions.</p><p>Remake stairs with increments only on occupied intervals. To pin \\(q_h(x)\\), take any point in its constant fibre and a guard point. Pull back a target fibre path to an exact boundary anchor; append a guard path to another anchor. Almost every occupied layer is missed by these appended paths. Apply Lemma 14.3 with point-distance star/perimeter potentials and occupied-stair coefficients. Any connector between the fibre and guard has fixed positive \\(G\\)-cost if their labels are separated.</p><p>If \\(q_h-q_*\\) is too large, follow the constant-\\(q_h\\) fibre toward its exact anchor, obtaining a fixed-diameter discrepant stretch. The same packing and sphere argument as Lemma 14.6 contradicts the prior capacity. This controls \\(Th\\), not the derivative of the qualitative extension.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One can choose \\(N\\), with \\(Nw/\\ell=1+O(\\zeta)\\), and an endpoint-fixing nondecreasing stair \\(S\\), constant outside selected \\(p_j(E_j^{\\mathrm{occ}})\\), increasing by \\(\\ell/N\\) on each \\(I_j\\), with root-parameter …", "statement": "<p>**Stairs and sampling — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A feature interval has length \\(\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Root intervals have equal weight \\(w_j=w\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Occupied sets satisfy (4.1), or are initially the whole root intervals.</p><p class=\"li\">Finitely many forbidden intervals of arbitrarily small total length and finitely many integrable edge-count tests are prescribed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can choose \\(N\\), with \\(Nw/\\ell=1+O(\\zeta)\\), and an endpoint-fixing nondecreasing stair \\(S\\), constant outside selected \\(p_j(E_j^{\\mathrm{occ}})\\), increasing by \\(\\ell/N\\) on each \\(I_j\\), with root-parameter speed at most \\(1+O(\\zeta)\\), target-label Lipschitz constant \\(O(c_*^{-1})\\), and arbitrarily small displacement.</p><p>Weighted sample counts have expected density at most \\(1+O(\\zeta+c_*)\\) against label measure, and satisfy the associated upper bounds with arbitrarily small additive errors and probability tending to one under refinement.</p>", "proof": "<p>Remove forbidden intervals, split remaining components into slots, and allocate counts proportionally to component lengths. Small \\(w\\) makes rounding and slot-length errors small, also with one common \\(w\\) on finitely many features. Sample in slot middles wide enough for \\(I_j\\). Give the stair increase \\(\\ell/N\\) uniformly over occupied root length. Its speed is\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ell}{N|E_j^{\\mathrm{occ}}|}\n\\le\\frac{\\ell}{Nw(1-\\zeta)}=1+O(\\zeta).\n\\]\nSince \\(p_j'\\asymp c_*\\), the label slope is \\(O(c_*^{-1})\\). Ordered slots bound displacement by maximum slot length, rounding, and total forbidden length.</p><p>For an integrable nonnegative count \\(n\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[w_jn(\\tau_j)]\n=\\frac{w_j}{|J_j^{\\mathrm{allow}}|}\n\\int_{J_j^{\\mathrm{allow}}}n(t)\\,dt.\n\\]\nFor bounded \\(n\\), independence and \\(\\max w_j\\to0\\) force variance to zero. Truncate general \\(n\\in L^1\\), treating tails by the first moment. Use finite tests simultaneously, and summable failure allowances on an exhaustion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Outside a null set of root parameters, routing produces a connected oriented properly embedded surface \\(L_{j,\\lambda}\\), with exactly the original root boundary and coorientation.", "statement": "<p>**Diagonal routing and good parameters — original Proposition 4.3 and Remark 4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The pre-stack contract of Proposition 14 holds.</p><p class=\"li\">In a circle box use \\((\\theta,u,v)\\in S^1\\times[0,a]\\times[0,b]\\), reversing coordinates as necessary so that \\(v=0,u=0\\) face the exterior of bounded inner disks.</p><p class=\"li\">The diagonal parameter is\n\\[\nd=a-u+v.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">No routing occurs in essential arc boxes.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Outside a null set of root parameters, routing produces a connected oriented properly embedded surface \\(L_{j,\\lambda}\\), with exactly the original root boundary and coorientation. Distinct surfaces are disjoint except at prescribed essential opposite-root arcs. Each is a tree assembly of punctured disks and annuli, possibly with ends at infinity. For every compact set, only finitely many reached states have their entire original parent collar meeting that set.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every intersection circle is trivial on both walls: it bounds a disk on the disk wall and cannot be essential on the annulus. Cut original levels along circle strips. There is one root piece containing the actual boundary, including both annular ends; all other pieces are punctured disks, each with one incoming rim and remaining outgoing rims. Essential arc boxes lie on root pieces.</p><p>The diagonal segments match combined outgoing intervals \\([0,a]\\cup[a,a+b]\\) to incoming intervals \\([0,b]\\cup[b,a+b]\\), by partial affine maps of absolute slope one. Discard corner values \\(0,a,b,a+b\\). A state is a tracked piece and its local parameter. Every nonroot state has a unique predecessor and roots none, so reachable states form trees without repetition or merging between distinct starts.</p><p>Countably many finite itineraries transport parameters by \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\pm\\lambda+c\\). Corner conditions exclude countably many starting values. For a piece \\(P\\) in collar \\(C_k\\), itinerary images from all roots are disjoint, giving\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\int_0^{w_j}N_P(j,\\lambda)\\,d\\lambda\\le w_k.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nFor a compact \\(K\\), only finitely many parent collars meet \\(K\\). Sum (4.3); almost every parameter has finitely many reached states with such parents. Intersect over a compact exhaustion.</p><p>Glue pieces and diagonal annuli for these good parameters. The assembly is locally finite and properly embedded. Orient successively along the tree. Finite capped tree assemblies have the root disk or annulus type. Uniqueness of ancestry gives disjointness, except in unmodified essential root boxes. Ordinary parameter length is conserved without commensurability of weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( I(\\gamma,L)=I(\\gamma,W_{j,\\lambda}). \\) Intersection numbers are defined by general position relative to endpoints.", "statement": "<p>**Conservation of oriented periods — original Proposition 4.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(L=L_{j,\\lambda}\\) is a good routed layer.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(W_{j,\\lambda}\\) is the \\(h_*\\)-pullback of the corresponding standard wall, with increasing-label coorientation.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\gamma\\) is a compact path with endpoints in \\(\\partial M_x\\setminus\\partial L\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nI(\\gamma,L)=I(\\gamma,W_{j,\\lambda}).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nIntersection numbers are defined by general position relative to endpoints. If \\(\\gamma\\) has finitely many actual hits, its signed local crossings give this number, and\n\\[\n|I(\\gamma,L)|\\le\\#(\\gamma\\cap L),\n\\]\ncounting occurrences along the path.</p>", "proof": "<p>Truncate the routing tree at finite depth and cap frontier outgoing rims by their original inside disks. This gives a finite compact oriented two-chain \\(A_n\\). Every cap stays in the original parent collar. Goodness implies that, for sufficiently large \\(n\\), omitted pieces and caps miss a compact neighborhood of the path or any fixed compact homotopy.</p><p>The exact boundary contract gives \\(\\partial A_n=\\partial W_{j,\\lambda}\\). Thus \\(A_n-W_{j,\\lambda}\\) is an absolute compact two-cycle, bounding a finite compact three-chain because \\(H_2(M_x;\\mathbb Z)=0\\). Push cycle and chain slightly into the interior through a boundary collar, avoiding endpoint neighborhoods. The intersection boundary formula gives zero intersection with their difference, proving (4.4).</p><p>At an isolated actual hit, local sides give algebraic crossing \\(1,-1\\), or \\(0\\), equal to the total of a small general-position perturbation. The same argument in proper half-space charts handles boundary hits. Compact gaps prevent additional crossings.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently accurate stairs, every compact rectifiable connector \\(\\sigma\\) from that layer to the guard satisfies \\( \\int_\\sigma G\\,d\\mathcal H^1\\ge c_\\delta>0, \\) independently of sample weights and itinerary …", "statement": "<p>**Crossing lower bound — original Lemma 4.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Pre-stacks avoid guards and have upper slope majorant \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\beta=\\pi_T\\in\\Gamma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\alpha\\) take values in the metric star with one branch per subcell, coordinate \\(b=-t\\ge0\\), and common node \\(b=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Polar reconstruction gives\n\\[\n|q-\\beta|\\le Cb.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an \\(A(D,t_L)\\) root use potentials\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(\\alpha,\\alpha_L),\\qquad\n\\operatorname{dist}_{\\mathbb R^3}(\\beta,\\partial D);\n\\]\nfor a \\(B(e,s_L)\\) root use\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(\\alpha,S_e),\\qquad|\\beta-\\beta_L|,\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(S_e\\) consists of the common node and all branches incident to \\(e\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A guard label is at physical distance at least \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) from a good layer's standard support, on a fixed compact separation test.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently accurate stairs, every compact rectifiable connector \\(\\sigma\\) from that layer to the guard satisfies\n\\[\n\\int_\\sigma G\\,d\\mathcal H^1\\ge c_\\delta&gt;0,\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nindependently of sample weights and itinerary length.</p>", "proof": "<p>The potentials are \\(1\\)-Lipschitz in graph metrics, vanish on the root support, and jointly detect physical distance by (4.5) and polar reconstruction. Opposite-color testing potentials vanish along all essentially incident features.</p><p>Use whole-interval stairs first. For the tested color define\n\\[\nc_j(\\lambda)=\\frac d{d\\lambda}U(S(p_j(\\lambda))).\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nThese are uniformly bounded. Extend the connector along the layer and guard to a boundary-to-boundary path \\(\\gamma\\). For almost every tested parameter with nonzero coefficient, added pieces contribute no intersections: guards avoid all pre-stacks, routed layers are disjoint except at essential boxes, and coefficients vanish on those essential features.</p><p>Lemma 14.3 and one-dimensional oriented level counting give\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\int c_j(\\lambda)I(\\gamma,L_{j,\\lambda})\\,d\\lambda\n=\nU(S(\\ell(h_*(a_g))))-U(S(\\ell(h_*(a_L)))).\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]\nA piecewise regular representative of the path proves the second equality by the fundamental theorem on graph edges, with no cycle or path-length error.</p><p>One-dimensional coarea on closed path portions uses \\(u_j\\), or \\(d=w_j-u_j+u_k\\) in circle boxes. Partial itineraries have slope \\(\\pm1\\), and unique ancestry prevents multiplicity. At essential boxes pay both parameters. Thus\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\int\\#(\\sigma\\cap L_{j,\\lambda})\\,d\\lambda\n\\le C\\int_\\sigma G\\,d\\mathcal H^1.\n\\]\nRestricted traces have Lipschitz extensions; at density times their derivatives are bounded by the point-to-base upper slopes. If the right side is finite, weighted signed sums are absolutely integrable. The positive potential difference proves the result. Proper perimeter projection makes the local stair-accuracy prescriptions consistent for remote features by using their invariant whole supports.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Some rectifiable path in \\(\\Sigma_R\\) joins \\(z_1,z_2\\) with cost at most \\( C_pR^{1-2/p}\\left(\\int_{\\Sigma_R}g^p\\,d\\mathcal H^2\\right)^{1/p}. \\) Fixed bi-Lipschitz chart changes alter only the constant.", "statement": "<p>**Spherical path estimate — original Lemma 4.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(p&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Sigma_R\\) is a sphere of radius \\(R\\), whole or intersected with a closed half-space containing its center.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(g\\ge0\\) is measurable with finite surface \\(L^p\\)-norm.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(z_1,z_2\\in\\Sigma_R\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Some rectifiable path in \\(\\Sigma_R\\) joins \\(z_1,z_2\\) with cost at most\n\\[\nC_pR^{1-2/p}\\left(\\int_{\\Sigma_R}g^p\\,d\\mathcal H^2\\right)^{1/p}.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nFixed bi-Lipschitz chart changes alter only the constant.</p>", "proof": "<p>Scale to \\(R=1\\). Choose a fixed cap above the equator parallel to the cutting plane. Shortest arcs from an endpoint above the equator stay in the hemisphere. For an endpoint below it, restrict cap points by nonnegative horizontal scalar product; this retains a fixed fraction and makes initial vertical velocity nonnegative. The geodesic equation keeps the arc above the starting height and in the prescribed half-space.</p><p>Average arc costs over the permitted cap fraction. Spherical polar integration gives kernel \\(C/\\sin\\theta\\). Its conjugate power is integrable since \\(p'=p/(p-1)&lt;2\\), including endpoint and antipodal singularities. Hölder bounds both endpoint-to-cap costs. Average the middle cap-to-cap cost in the same way. Some pair of cap points realizes the averaged bound. Rescale length and area.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Sufficiently fine net spacing and sufficiently small approximation and stair errors force every point of every good routed layer, under \\(q_*\\), within the prescribed accuracy of its standard physical support.", "statement": "<p>**Guard barrier — original Proposition 4.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Buffered charts, capacities \\(C_a\\), and positive local layer-support accuracies are fixed before guards and final resolution.</p><p class=\"li\">Guards have locally uniform positive reach: their boundary anchors lie on distinct full blocks with positive separation on compact label tests.</p><p class=\"li\">Pre-stacks avoid the chosen guards and satisfy (4.7).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Sufficiently fine net spacing and sufficiently small approximation and stair errors force every point of every good routed layer, under \\(q_*\\), within the prescribed accuracy of its standard physical support.</p>", "proof": "<p>If discrepancy exceeds \\(\\delta\\), follow the layer toward its exact boundary. Uniform continuity and chart margins give a connected discrepant stretch of diameter at least \\(a&gt;0\\), uniformly in the sampled layer. Pack at least \\(ca/h\\) disjoint chart balls of radius \\(2h\\) along it. A guard-net point lies within \\(h/2\\) of each center, with label separated from the layer support.</p><p>The layer and guard both meet every chart sphere of radius \\(R\\in[h,2h]\\): connected continuation and the uniform guard reach ensure escape. Lemma 14.4 bounds every connector cost below; Lemma 14.5 gives, for almost every \\(R\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\Sigma_R}G^p\\,d\\mathcal H^2\\ge cR^{2-p}.\n\\]\nIntegrate in radius and sum the disjoint balls:\n\\[\n\\int_{U_a}G^p\\ge ca\\,h^{2-p}.\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]\nBecause \\(p&gt;2\\), sufficiently small \\(h\\), chosen from the prior capacity, contradicts (4.7). Half-space spheres handle boundary charts.</p><p>Guard avoidance can be implemented without isotopies fixing guards. Exclude small parameter neighborhoods of their labels before sampling. Properness bounds relevant guards on each compact feature. Carrier levels outside enlarged gaps have positive spatial buffers; finer meshes, boundary motions, controlled placements, and cell-sized snapping stay inside them.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.7", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Good routed layers are compact disks or annuli of root type.", "statement": "<p>**Stable occupied intervals — original Lemma 4.10.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Barrier accuracies tend to zero at infinity so that fixed compact standard supports confine layers to compact source sets, and only finitely many roots can meet a compact source set.</p><p class=\"li\">Good routed parameters are as in Lemma 14.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Good routed layers are compact disks or annuli of root type. For each root, finitely many separated closed stability intervals capture at least \\((1-\\zeta)w_j\\), and their layers form compact locally finite bi-Lipschitz product stacks, preserving concurrent essential parameters and exact boundary data.</p>", "proof": "<p>Confinement and goodness make the reached tree finite. Attaching its punctured disk pieces fills the root's holes, giving the original type. All itinerary inequalities are strict and finite, so they persist on an open parameter interval. Good parameters have full measure; choose finitely many separated closed subintervals capturing the required length.</p><p>Use original pattern trivializations with affine parameter transports. Parametrize diagonal segments by fractional position. Match their circle parametrizations by lifts of orientation-preserving circle maps, interpolated across the bands. Compact stability intervals give finite bi-Lipschitz bounds. Essential root boxes retain the concurrent parameter. The changed-coordinate PL contract applies to endpoint arrangements.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The exterior derivative of \\(P_0Th\\) depends only on pre-stack parameter differentials, not on \\(Dh\\) or itinerary length.", "statement": "<p>**Target stairs, parameter costs, and strict compositions — original Propositions 4.12 and 4.13.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\) is supplied by Proposition 14.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(S_D,S_e\\) are occupied radial and perimeter stairs.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(r_{u,D}\\ll a_D\\ll1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H_a\\) is nondecreasing, uniformly Lipschitz, zero on \\(u\\le a\\), equal to \\(u\\) on \\(u\\ge3a\\), and \\(0\\le H_a(u)\\le u\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On a face sector define\n\\[\nP_0(d_D+r(\\xi_e(s)-d_D))\n=d_D+\\rho_D(r)(\\xi_e(S_e(s))-d_D),\n\\tag{4.13}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho_D(r)=H_{a_D}\\!\\left(e^{S_D(L_D\\log r)/L_D}\\right),\n\\tag{4.14}\n\\]\nwith \\(\\rho_D=0\\) farther inward.</p><p class=\"li\">Extend to a full convex cell from an interior point \\(z_i\\) by\n\\[\nP_0(z_i+b(y-z_i))\n=z_i+b(P_{\\lambda(b)}(y)-z_i),\n\\tag{4.16}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\lambda=0\\) away from a thin boundary layer, \\(\\lambda(1)=1\\), and the boundary factors interpolate separately from identity to the displayed factors.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exterior derivative of \\(P_0Th\\) depends only on pre-stack parameter differentials, not on \\(Dh\\) or itinerary length. It admits locally bi-Lipschitz homeomorphic approximations \\(P^\\epsilon T_\\eta h\\) onto \\(\\Lambda\\), with arbitrarily small prescribed local \\(W^{1,p}\\) and value differences, summable on a compact exhaustion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 15.1 identifies every active derivative and removes all derivatives of complementary chamber fillings from the estimate. Lemma 15.2 supplies strict self-homeomorphisms and the required tangentially compatible strong limits. Prescribe summable compact errors; regions later removed by source compression require only finite local strict derivatives.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On an occupied unchanged pre-stack piece the active root differential is \\(\\pm du_j\\).", "statement": "<p>**Parameter costs — original Proposition 4.12.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The setting of Proposition 15 holds.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On an occupied unchanged pre-stack piece the active root differential is \\(\\pm du_j\\). On a routed circle box it is\n\\[\n\\pm d(w_j-u_j+u_k)=\\pm(-du_j+du_k),\n\\tag{4.17}\n\\]\nwith coordinate reversals understood, and only one root scalar is active. Essential boxes retain both original scalars independently. Moreover\n\\[\n\\rho_D(r)\\le Cr,\\qquad |\\rho_D'(r)|\\le C/c_*,\n\\tag{4.15}\n\\]\nand differentiation with respect to an occupied root parameter costs \\(O(r)\\), with log-radius/perimeter speed at most \\(1+O(\\zeta)\\) above activation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every itinerary is a composition of translations and reflections, so its derivative is \\(\\pm1\\). A diagonal segment has one reachable ancestry. Essential boxes were not switched. Exact realization uses the original affine \\(p_j\\); the subsequent stair has bounded speed against the root parameter and is constant in inactive directions. Apply the chain rule in piecewise product coordinates.</p><p>Small stair motion relative to \\(L_D\\) gives (4.15). Forward polar derivatives are \\(O(r)\\). In full cells, first choose layer thickness using fixed tangential bounds and absolute continuity on the prescribed exact block maps, then make factor motions small relative to it. The normal derivative and added energy are summably small; outside those layers \\(P_0Th=q_0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Replace radial and perimeter factors by \\( (1-\\epsilon_D)\\rho_D(r)+\\epsilon_Dr,\\qquad (1-\\epsilon_e)S_e(s)+\\epsilon_es. \\) For sufficiently small positive local feature constants these define a locally bi-Lipschitz …", "statement": "<p>**Strict target compositions — original Proposition 4.13.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(h,P_0\\) and fixed feature geometry are as above.</p><p class=\"li\">Stairs and cutoffs are piecewise smooth, finitely piecewise on compacts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Replace radial and perimeter factors by\n\\[\n(1-\\epsilon_D)\\rho_D(r)+\\epsilon_Dr,\\qquad\n(1-\\epsilon_e)S_e(s)+\\epsilon_es.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small positive local feature constants these define a locally bi-Lipschitz self-homeomorphism \\(P^\\epsilon\\), and\n\\[\nP^\\epsilon Th\\to P_0Th\\quad\\text{in }W^{1,p}_{\\mathrm{loc}}.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(P^\\epsilon,h\\),\n\\[\nP^\\epsilon T_\\eta h\\to P^\\epsilon Th\n\\quad\\text{in }W^{1,p}_{\\mathrm{loc}},\n\\]\nwith arbitrary local error prescriptions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Each strict scalar factor has positive lower slope on compact features. Polar coordinates, centers, and conical cell coordinates give both local Lipschitz directions and bijectivity. Piecewise derivative differences tend to zero as feature parameters decrease.</p><p>For the quantizer limit, use Proposition 10's dimensional absolute continuity: inverse images of sector cuts and stair knots are null within generic strata. At face points, full-cell tangential derivatives approach the boundary transformation. At edge points, edge-tangent differentiation keeps \\(r\\) fixed; the sector and conical derivatives give the same common subedge derivative. At vertex levels \\(D(Th)=0\\) almost everywhere. Therefore\n\\[\nD(P^\\epsilon T_\\eta h)\\to D(P^\\epsilon Th)\n\\]\nalmost everywhere locally. Fixed piecewise bounds and the restrictions on \\(\\eta,D\\eta\\) give an integrable local majorant. Dominated convergence and a proper locally finite diagonal selection prove the result.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a locally finite disjoint normal family \\(S_j'\\), of the same individual types and coorientations and with exact boundary traces, consisting of normal triangles and quadrilaterals, such that …", "statement": "<p>**Fine meshes and normalization with individual edge bounds — original Proposition 5.6, with Lemmas 5.1–5.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is an orientable irreducible PL three-manifold with a locally finite triangulation.</p><p class=\"li\">Physical coordinates may be locally bi-Lipschitz images of working PL coordinates.</p><p class=\"li\">\\((S_j)\\) is a locally finite disjoint family of compact proper PL disks or annuli; each annular core is noncontractible.</p><p class=\"li\">Surfaces are in general position, have straight normal boundary arcs, and\n\\[\n\\#(S_j\\cap e)\\le1\n\\]\nfor every boundary edge \\(e\\) and wall \\(j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a locally finite disjoint normal family \\(S_j'\\), of the same individual types and coorientations and with exact boundary traces, consisting of normal triangles and quadrilaterals, such that\n\\[\n\\#(S_j'\\cap e)\\le\\#(S_j\\cap e)\n\\]\nfor every individual wall and mesh edge. For finite families the replacement is an ambient isotopy relative to the boundary. The construction concerns one color and need not preserve the other.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a finite family, choose an admissible boundary-relative isotopic position minimizing total edge hits, then face-intersection circles, while retaining every initial individual edge bound.</p><p>An innermost face circle bounds a face disk disjoint from all walls and, by incompressibility, a disk on its wall. Their sphere bounds a ball. Other walls and the connected remaining part of the same wall reach the actual boundary, so lie outside. Moving the disk across the ball removes weight or face circles, contradiction.</p><p>A face arc returning to the same edge has an innermost empty bigon. Its edge side has no further hit, and cannot be a boundary edge by the per-wall bound. The bigon isotopy removes two hits without affecting other edges.</p><p>If a tetrahedron piece is not a disk, cut along all pieces. Failure of a boundary-copy fundamental-group injection gives a compression disk by the Loop Theorem. If all injections held, graph-of-spaces normal form would inject the nontrivial fundamental group of a nondisk piece into the tetrahedron's trivial group, impossible. Thus a compression disk exists with interior missing all pieces. Its boundary bounds a disk on the whole incompressible wall; that disk leaves the tetrahedron piece and contains a whole boundary polygon, hence an interior edge hit. Irreducibility replaces it by the compression disk, lowering weight.</p><p>Disjoint proper disks cut the tetrahedron into balls with dual tree. If a disk meets an edge twice, its edge traversal has an immediate backtrack. An empty bigon in the intervening complementary ball removes two hits. Thus every disk meets each edge at most once. Its normal boundary separates the four vertices into \\(1+3\\) or \\(2+2\\), giving a normal triangle or quadrilateral. Straightening and standard disk fillings preserve the family by boundary-relative ball homeomorphisms.</p><p>For infinite families, normalize finite subfamilies and extract stabilized finite normal data wall by wall, including labels, coorientations, matching, and edge order. Each wall can occupy only finitely many tetrahedra incident to its original hit edges; on compacts only finitely many candidate walls occur. Thus stabilized data realize a locally finite disjoint normal family with unchanged topology and boundary placements. No infinite ambient isotopy limit is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Arbitrarily fine conforming locally finite triangulations have bounded boundary shapes and incidences and fixed local volume bounds.", "statement": "<p>**Fine meshes with lattice cores — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A working polyhedral structure and boundary product collar are fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Positive local mesh-size bounds and finitely many prescribed lattice patches with fixed bases \\(A\\) are given.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Arbitrarily fine conforming locally finite triangulations have bounded boundary shapes and incidences and fixed local volume bounds. They can have constant background step \\(H\\) on inset cores and exact Kuhn meshes in the prescribed bases. Transition constants depend on fixed bases and coarse charts, not \\(H\\) or later guards. Most smooth chart interiors can have universal physical shapes, with remaining transitions confined to predetermined arbitrarily small weighted-error sets.</p><p>The statement persists after pushing the boundary to depth \\(d_0(x)&gt;0\\), with constants independent of the smallness of \\(d_0\\), provided its absolute square-coordinate slope is bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use globally ordered coarse vertices and dyadic Kuhn subdivisions. A positive sufficiently small-slope size minorant makes neighboring stopping levels differ by a bounded amount. Overlay nested face subdivisions and cone; finite normalized configurations give shapes and incidence bounds. Constant cores follow by Lipschitz infimum envelopes of preferred sizes plus path distance.</p><p>For lattice insertion, separate pure lattice regions by a buffer wider than the covering radius and fill with a separated net with fixed jitter. In a metric where both lattices are orthogonal, Delaunay locality ensures candidate cells contain fixed sites from at most one lattice. Place free sites successively avoiding small neighborhoods of affine spans of at most three earlier nearby sites. Bounded candidate counts and discrete determinant gaps yield a positive mesh-unit lower volume for nondegenerate candidate tetrahedra. Resolve nonsimplicial cells by compatible pulling orders, recovering Kuhn boxes deep inside pure regions.</p><p>For arbitrary \\(A=U\\Sigma V^T\\), couple the identity lattice to \\(U\\Sigma\\) in Euclidean metric, then \\(U\\Sigma\\) to \\(A\\) in metric \\((U\\Sigma)^{-T}(U\\Sigma)^{-1}\\), with a pure intermediate band. On small smooth chart patches insert inverse derivative bases to obtain universal physical shapes. Fix matrix bounds before shrinking weighted transition margins, then take \\(H\\) small.</p><p>Push the boundary in collar coordinates by a monotone PL depth change equal to identity past \\(Cd_0\\), with uniformly bounded positive depth slopes and cross slopes controlled by \\(|\\nabla d_0|\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Central boundary traces become single straight normal arcs in boundary triangles, with at most one hit per individual edge.", "statement": "<p>**Boundary trace preparation — original Lemma 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Boundary squares have normalized product coordinates whose shared varying coordinates agree affinely, possibly reversed.</p><p class=\"li\">The cut depth lies in exact \\(h_*\\)-data, and mesh size is much smaller than \\(d_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Central boundary traces become single straight normal arcs in boundary triangles, with at most one hit per individual edge. Sample-neighborhood label intervals have disjoint same-color PL ribbon continuations. The two-color outer collar is jointly locally bi-Lipschitz equivalent to the cut pattern times depth, preserving both parameters. Inverse-label and edge-coarea bounds are independent of \\(d_0\\), excluded-belt widths, and final scales. A label interval comparable to \\(c_*w_j\\) has fractional crossed-edge width at least\n\\[\n\\frac{c\\epsilon c_*w_j}{\\ell H_0},\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is feature length and \\(H_0\\) the dyadic square scale.</p>", "proof": "<p>On dyadic bins of length \\(H_0=2^{-k}\\), use increasing reversal-symmetric maps \\(\\phi_k\\), slope \\(\\epsilon\\) outside narrow excluded endpoint belts, fixing bin endpoints. Blend adjacent scales implicitly:\n\\[\nx_i=(1-\\chi(x))\\phi_k(z_i)+\\chi(x)\\phi_{k+1}(z_i),\n\\quad\n\\chi(x)=2(1-\\delta(x)/H_0).\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nSince \\(|\\phi_k-\\phi_{k+1}|\\le CH_0\\), sufficiently small \\(\\operatorname{Lip}\\delta\\) makes this a contraction in \\(x\\). Scalar monotonicity gives a unique inverse with bounds \\(C/\\epsilon\\), independent of belt widths. Partial turn-on retains the minimum response. Using normalized depth \\(d/d_0(x)\\), the added inverse-response terms are bounded by \\(C(\\delta/d_0)(1+|\\nabla d_0|)\\).</p><p>Allowed trace positions lie near bounded reference lists per star. Jitter mesh vertices, freely inside squares and along seams, to separate vertices from axis references, make non-seam edges coordinate-oblique, and keep edges away from pair-reference points. Bad-event probabilities tend uniformly to zero with the required slack, with bounded dependency degree. The Lovász local lemma on finite exhaustions and compact-product passage gives simultaneous placements. Fix this slack before \\(\\epsilon,\\operatorname{Lip}\\delta\\).</p><p>Each allowed trace is a graph arc with endpoints away from vertices. Replace it by its chord at fixed running coordinate. Endpoint velocity cones give a positive normal response \\(c'\\epsilon\\), preserved by convex interpolation and compatible across edges. Ordered stair triangulations between close endpoint placements give interval ribbons; positive determinant response and embedded boundaries show they fill precisely their shells. Equal subdivision changes only displacement differences.</p><p>Run these homotopies from true boundary to the cut, mirror the central homotopy inside, and stretch depth back to the original exterior with fixed slope bounds. Central sampled chords stay fixed during interval adaptation. Fill parameter gaps linearly at fixed running coordinate, then solve the two small-cross-slope graph equations by contraction. This gives one common product identification preserving both labels. Conversion from normal response to edge fraction proves (5.1).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Away from constrained collars, shape-preserving ambient jitter gives Sobolev ACL edges with chain rule.", "statement": "<p>**Generic ACL edges and conditional count charging — original Lemma 5.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the meshes and boundary preparation above.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(W_e\\) is the sum of sampled root weights over central hits of both colors on edge \\(e\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Away from constrained collars, shape-preserving ambient jitter gives Sobolev ACL edges with chain rule. Subsequent samples can satisfy\n\\[\nW_e\\le(1+C(\\zeta+c_*))\\operatorname{Var}_e(q_*)+\\epsilon_e,\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nwith separate-color and smooth-sector scalar versions and arbitrarily small errors. Modified boundary portions use Lemma 16.2's fixed inverse-label bounds. Expected ambient charges for powers of edge-variation means have bounded overlap and fixed local density factors, independent of resolution.</p>", "proof": "<p>Interpolate small independent physical vertex vectors in working simplex coordinates, tapering to zero in exact collar data. Fix local fractions from chart and incidence bounds; use universal fractions in physical bulk and arbitrarily small fixed fractions on sharp patches. Mesh size is much smaller than taper depth. These ambient maps are locally bi-Lipschitz and globally homeomorphic by fine proper motion and degree.</p><p>At each unconstrained edge time, one endpoint coefficient is bounded below, so the random point has density at most \\(Ch^{-3}\\) in a neighboring star and normalized speed \\(O(h)\\). Fubini via smooth Sobolev approximations gives ACL, chain rule, and expected edge integrals. Bounded star overlap gives ambient charging.</p><p>Coarea makes hit-count functions integrable. Apply Lemma 14.1 after fixing edges. Generic samples avoid vertices, chart breaks, tangencies, and corner labels and have finitely many hits. Generic seam-time sets are null; ACL sends them to null scalar-label sets. On high-radius sectors graph speed is bounded by aspect factor times \\(|Dq_0|/r\\); modified collars use the fixed inverse bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The family can be made PL, of the same individual types, retaining smaller boundary collars, without increasing any individual surface–edge count.", "statement": "<p>**Relative PL preparation with edge counts — original Lemma 5.4 and Remark 5.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A locally finite disjoint family of compact proper locally flat surfaces avoids mesh vertices and has finitely many isolated edge hits.</p><p class=\"li\">Near the manifold boundary it already has prescribed PL product collars, transverse crossings, and straight triangle traces.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The family can be made PL, of the same individual types, retaining smaller boundary collars, without increasing any individual surface–edge count. Surviving interior hits can have flat transverse disk germs. Changes can be confined to any prescribed neighborhood and fine position tolerance. No Lipschitz bound on these preparatory changes is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>At an isolated hit choose a flattening surface chart and a short polygonal cylinder around the straight edge, with caps disjoint from the surface. Prepare the surface PL and transverse near the cylinder side, leaving the axis unchanged. Remove innermost side loops null-homotopic in the side annulus by replacing surface subdisks with small side-disk pushoffs. Remaining essential loops have linking number \\(\\pm1\\) and their bounded surface disks contain the unique possible axis hit; these disks are nested. Replace the outermost by an unknotted disk with the same rim and one flat transverse axis hit. If no side loop remains, the cylinder is missed. Relative disk extension from locally flat Schoenflies realizes the change ambiently in a small chart ball. Supports are disjoint and locally finite.</p><p>Retain smaller crossing balls and boundary collars. Elsewhere the surfaces have compact-local positive gaps from edges. Cut along the collared surfaces, triangulate both copies compatibly, and use Hamilton's relative triangulation construction to extend over the cut manifolds. Glue to obtain an auxiliary PL structure making the surfaces PL and agreeing on protected neighborhoods. Relative PL approximation of the identity between structures, below the edge gaps, transfers the surfaces to the original structure without new hits. Work in disjoint locally finite regular neighborhoods and glue. Uniform value approximation alone would not have controlled the edge counts; the protected germs and gaps do.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Normal disks thicken into pre-stacks satisfying the complete contract of Proposition 14.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform weak pre-stack templates — original Lemma 5.10.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Both colors have been normalized separately by Proposition 16.</p><p class=\"li\">Boundary data are those of Lemma 16.2.</p><p class=\"li\">In a universally shape-controlled physical tetrahedron \\(\\sigma\\), \\(h_\\sigma\\) is its size and \\(W_e\\) the total weighted central hit count on edge \\(e\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Normal disks thicken into pre-stacks satisfying the complete contract of Proposition 14. Away from special boundary widths,\n\\[\nG|_\\sigma\\le Ch_\\sigma^{-1}\\max_{e\\subset\\sigma}W_e.\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nFixed local chart and boundary terms apply on preselected error regions. No quantitative general-position constant depending on the number of actual sheets is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>On each interior edge cluster each color near its own separated mid-edge reference, preserving order, and allocate each hit width at least \\(cw_j/W_e\\). Boundary widths remain the prescribed ones; Lemmas 16.2 and 17.1 give fixed terms \\(C\\ell/(\\epsilon c_*)\\), with no inverse belt or collar thickness.</p><p>For each finite reference configuration, move one color by a smooth diffeomorphism fixed near the tetrahedron boundary to make main triangles transverse, creases transverse to opponent triangles, and opposite creases disjoint. Approximate it by a PL homeomorphism with derivatives sufficiently close to preserve all finite margins. Reference data range in compact sets with fixed endpoint and color slack; a finite subcover gives finitely many motions, uniform complexity and bi-Lipschitz bounds. Narrow actual clusters to fit those tolerances.</p><p>At smooth crossings use a transverse intrinsic direction. At a main crease use its direction; conversely choose an opponent-plane direction crossing both facets with the same sign. At tetrahedron faces use the transverse boundary-arc direction, with the same sign from both sides. These cones persist for prism pieces and finite motions. Extend parameter formulas slightly with strict orders and margins, so no extra lateral frontier occurs. Lemma 17.2 gives full product intersections and pattern trivializations. Lemma 16.2 attaches the joint boundary collar. Lemma 17.1 and proportional widths prove (5.6). All parameter pieces are PL in common working coordinates followed by common locally bi-Lipschitz changes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Compatible same-color edge orders give disjoint templates, separated by at least \\(c(\\gamma)\\) times minimum edge gap.", "statement": "<p>**Normal templates and affine shell prisms — original Lemma 5.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in a normalized tetrahedron.</p><p class=\"li\">Crossing fractions lie in \\([\\gamma,1-\\gamma]\\), \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use a normal triangle or two main triangles on a fixed diagonal for a quadrilateral.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(u\\) has range of length at most \\(w\\), and \\(\\Delta_e\\) is the fractional width on crossed edge \\(e\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Compatible same-color edge orders give disjoint templates, separated by at least \\(c(\\gamma)\\) times minimum edge gap. Ordered affine stair prisms map homeomorphically onto the whole shell, and\n\\[\n|\\nabla u|\\le C(\\gamma)\\max_{e\\text{ crossed}}\\frac w{\\Delta_e}.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nFixed physical shape and scale factors apply after coordinate changes.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a quadrilateral partition \\(I|J\\), write barycentric coordinates as \\(\\lambda=((1-u)a,ub)\\), \\(a_0+a_1=b_0+b_1=1\\), and \\(r_{ij}=t_{ij}/(1-t_{ij})\\). On the main triangle \\(00,01,11\\),\n\\[\n\\frac u{1-u}=\n\\frac{r_{01}a_0+r_{11}a_1}{(r_{01}/r_{00})b_0+b_1},\n\\qquad r_{11}a_1b_0\\le r_{00}a_0b_1.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nThe other half is symmetric. The expression is monotone in edge positions, and common multiplication of odds multiplies the graph odds. Compact endpoint margins give positive common-increment response and bounded graph slopes. Different compatible types include a triangle; face order puts the other disk's convex hull on its specified side, with quantitative gap.</p><p>Triangulate triangle times interval by a consistent stair order. A stair tetrahedron has one duplicated crossing-edge vertex. Its separating-plane altitude has the correct sign and is bounded below by \\(c(\\gamma)\\) times that edge displacement; the displayed quadrilateral half has plane coefficients proportional to\n\\((-r_{01},-r_{11},r_{01}/r_{00},1)\\). Boundary ribbons are embedded and ordered. Positive simplex signs and degree one of the embedded sphere boundary give a homeomorphism onto the shell, excluding branching. Matrix inversion and altitude yield (5.4). Fine tangential comparisons use displacement differences, not common translations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Subdivision by \\(a_k\\le f_k\\le b_k\\) is jointly bi-Lipschitz trivial over \\(J\\), preserving \\(f_k\\) on strips and the boundary.", "statement": "<p>**Sufficient simultaneous pattern-triviality test — original Lemma 5.8 and Remark 5.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Sigma\\) is a compact smooth disk or annulus, \\(J\\) a compact interval, and \\(E:\\Sigma\\times J\\to C\\) bi-Lipschitz.</p><p class=\"li\">Finitely many Lipschitz \\(f_k(z,\\lambda)\\) are defined near disjoint compact enlarged strips\n\\[\nK_k=\\{a_k-\\delta_k\\le f_k\\le b_k+\\delta_k\\},\\quad a_k&lt;b_k,\\quad\\delta_k&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Strips have interior domain margins and no additional lateral frontier except on \\(\\partial\\Sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Near each point there is a smooth surface field \\(V\\), tangent to \\(\\partial\\Sigma\\), with\n\\[\nD_zf_k(z,\\lambda)V(z)\\ge c&gt;0\n\\]\nalmost everywhere, equivalently strict integrated increase on every nearby flow line.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Subdivision by \\(a_k\\le f_k\\le b_k\\) is jointly bi-Lipschitz trivial over \\(J\\), preserving \\(f_k\\) on strips and the boundary. At fixed \\(\\lambda\\), each connected strip is a bi-Lipschitz product over \\(f_k\\); prescribed arc or circle level fibres therefore give full two-parameter product boxes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\lambda_0\\). Compactness and a partition of unity combine admissible directions into fields \\(V_k\\), with disjoint supports and a common positive lower slope near \\(\\lambda_0\\). Differentiation along the combined field has no derivatives of partition coefficients. Flow-box ACL and continuity extend the integrated slope inequalities to every flow line and parameter.</p><p>Strict monotonicity gives unique small jointly Lipschitz times \\(t_k(z,\\lambda)\\) solving\n\\[\nf_k(\\Phi_k^{t_k(z,\\lambda)}z,\\lambda)=f_k(z,\\lambda_0).\n\\]\nCut these times off in the extra label margins. In flow coordinates, the uncut reparametrization \\(h_\\lambda\\) has lower slope \\(c_k/M_k\\). The cut version\n\\[\ns+\\chi_k(F_{\\lambda_0}(y,s))(h_\\lambda(y,s)-s)\n\\]\nhas derivative at least\n\\[\n\\min\\{1,c_k/M_k\\}\n-\\operatorname{Lip}(\\chi_k\\circ F_{\\lambda_0})\\|h_\\lambda-s\\|_\\infty&gt;0\n\\]\nfor close \\(\\lambda\\). Thus it is a jointly bi-Lipschitz increasing orbit reparametrization, onto also on periodic orbits, and identity off its support. Extra margins ensure full strip correspondence. Compose finitely many parameter-neighborhood transports to cover \\(J\\).</p><p>At fixed \\(\\lambda\\), positive flow speed gives unique hitting times from \\(f_k=a_k\\) to every intermediate value, Lipschitz in data. Flow boxes identify the level set as a compact Lipschitz one-manifold and yield the strip product. At creases, a direction with the same positive one-sided signs gives the same intrinsic integrated test. Constants are qualitative and do not supply later integral bounds or common ambient PL coordinates.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 18", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The islands admit pre-stacks satisfying Proposition 14's contract, with:", "statement": "<p>**Sharp-island geometric contract — original Proposition 6.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work on finitely many protected aligned source lattice patches, strictly inside the exterior and away from boundary collars.</p><p class=\"li\">Coarse charts, basis bounds, slot loss \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), fixed template choices, and positive nested patch margins are fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">A common background Kuhn step is \\(H\\), and all relevant features in enlarged equal-weight islands use root weight \\(W\\ll H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The ideal scalar-covector modes are\n\\[\n(e_1^T,e_2^T)\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n(b_1e_1^T,b_2e_1^T),\\qquad |b_1|+|b_2|=1.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">An inter-edge increases the indicated axis by \\(H\\); a level edge keeps it constant.</p><p class=\"li\">An eligible edge has upper counts\n\\[\nN_{e,i}\\le(B_i+\\delta')H/W\\text{ on inter-edges},\\qquad\nN_{e,i}\\le\\delta'H/W\\text{ on level edges},\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(B_i=1\\) independently and \\(B_i=|b_i|\\) in parallel mode.</p><p class=\"li\">A regular tetrahedron has all required edges eligible.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The islands admit pre-stacks satisfying Proposition 14's contract, with:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Upper pre-slope \\(G\\le CM_\\sigma\\) in aligned units on enlarged equal-weight islands, independent of microscopic widths, crossing angles, later guards, \\(H,W\\), and final \\(\\delta'\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. On deep regular low-leakage portions, scalar gradients satisfy the nearly unit-axis estimate (6.6) below. Concurrent independent scalars and switched diagonal signed sums have squared cost at most \\(2+C\\eta\\); parallel bands are disjoint and cost at most \\(1+C\\eta\\). Occupied stairs multiply by at most \\((1+C\\zeta)^2\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Excluded fixed-\\(NW\\) neighborhoods of tetrahedron faces, main slab creases, and nonslabs occupy relative volume at most\n\\[\nC_N(W/H+\\delta').\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nNonregular, high-leakage, fixed-neighbor, and patch-margin portions are charged by their full \\(M^p\\)-cost.</p><p>These are scalar-pair estimates in compatible smooth sectors after label pinning, away from activation cutoffs.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 18.1–18.6 construct masters, axial portions, canonical control, and broadening while preserving the complete two-color parameter pattern. The canonical complexity estimate applies exactly where displacements are \\(O(s_\\sigma)\\); larger ordinary weak widths return only through quantitative clustered cells. Hence density is \\(CM_\\sigma\\) throughout.</p><p>Independent near-axis covectors have squared combined norm at most \\(2+C\\eta\\). A routed diagonal is one signed sum of those covectors and has the same bound; no itinerary factor occurs. Parallel protected bands are disjoint and cost at most \\(1+C\\eta\\). Lemma 14.1 gives the stair factor.</p><p>There are \\(O(H/W)\\) slab creases of length \\(O(H)\\), so fixed-\\(NW\\) tubes cost \\(C_NH^2W\\). There are \\(C\\delta'H/W\\) nonslabs, each area \\(O(H^2)\\); their thickening costs \\(C_N\\delta'H^3\\). Face buffers cost \\(C_NH^2W\\). Divide by tetrahedron volume \\(O(H^3)\\) to obtain (6.9). Auxiliary trimming and flow margins enlarge only the fixed factor. Other portions are paid by full majorant cost.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Hits on eligible inter-edges fit consecutive slots of spacing at least \\((1-C\\eta)W\\), starting at a common \\(O(\\eta H)\\) offset, with fractions in \\([\\gamma(\\eta),1-\\gamma(\\eta)]\\).", "statement": "<p>**Slots, slab slopes, and gaps — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The aligned modes and eligible counts of Proposition 18 hold.</p><p class=\"li\">Count tolerances are sufficiently small relative to \\(\\eta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Hits on eligible inter-edges fit consecutive slots of spacing at least \\((1-C\\eta)W\\), starting at a common \\(O(\\eta H)\\) offset, with fractions in \\([\\gamma(\\eta),1-\\gamma(\\eta)]\\). In a regular tetrahedron all but \\(C\\delta'H/W\\) disks are coordinate-layer slabs. Their main triangles have slopes \\(O_\\eta(\\delta')\\), and successive slabs have axial gaps at least \\((1-C\\eta)W\\). In parallel mode this holds also between the two consecutive separate color bands. Arbitrarily small generic slot perturbations are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Allocate separate lists in independent mode and one first-color-then-second-color list in parallel mode. The total parallel count is at most \\((1+2\\delta')H/W\\). Shorten spacing by a sufficiently large multiple of \\(\\eta W\\) to leave endpoint margins. Noneligible edges use margin-spaced placements and outer buffers use weak clustered placements.</p><p>A nonslab hits a level edge, so there are at most \\(C\\delta'H/W\\). Each slab hits every inter-edge once, and nested slab orders agree. Only nonslabs alter complete edge ranks, giving \\(O(\\delta')\\) differences of fractions, also for the preceding parallel band. Formula (5.5) gives near-horizontal main triangles.</p><p>For the gap, move all lower fractions by an increment \\(\\Delta\\asymp W/H\\) below the least edge gap. Odds multipliers equal\n\\[\n1+\\frac{\\Delta}{t_*(1-t_*)}+O_\\eta(\\Delta(\\delta'+\\Delta)).\n\\]\nMonotonicity and common-multiplier response give nearly unit height increase. Convert to a common transverse foot: probability coordinates change by \\(O_\\eta(W/H)\\), and the near-horizontal slope loses only \\(O_\\eta(\\delta'W/H)\\). Choose tolerances, \\(W/H\\), and generic motions below unused slack.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(M\\ge m\\); neighbor ratios are at most \\(\\theta^{-1}\\); for \\(p\\ge1\\), \\( \\sum_\\sigma H^3M_\\sigma^p\\le C_{p,D,\\theta}\\sum_\\sigma H^3m_\\sigma^p. \\) If \\(m_\\sigma\\le B+e_\\sigma\\), the full \\(M^p\\)-cost above a …", "statement": "<p>**Neighboring majorant and auxiliary mesh — original Lemmas 6.2 and 6.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The finite enlarged lattice has star-neighbor degree at most \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(N_e\\) is the initial total upper hit count.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(0&lt;\\theta&lt;1/(2D)\\), and\n\\[\nm_\\sigma=1+\\frac WH\\max_{e\\subset\\sigma}N_e,\\qquad\nM_\\sigma=\\sum_\\tau\\theta^{\\operatorname{dist}_{\\mathrm{star}}(\\sigma,\\tau)}m_\\tau,\n\\qquad s_\\sigma=W/M_\\sigma.\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(M\\ge m\\); neighbor ratios are at most \\(\\theta^{-1}\\); for \\(p\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_\\sigma H^3M_\\sigma^p\\le C_{p,D,\\theta}\\sum_\\sigma H^3m_\\sigma^p.\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nIf \\(m_\\sigma\\le B+e_\\sigma\\), the full \\(M^p\\)-cost above a sufficiently large fixed threshold, including any fixed number of neighbor layers, is controlled by \\(\\sum H^3e_\\sigma^p\\). An inset region with boundary deep in the quantitative clustered belt has a conforming auxiliary triangulation with sizes comparable to \\(s_\\sigma\\), bounded shapes and stars, and maximum size \\(CW\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Neighbor distances differ by at most one. Row and column sums of \\(\\theta^{\\operatorname{dist}}\\) are bounded by \\(\\sum_{k\\ge0}D^k\\theta^k\\). Jensen followed by summation proves (6.4). Apply the same estimate to \\(e\\); above twice the baseline convolution plus one, the error convolution dominates \\(M\\). Neighbor comparability extends costs to fixed layers.</p><p>Refine original tetrahedra dyadically to \\(s_\\sigma\\). Neighbor level differences are bounded by the fixed ratio. Overlay nested face subdivisions and cone as in Lemma 16.1, producing a finite shape list.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are compact master pre-stacks satisfying the full qualitative contract.", "statement": "<p>**Master systems and axial translations — original Lemmas 6.4 and 6.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Slot placements continue to weak clustered placements.</p><p class=\"li\">Quantitative equal-weight belts separate microscopic noncluster widths from the snapping-patch boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">Low leakage means \\(M_\\sigma\\le C_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are compact master pre-stacks satisfying the full qualitative contract. Noncluster widths may be microscopic; fully clustered equal-weight widths are comparable to \\(s_\\sigma\\). Common dyadic subdivision of main triangles by factor comparable to \\(H/W\\) causes no corresponding tangential or inverse-parameter loss.</p><p>On regular low-leakage portions, a fixed large \\(W\\)-multiple away from faces, creases, nonslabs, nonregular tetrahedra, and placement transitions, strips can become exact axial translations of supporting main triangles, with half-width \\(a_0=c_0W\\). Their coordinate is the affine conversion of signed axial height \\(l_P/a_0\\in[-1,1]\\) to \\([0,W]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Make noncluster central diagrams qualitatively generic by arbitrarily small edge-position variations: plane coincidence and crease containment impose nontrivial equalities, and opponent-crease intersections prescribe a one-dimensional null set in two endpoint parameters. Fully clustered tetrahedra use Proposition 17's robust finite motions.</p><p>Choose endpoint displacements qualitatively tiny in nonrobust diagrams and \\(c\\min_{\\sigma\\supset e}s_\\sigma\\) in the quantitative clustered belt. Central gaps are at least \\(cs_\\sigma\\), from slot gaps or \\(H/(1+N_e)\\). Mixed-width faces retain the common crossing direction and strict cones.</p><p>Subdivide each main triangle by one common factor comparable to \\(H/W\\), interpolating old endpoint displacement vectors affinely. At fixed layer parameter, tangential columns contain differences of those displacements, so common translation cancels and no subdivision factor enters. The vertical column is a convex combination of consistently oriented crossing-edge motions, with definite separating-normal component relative to its size. Positive prism degree and Lemma 17.2 preserve the master product structure.</p><p>In safe regular low-leakage regions replace endpoint motions by \\(\\pm a_0e_i\\). Since fine sides are comparable to \\(W\\), the switch adds only \\(C(\\eta)c_0\\) tangent error. Choose \\(c_0\\) small. Projection perpendicular to the axis remains injective; at a fixed projected foot the layer height increases strictly, even with microscopic unswitched motions. Slab gaps preserve same-axis disjointness; independent opponents retain independent normals. A continuous interpolation preserves the whole pattern. Fully translated prisms reproduce the exact axial-height formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On each auxiliary simplex, master collar domains and affine parameter pieces have a common consistent straight simplicial subdivision with at most \\(N\\) simplices.", "statement": "<p>**Bounded local complexity — original Lemma 6.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In every relevant original tetrahedron, same-family central disks are \\(cs_\\sigma\\)-separated and have boundedly many shape-controlled main triangles of size \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Main triangles are subdivided into size-\\(W\\) fine triangles with fixed stair rules; vertex motions are at most \\(As_\\sigma\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Subsequent template adjustments are PL homeomorphisms with fixed two-sided constant \\(L\\), affine on boundedly many convex pieces of bounded face complexity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On each auxiliary simplex, master collar domains and affine parameter pieces have a common consistent straight simplicial subdivision with at most \\(N\\) simplices. Domains are subcomplexes and parameters affine with values in \\([0,W]\\). \\(N\\) is independent of disk number and opposite crossing angles. The fixed subdivision simplices are nondegenerate, but no uniform shape bound is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>The inverse image of an auxiliary cell under a template adjustment has diameter \\(O(s_\\sigma)\\). Any prism meeting it has a central disk meeting an \\(O(s_\\sigma)\\)-ball. Disk separation bounds their number by volume packing, for both colors. Relevant fine triangles lie in an \\(O(W)\\) planar ball and each has area at least \\(cW^2\\); area packing bounds their number. Fixed prism and adjustment complexities give boundedly many affine pieces.</p><p>Central-disk separation follows from same-type normal graph order and, for different compatible types, the triangular separating plane. The edge gaps obey\n\\[\n\\frac H{1+n}\\ge\\frac W{1+nW/H}\\ge W/M_\\sigma=s_\\sigma.\n\\]\nTake all supporting planes of relevant pieces in each auxiliary cell. Their bounded arrangement gives convex polyhedra. Overlay restrictions on shared faces and edge cut points, triangulate lower dimensions first, and cone interiors. All incidences are bounded; relative-interior cone points give nondegenerate consistent simplices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a cell-preserving PL homeomorphism \\(J\\), from new coordinates to old coordinates, identity on \\(\\mathcal P\\), with uniform forward bound \\(\\operatorname{Lip}(J|_D)\\le C\\), such that:", "statement": "<p>**Canonical snapping with protected buffers — original Lemma 6.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal G\\) is the finite auxiliary triangulation.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal C,\\mathcal P\\) are disjoint canonical and protected subcomplexes.</p><p class=\"li\">Canonical cells have the subdivisions of Lemma 18.4.</p><p class=\"li\">On each other positive-dimensional cell \\(D\\), old master parameters have upper local slope at most \\(BW/h_D\\) on their closed domains.</p><p class=\"li\">Parameters are locally Lipschitz for the fixed configuration.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a cell-preserving PL homeomorphism \\(J\\), from new coordinates to old coordinates, identity on \\(\\mathcal P\\), with uniform forward bound \\(\\operatorname{Lip}(J|_D)\\le C\\), such that:</p><p class=\"li\">on canonical cells, \\(u_j\\circ J\\) has a \\(CW/h_D\\)-Lipschitz extension to \\(D\\);</p><p class=\"li\">on other cells, transported upper local slopes are at most \\(CBW/h_D\\).</p><p>Thus transported density is at most \\(C(1+B)M_\\sigma\\), with incident maxima. Constants do not depend on old subdivision altitudes or opposite-sheet angles. The inverse is locally Lipschitz only for the fixed input, without a uniform bound. If the outer boundary is protected, extend by the identity.</p>", "proof": "<p>A subdivision type records its abstract complex, ordered coarse vertices, and smallest coarse face containing each simplex. Bounded simplex count gives finitely many realizable marked types. Choose one nondegenerate straight representative on each standard simplex \\(\\Delta_d\\). Their finite inverse edge-matrix bounds are fixed.</p><p>Let \\(R_D:D\\to\\Delta_d\\) carry the old subdivision to its representative. Its inverse has\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Lip}(R_D^{-1})\\le Ch_D,\n\\]\nsince its affine derivatives are old edge matrices, bounded by \\(Ch_D\\), times fixed representative inverse matrices. No old inverse matrix enters.</p><p>For coning a boundary map \\(g:\\partial D_0\\to\\partial E_0\\), with centers and inner/outer radii \\(r,R,r',R'\\), the radial cone satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Lip}(C_g)\\le L_0(1+R/r)+R'/r.\n\\]\nThe radial gauge gives \\(|t-s|\\le|x-y|/r\\), and\n\\(t|z-w|\\le(1+R/r)|x-y|\\). The forward estimate requires no inverse bound. Compatible facetwise bounds give a boundary Euclidean bound depending only on coarse simplex shape, by paths through common facets using barycentric-coordinate estimates.</p><p>Construct canonical maps by dimension:\n\\[\nJ_D=R_D^{-1}K_D.\n\\]\nAt vertices use identity and on edges affine standardization. On a facet \\(F\\),\n\\[\nK_D|_F=(R_D|_F\\circ R_F^{-1})K_F.\n\\]\nThe parenthesized comparisons are from finite representative lists and have uniform two-sided bounds. They agree on smaller faces. Cone the compatible boundary map between barycenters; obtain\n\\(\\operatorname{Lip}K_D\\le C/h_D\\) and\n\\(\\operatorname{Lip}K_D^{-1}\\le Ch_D\\). Therefore \\(\\operatorname{Lip}J_D\\le C\\).</p><p>On the representative, extend a parameter from its subcomplex by assigning zero at other vertices and interpolating affinely. Vertex range \\([0,W]\\) and fixed representative shapes give Lipschitz bound \\(CW\\). Composition with \\(K_D\\) proves the canonical parameter estimate.</p><p>Set \\(J=\\operatorname{id}\\) on protected cells. Extend across remaining cells by dimension using barycentric coning of the already prescribed boundary maps. Only uniform forward bounds are needed. Since \\(J\\) preserves each cell,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{lip}(u_j\\circ J)\\le\n\\operatorname{Lip}(J)\\operatorname{lip}u_j\\circ J\n\\le CBW/h_D.\n\\]\nTake incident maxima at interfaces. Finite nondegenerate PL pieces give an input-dependent locally Lipschitz inverse.</p><p>Choose canonical cells to contain all uncontrolled microscopic widths, with faces. Protected cells lie strictly inside translated regions and at the outer quantitative belt; leave fixed auxiliary-star buffers of already controlled slope between them. Auxiliary size is \\(O(W)\\), so these trim operations enlarge only fixed-\\(W\\) buffers. This separation is essential to the finite representative comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 18.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A common ambient locally bi-Lipschitz change preserving every layer parameter broadens half-width to \\( a_1=(1-C\\eta)W/2, \\) with slack below half the slab gap, equal to \\(a_1\\) deep inside and \\(a_0\\) near protected …", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous broadening — original Lemma 6.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Snapping leaves deep protected axial translation regions unchanged.</p><p class=\"li\">Supporting-plane signed axial height is \\(l_P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(a_0=c_0W\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A common ambient locally bi-Lipschitz change preserving every layer parameter broadens half-width to\n\\[\na_1=(1-C\\eta)W/2,\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nwith slack below half the slab gap, equal to \\(a_1\\) deep inside and \\(a_0\\) near protected boundaries. Density remains \\(CM_\\sigma\\). Where width is constant \\(a_1\\),\n\\[\n\\nabla u_j=\\epsilon_je_i+O(\\eta),\\qquad\n|\\nabla u_j|\\le1+C\\eta,\\quad\\epsilon_j\\in\\{-1,1\\}.\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a smooth cutoff across a buffer \\(NW\\), with derivative \\(O((NW)^{-1})\\), and let \\(a(x)\\in[a_0,a_1]\\), \\(|\\nabla a|\\le C/N\\). Define\n\\[\nu_P(x)=\\frac W2\\left(1+\\frac{l_P(x)}{a(x)}\\right).\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]\nAll supporting formulas extend through the motion margins. Same-axis bands stay disjoint; independent strips have near-independent normals.</p><p>Interpolate \\(a_\\tau\\) and solve locally\n\\[\nD_x(l_P/a_\\tau)X_\\tau+\\partial_\\tau(l_P/a_\\tau)=0.\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nThere are one or two independent constraints. Their gradient matrix has a smooth right inverse; solutions have size \\(O(W)\\). A finite space-time cover includes every possibly present constraint in each local list, and a partition of unity preserves the linear equations. Take zero where \\(\\partial_\\tau a_\\tau=0\\). The compactly supported flow exists and, with larger buffers, stays in the valid neighborhoods. Forward and backward flows preserve all strip labels exactly.</p><p>Estimate parameters directly, not the uncontrolled flow:\n\\[\n\\nabla u_P=\\frac W{2a}\\left(\\nabla l_P-(l_P/a)\\nabla a\\right).\n\\]\nBecause \\(a\\ge c_0W\\), this is uniformly bounded. Broadening occurs only where \\(s_\\sigma\\asymp W\\); elsewhere use snapping. For constant \\(a_1\\), \\(\\nabla a=0\\), near-horizontal slopes and \\(W/(2a_1)=1+O(\\eta)\\) give (6.6).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 19", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Separate-color preparation and normalization, weak templates, and sharp-island modifications give the full pre-stack contract of Proposition 14, with ordinary bound (5.6), fixed boundary/chart terms, and island bound …", "statement": "<p>**Geometric realization of pre-stacks and order of constants — original Proposition 6.10.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Central starting walls have Lemma 16.2's boundary data and individual edge upper counts.</p><p class=\"li\">Weighted edge tests are selected as in Lemma 16.3.</p><p class=\"li\">Finitely many calibrated islands have the fixed data of Proposition 18.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Separate-color preparation and normalization, weak templates, and sharp-island modifications give the full pre-stack contract of Proposition 14, with ordinary bound (5.6), fixed boundary/chart terms, and island bound \\(G\\le CM_\\sigma\\). All quantitative constants are fixed before final guard gaps, mesh resolutions, and sample weights. Thus any crude compact-local integral capacities obtained from these bounds and edge-variation bounds are independent of those later choices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Proposition 16 separately by color, preserving exact boundary traces and every wall–edge upper bound. Proposition 17 gives weak collars and joint products. Proposition 18 preserves these properties on calibrated islands by common ambient changes, attached through the joint boundary collar. Compact individual walls and local finiteness give all qualitative bi-Lipschitz statements.</p><p>First fix coarse charts, compact bases, feature lengths and aspects, \\(\\eta\\), boundary-jitter slack, finite weak motions and their angle tolerances, and compression \\(\\epsilon\\). These fix normal shapes, auxiliary neighbor ratios, canonical cardinalities, finite representatives, and translation/broadening buffers. Next choose level-count tolerances and generic perturbations; fix sharp chart/jitter fractions, then smaller analytic mean errors. These choices precede guards.</p><p>Fix edge-density and variation bounds, giving candidate \\(\\int G^p\\) capacities. After guards and their gaps, choose \\(d_0\\), meshes, and all motions below spatial buffers. Boundary inverse constants do not deteriorate under thinning; interior shapes and jitter factors persist under refinement. Sample last, with \\(W/H\\) sufficiently small in finite equal-weight islands. Short allowed components only require smaller sample weights.</p><p>Microscopic widths add no capacity factor: canonical labels are estimated on fixed representatives and buffer changes use uniform forward bounds. Fine subdivision uses displacement differences, and broadening is differentiated directly. Qualitative inverse constants, pattern trivializations, and later ball extensions never enter the energy estimates. Enlarge \\(G\\) on volume-null interfaces to actual local closed-domain upper slopes for rectifiable-path coarea; volume capacities are unchanged.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 20", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One can first fix finite capacities \\(A_j\\) on a locally finite family of buffered compact barrier charts \\(U_j\\), independently of guard spacing and final resolution.", "statement": "<p>**Prior capacities and simultaneous analytic choices — original Lemma 7.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(f:\\Omega\\to\\Lambda\\) satisfies Theorem 1, with positive orientation.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(E=\\int_\\Omega|Df|^p\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A desired positive power error is fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">The geometric constructions are Propositions 4–19.</p><p class=\"li\">Capacities mean finite upper bounds for local integrals of \\(G^p\\), not Sobolev capacities of sets.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can first fix finite capacities \\(A_j\\) on a locally finite family of buffered compact barrier charts \\(U_j\\), independently of guard spacing and final resolution. Afterwards one can choose guards, meshes, jitter, and samples so that substitution of selected edge counts into Proposition 19's bounds gives\n\\[\n\\int_{U_j}G^p\\le A_j\n\\]\nas upper budgets for the eventual pre-stacks. The same data satisfy:</p><p class=\"li\">small weak costs on the exterior residual, buffered full-rank correction shells, and marked collars weighted by \\(\\ell_U/d_U\\);</p><p class=\"li\">sharp-template count tests and arbitrarily small dirty-region pre-derivative costs;</p><p class=\"li\">all null avoidances and finite-hit conditions needed by Proposition 13;</p><p class=\"li\">uniform scalar-label accuracy on each saturation cube, as specified below.</p><p>These are selected upper budgets; actual walls realizing them are constructed in Proposition 21.</p>", "proof": "<p>On each buffered compact chart include every pre-slope, even later inactive ones. Proposition 19, (7.14), and (7.18) give a finite expected crude budget \\(B_j\\), uniform in sufficiently fine resolution and guard gaps, using local \\(1+|Dq_*|^p\\) with fixed inverse-radius, aspect, and chart factors. Equal-weight regions use convolution, with fixed physical conversion. This takes powers of variation upper bounds, not high moments of raw crossing counts.</p><p>Set \\(A_j=2^{j+6}(1+B_j)\\). Markov gives capacity failure probability at most \\(2^{-j-6}\\). Make the bounded number of small aggregate objectives have expected values sufficiently below their targets that total failure probability is below \\(1/8\\). Capacity failures also sum below \\(1/8\\). Thus permissible edge jitters have positive probability.</p><p>Only now choose guards by Proposition 14's barrier using these capacities. Include high/low-region, value, and saturation requirements. On each saturation cube require\n\\[\nY=(S_D(t(q_h)),S_e(s(q_h)))\n\\tag{7.22}\n\\]\nuniformly as close to \\(Y_0\\) as prescribed relative to aligned side length, in the same sector and where activation is identity.</p><p>Properness makes relevant guard gaps finite per feature. Reserve arbitrarily small absolute gap budgets and summably small dyadic sampling belts. Choose \\(d_0\\), meshes, and motions below guard and regional buffers, with constant sharp cores. Select an allowed ACL jitter satisfying all upper budgets. Sample last; choose \\(W/H\\) sufficiently small and refine short allowed components. Lemma 14.1 gives simultaneous positive conditional probability for count tests, with summable local failure probabilities.</p><p>Intersect also full-probability exclusions for nonsingleton-value labels, scalar labels of seam-time sets, vertices, and finite-hit coarea tests, including exact collars.</p><p>The choice order, recorded in original Remark 7.5, is: \\(p\\) and error; \\(\\gamma,\\eta,\\zeta,c_*,C_w\\); affordable rank tails and jets; full-rank bands; carrier and weighted collars; polar geometry, layers, activation and central carrier; sharp bases/templates, tolerances, chart/jitter fractions and smaller mean tests; finite saturation patches; paid transitions and crude capacities; guards; final boundary widths, meshes, jitter and samples; then strict parameters, marked compression, and smoothing.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The data can be selected in the following order:", "statement": "<p>**Initial reservations, polar localization, and calibrated source patches — Sections 7.1–7.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose small rank-one aspect \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\), slot loss \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), then \\(\\zeta,c_*&gt;0\\) sufficiently small relative to \\(\\eta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The ordinary weak constant \\(C_w\\) may depend on \\(p,\\gamma,\\eta,\\zeta,c_*\\), but not later jets, charts, resolutions, or guards.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The data can be selected in the following order:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Retain compact positive deficient-rank data and compact full-rank data, with discarded \\((1+|Df|^p)\\)- or full-rank energy tails small against \\(C_w\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Reserve finitely many full-rank cubes \\(B_i\\), affine jets \\(b_i\\), and small buffered bands by Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Construct the carrier and calibrated quantizer, with compact retained set \\(K_c\\), and fix regional and marked-collar budgets.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Fix polar subdivision, full-cell layers, activation radii, exact central carrier, and a final retained \\(K^\\sharp\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">5. Select finitely many separated saturation patches with aligned bases and arbitrarily small affine-gradient mean errors, away from correction supports and marked cubes.</p><p>On active high regions, if \\(q_h=Th\\) is subsequently pinned sufficiently close to \\(q_*\\),\n\\[\n|D(P_0q_h)|\\le Cr(q_h)G.\n\\tag{7.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\gamma,\\eta\\) so the eventual leading error \\(C_p(\\eta+\\gamma)(1+E)\\) is affordable, and fix \\(C_w\\) before initial tails. Truncate positive singular values and full-rank inverse jets. Proposition 7 reserves disjoint full-rank supports; choose shell parameter \\(s\\) after finite jet bounds, so all enlarged bands \\(|t_i|\\le C's\\) have arbitrarily small original energy with correction factors. Require\n\\[\n\\sum_i\\int_{B_i}|Df-Db_i|^p\\text{ small},\\qquad\n|b_i^{-1}f-\\operatorname{Id}|\\ll cs\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nin normalized coordinates. Capture full-rank energy even inside \\(\\{t_i&lt;-3s\\}\\) up to tail.</p><p>Fix source fine tolerance before flattening, including full-rank tests. Use \\(a_\\Lambda\\le a\\circ f^{-1}/4\\). Construct Proposition 6's two rounds, with comparisons avoiding retained central data and sufficiently fine for full-rank bands. For rank one choose directions and \\(\\lambda\\) so range-axis error is \\(o(\\gamma^2)\\), and \\(a_*\\) close enough to one. Near \\(K_c\\),\n\\[\nF_b=F_\\kappa,\\qquad q_0=TJF_b,\\qquad |Dq_0|\\le C|DF_b|.\n\\tag{7.4}\n\\]\nOn \\(Z=\\Omega\\setminus\\bigcup_i\\{t_i\\le-s\\}\\), outside \\(K_c\\) and marked interiors, regional charging makes carrier energy small against \\(C_w\\). Its two enlargements stay in \\(\\{t_i\\ge-3s\\}\\). For \\(2&lt;p&lt;3\\), fix collar widths after both rounds so\n\\[\n\\sum_U\\frac{\\ell_U}{d_U}\\int_{C_U}|DF_b|^p\n\\tag{7.5}\n\\]\nis as small as required, including relevant jet weights. These weights are now fixed.</p><p>Choose paired phases to retain rectangular interiors and avoid centers and sector rays by Proposition 10. For unprotected faces fix worst finite aspect factors first, then localize original vertices where weighted \\(\\int|Dq_0|^p\\) is small by Lemma 11.1, and subdivide afterwards. Choose full-cell conical layer thickness before factor motions. Then choose activation radii \\(a_D\\) and retain compact data with \\(r&gt;5a_D\\). Proposition 12 changes labels only in \\(r(q_*)&lt;a_D/100\\). On a neighborhood of final \\(K^\\sharp\\), \\(q_*=q_0\\) and\n\\[\n|Dq_0-Df|\\le C(\\lambda+1-a_*)|Df|.\n\\tag{7.7}\n\\]\nPrescribe later label closeness and stair motion so radii are comparable and all active labels lie in high-budget neighborhoods where \\(q_*=q_0\\). Low-region outputs are constant. Proposition 15 then gives (7.8), with no switching multiplicity. In full blocks, only paid conical layers differ from \\(q_0\\).</p><p>Trim \\(K^\\sharp\\) away from coarse nonsmooth chart loci. At a retained regular point \\(z\\) in one sector put\n\\[\nY_0=(t(q_0),s(q_0)),\\qquad B=D_xY_0(z).\n\\tag{7.9}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\operatorname{rank}B=2\\), choose unit \\(k\\in\\ker B\\) and\n\\[\nV=[B^\\dagger,k/\\|B\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}],\n\\quad BV=[I_2,0],\\quad \\|V^{-1}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=\\|B\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}.\n\\tag{7.10}\n\\]\nIf \\(B=av^T\\) has rank one, \\(|v|=1\\), set \\(\\alpha=|a_1|+|a_2|\\), choose orthogonal \\(Qe_1=v\\), and \\(V=Q/\\alpha\\). Then\n\\[\nBV=\\begin{pmatrix}b_1&amp;0&amp;0\\\\b_2&amp;0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\quad |b_1|+|b_2|=1,\\quad \\min(|b_1|,|b_2|)\\le C\\gamma.\n\\tag{7.11–7.12}\n\\]\nThe rectangular estimates and \\(o(\\gamma^2)\\) direction error give\n\\[\nr(z)\\|V^{-1}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le C|Df(z)|.\n\\tag{7.13}\n\\]</p><p>In a smooth chart \\(x=g(\\xi)\\), use \\(\\xi=\\xi_z+Ay\\), \\(A=Dg(\\xi_z)^{-1}V\\). Uniform finite compact basis bounds precede errors. Choose chart/jitter fractions after geometric constants and mean errors after their density factors. Vitali select disjoint small outer aligned cubes and finite truncate, with inner saturation cubes capturing retained energy and strict sector margins. Patch transition belts are made small after their fixed factors, then resolved with \\(H\\) and later \\(W/H\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Ordinary weak costs are bounded by carrier budgets with constant \\(C_w\\), plus freely small absolute errors, uniformly over meshes sufficiently fine for all buffers and guards.", "statement": "<p>**Ambient edge tests and weak costs — original Lemma 7.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Geometry, residual envelopes, regional weights, and active high-budget neighborhoods are fixed by Lemma 20.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Paid error regions contain coarse facets, chart seams, patch and lattice transitions, nonphysical mesh pieces, and boundary modifications, with arbitrarily small weighted integrals of \\(1+|Dq_*|^p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Ordinary weak costs are bounded by carrier budgets with constant \\(C_w\\), plus freely small absolute errors, uniformly over meshes sufficiently fine for all buffers and guards. This holds simultaneously on exterior residuals, full-rank correction shells, and marked collars weighted by \\(\\ell_U/d_U\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For an initial edge portion \\(l\\), conditional sampling gives\n\\[\nW_l\\le(1+C(\\zeta+c_*))\\operatorname{Var}_l(q_*)+\\epsilon_l.\n\\tag{7.14}\n\\]\nAdd portions before normalization, since only whole-edge individual bounds survive normalization. Relevant features are finite on compacts.</p><p>In high-budget regions graph label speed satisfies\n\\[\n|D(\\text{graph labels})|\\le C_{\\mathrm{asp}}|Dq_0|/r.\n\\tag{7.15}\n\\]\nThis is stratumwise: on radial spokes perimeter-vertex labels are constant; on subedges radial-star labels are constant; derivatives vanish on vertex levels. No inverse ambient dihedral angle enters. Modified collar portions use Lemma 16.2's fixed inverse responses and restricted coarea; later interval adaptation leaves sampled central chords unchanged.</p><p>On a weak tetrahedron the evaluated active slope is\n\\[\nCr_\\sigma h_\\sigma^{-1}\\max_{l\\subset\\sigma}W_l.\n\\tag{7.16}\n\\]\nThere is no baseline when no crossing occurs. Hölder and (7.14)–(7.15) bound its \\(p\\)-cost by small errors plus\n\\[\nCh_\\sigma^3\\sum_{l\\subset\\sigma}\\int_0^1\nC_{\\mathrm{asp}}(x_l(u))^p|Dq_0(x_l(u))|^p\\,du.\n\\tag{7.17}\n\\]\nOnly deterministic active high-budget buffers need this estimate.</p><p>Jitter gives, for every nonnegative ambient integrable \\(F\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\left[h_\\sigma^3\\int_0^1F(x_l(u))\\,du\\right]\n\\le C\\int_{\\operatorname{star}^+(\\sigma)}F.\n\\tag{7.18}\n\\]\nStar overlap is bounded. Physical-bulk factors are universal; exact tapers use fixed local Lipschitz bounds in paid regions. Original-vertex aspects were paid before subdivision. Charge the residual envelope's retained-data part by captured weight and (7.4), and its complement by regional carrier budgets. Shells and weighted collars lie in reserved enlargements. Sharp transition belts are paid by Lemma 20.3. Full-cell excess layers are already small.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 20.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Edges and samples can satisfy \\( \\frac{N_{l,i}W}{H} \\le(1+C(\\zeta+c_*)) \\left|(BV)_i\\frac{\\Delta y_l}{H}\\right|+e_l, \\qquad e_l\\ge0, \\) with \\(\\sum_lH^3e_l^p\\) arbitrarily small after all fixed conversion factors.", "statement": "<p>**Sharp counts and discarded costs — original Lemma 7.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">On each enlarged calibrated patch, \\(H\\) is the common aligned Kuhn step, \\(W\\) the common root weight, and \\(g'\\) the physical chart including tiny volume jitter.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Dg'\\) is relatively close to the center basis \\(V\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B=D_xY_0(z)\\), and \\(\\Delta y_l\\) is the aligned displacement along edge \\(l\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Edges and samples can satisfy\n\\[\n\\frac{N_{l,i}W}{H}\n\\le(1+C(\\zeta+c_*))\n\\left|(BV)_i\\frac{\\Delta y_l}{H}\\right|+e_l,\n\\qquad e_l\\ge0,\n\\tag{7.19}\n\\]\nwith \\(\\sum_lH^3e_l^p\\) arbitrarily small after all fixed conversion factors. Tetrahedra failing sharp eligibility and all dirty portions of Proposition 18 have arbitrarily small aggregate evaluated pre-derivative \\(p\\)-cost.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use individual scalar variation in the one smooth sector and subtract \\(BVy\\). The edge error is controlled by \\(|DY_0-B|\\), fixed chart speed, and chart/jitter derivative error times \\(\\|B\\|\\). Normalize by \\(H\\), apply Hölder, and use (7.18). Mean errors were chosen after the fixed inverse jitter-density factors. Sampling adds the near-unit multiplicative density and freely small errors.</p><p>Fix inter-edge thresholds much smaller than \\(\\eta\\), and level thresholds \\(\\delta'\\) after \\(\\eta\\)-dependent templates. Discard tetrahedra with excessive \\(e_l\\). Independent inter-counts fit \\((1+o(\\eta))H/W\\); parallel combined ideal count is \\(H/W\\). Level ideal variation is zero, giving count \\(\\delta'H/W\\). These supply Proposition 18's fitting and nonslab-rank conditions.</p><p>For \\(m_\\sigma=1+\\max_lN_lW/H\\), write \\(m_\\sigma\\le m_0+Ce_\\sigma\\). Convolution gives\n\\[\nM_\\sigma\\le M_0+L_\\sigma,\\qquad\n\\sum_\\sigma H^3L_\\sigma^p\\le C\\sum_\\sigma H^3e_\\sigma^p.\n\\tag{7.20}\n\\]\nThreshold-bad volume pays the baseline; error convolution pays the rest. Above \\(2M_0\\), full \\(M^p\\)-cost is error-controlled. Neighbor ratios extend this to fixed surrounding layers. Thin margins pay baseline by volume and leakage by (7.20).</p><p>On low-leakage regular tetrahedra, dirty relative volume is\n\\[\nC_N(W/H+\\delta').\n\\tag{7.21}\n\\]\nTheir \\(M\\) is bounded. Choose \\(\\delta'\\), then \\(W/H\\), small after \\(N\\) and fixed factors. This pays all dirty costs; other discarded and margin portions are paid as above.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 21", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The same mesh and samples admit actual pre-stacks satisfying Proposition 14's complete contract, avoiding the selected guards, with \\( \\int_{U_j}G^p\\le A_j \\) and the weak and sharp bounds of Lemmas 20.2–20.3.", "statement": "<p>**Actual initial walls with selected budgets — original Proposition 7.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Guards, final mesh, samples, and count upper budgets are those selected in Proposition 20.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F_*,M_x,M_y\\) are the central carrier data.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The same mesh and samples admit actual pre-stacks satisfying Proposition 14's complete contract, avoiding the selected guards, with\n\\[\n\\int_{U_j}G^p\\le A_j\n\\]\nand the weak and sharp bounds of Lemmas 20.2–20.3. Therefore all hypotheses of conditional wall realization hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal W\\) be the entire sampled standard two-color wall family. It is locally finite with compact proper walls, so its union is relatively closed. Countably many nonsingleton values forbid only countably many radial or intermediate meridian labels. The selected samples avoid them on whole walls.</p><p>For the original carrier and final edges, ACL/coarea gives finite hits off the exact collar. Volume jitter makes nonexact seam-time sets null, and ACL makes their scalar-label images null; samples avoid these labels and mesh vertices. In the exact collar, original carrier labels are locally Lipschitz, and additional coarea tests give finite hits without numerical budget. Thus Proposition 13 applies.</p><p>Choose one common opening \\(f_o\\), retaining exactly the original hits of both colors on every edge, sufficiently fine for guard buffers, and equal to \\(h_*\\) on the whole boundary-preparation support. Pull back both standard families by this one homeomorphism. They have the required compact proper locally flat types and essential annular cores.</p><p>Apply the joint boundary warp, chord preparation, mirrored continuation, and depth stretch only in that exact neighborhood. Its counts are precisely the prepared counts already used in selection; outside it opening preserves original counts. Interval-ribbon adaptation leaves central sample chords fixed. Hence no sample-dependent pulled-back edge is being estimated.</p><p>Apply Lemma 16.4 by color, then Proposition 16 by color. Neither increases an individual edge count. Proposition 19 gives pre-stacks, so selected count-based budgets bound actual \\(\\int G^p\\). Guard avoidance follows because normalized disks occur only in tetrahedra incident to original possible hits; all placements, collars, snapping, and preliminary changes remain in the reserved spatial buffers. On volume-null interfaces enlarge \\(G\\) to actual adjacent closed-domain slopes, preserving every volume budget.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 22", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "On the union of saturation interiors, \\( \\int|D(P_0Th)-Df|^p \\le C_p(\\eta+\\gamma)(1+E)+\\text{freely small errors}. \\) The leading constant is independent of compact jet and chart bounds.", "statement": "<p>**Saturation and physical gradient accuracy — original Lemma 7.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Apply Proposition 14 to the pre-stacks of Proposition 21, with Proposition 20's capacities and label accuracies.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\) is the resulting locally bi-Lipschitz realization.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P_0\\) is Proposition 15's nonstrict target map.</p><p class=\"li\">Saturation cubes, center matrices \\(B\\), bases \\(V\\), and charts \\(g'\\) are from Lemma 20.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On the union of saturation interiors,\n\\[\n\\int|D(P_0Th)-Df|^p\n\\le C_p(\\eta+\\gamma)(1+E)+\\text{freely small errors}.\n\\tag{7.23}\n\\]\nThe leading constant is independent of compact jet and chart bounds.</p>", "proof": "<p>On an aligned saturation cube \\(Q\\), write\n\\[\n\\widetilde Y=Y\\circ g',\\qquad D_0=BV.\n\\]\nPropositions 18 and 15 give, away from dirty portions,\n\\[\n|D_y\\widetilde Y|\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n\\sqrt2+C\\eta,&amp;\\operatorname{rank}D_0=2,\\\\\n1+C\\eta,&amp;\\operatorname{rank}D_0=1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{7.24}\n\\]\nAt essential independent crossings the two gradients are near-orthogonal; on a switched diagonal one scalar is their signed sum, with the same squared bound. Parallel bands are disjoint. Dirty evaluated \\(p\\)-costs are arbitrarily small by Lemma 20.3, including fixed physical weights.</p><p>The mean gradient is close to \\(D_0\\). For \\(Y_0\\circ g'\\), use affine-gradient and chart/jitter tests. For the difference, the cube boundary formula gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\fint_QD_y((Y-Y_0)\\circ g')\\right|\n\\le\\frac C{\\operatorname{side}Q}\n\\|(Y-Y_0)\\circ g'\\|_{L^\\infty(Q)},\n\\]\nsmall by (7.22). Continuous Sobolev traces justify the formula.</p><p>Use the paper's uniform-convexity inequality\n\\[\nc_p|Z-D_0|^p\n\\le |Z|^p-|D_0|^p\n-p|D_0|^{p-2}D_0:(Z-D_0),\\qquad p&gt;2.\n\\tag{7.26}\n\\]\nIt follows by integrating the Hessian of \\(|X|^p\\) along the segment: after scaling the difference to unit length, a fixed-length subinterval stays a fixed distance from the possible zero of its projection. In rank two \\(|D_0|=\\sqrt2\\); in rank one (7.11)–(7.12) give \\(1-C\\gamma\\le|D_0|\\le1\\). Integrate with \\(Z=D_y\\widetilde Y\\). Near-sharp energy and pinned mean yield\n\\[\n\\int_Q|D_y\\widetilde Y-D_0|^p\n\\le C_p(\\eta+\\gamma)|Q|+\\text{mean and dirty errors}.\n\\tag{7.27}\n\\]</p><p>There is no activation transition on these cubes. Reconstruct in the single smooth sector by\n\\[\nR(t,s)=d_D+e^{t/L_D}(\\xi_e(s)-d_D).\n\\]\nIts derivative columns are bounded by \\(Cr\\), independently of thin-rectangle aspect. Require relative chart error \\(\\|(Dg'-V)V^{-1}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\ll1\\). Then\n\\[\n\\|DR(\\widetilde Y)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\|(Dg')^{-1}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\n\\le Cr(z)\\|V^{-1}\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le C|Df(z)|,\n\\tag{7.28}\n\\]\nand the Jacobian is relatively close to \\(|\\det V|\\).</p><p>Split the physical derivative into the term containing \\(D_y\\widetilde Y-D_0\\) and\n\\(DR(\\widetilde Y)D_0(Dg')^{-1}\\). The latter is arbitrarily close to \\(Dq_0(z)\\), then to \\(Df(x)\\), by label, affine-gradient, chart, and (7.7) tests. Freely small errors absorb finite factors. The leading contribution is precisely\n\\[\nC_p(\\eta+\\gamma)|Df(z)|^p|\\det V||Q|.\n\\]\nDisjoint outer patches and their accurate tests imply\n\\[\n\\sum_Q|Df(z_Q)|^p|\\det V_Q||Q|\\le C(E+1).\n\\]\nSummation proves (7.23).</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 12, "cite"], [1, 11, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 9, "cite"], [2, 31, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 1, "cite"], [6, 11, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [7, 19, "cite"], [7, 35, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [10, 6, "cite"], [10, 15, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 24, "cite"], [12, 19, "cite"], [12, 20, "cite"], [13, 12, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [13, 22, "cite"], [13, 38, "cite"], [14, 12, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 12, "cite"], [16, 12, "cite"], [17, 19, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [17, 23, "cite"], [17, 25, "cite"], [17, 35, "cite"], [17, 64, "cite"], [18, 48, "cite"], [18, 49, "cite"], [19, 28, "cite"], [19, 29, "cite"], [19, 65, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [20, 28, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [21, 35, "cite"], [21, 36, "cite"], [21, 58, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [22, 58, "cite"], [23, 19, "cite"], [23, 59, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"], [24, 64, "cite"], [24, 65, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [25, 66, "cite"], [26, 24, "cite"], [26, 67, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [27, 64, "cite"], [29, 28, "part"], [30, 28, "cite"], [31, 0, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [31, 35, "cite"], [31, 37, "cite"], [31, 47, "cite"], [31, 64, "cite"], [31, 65, "cite"], [32, 35, "cite"], [32, 37, "cite"], [32, 64, "cite"], [33, 32, "cite"], [33, 65, "cite"], [34, 32, "part"], [35, 36, "cite"], [35, 37, "cite"], [35, 64, "cite"], [35, 65, "cite"], [36, 37, "cite"], [36, 64, "cite"], [36, 68, "cite"], [37, 0, "cite"], [37, 45, "cite"], [37, 53, "cite"], [37, 56, "cite"], [37, 63, "cite"], [37, 64, "cite"], [37, 68, "cite"], [37, 69, "cite"], [38, 37, "cite"], [38, 51, "cite"], [38, 56, "cite"], [38, 64, "cite"], [39, 44, "cite"], [40, 37, "cite"], [40, 41, "cite"], [41, 43, "cite"], [42, 43, "cite"], [43, 37, "cite"], [44, 37, "cite"], [45, 0, "cite"], [45, 64, "cite"], [45, 65, "cite"], [45, 69, "cite"], [46, 45, "cite"], [47, 45, "cite"], [48, 53, "cite"], [48, 63, "cite"], [48, 64, "cite"], [48, 68, "cite"], [49, 58, "cite"], [50, 51, "cite"], [50, 53, "cite"], [50, 63, "cite"], [50, 66, "cite"], [51, 63, "cite"], [52, 68, "cite"], [53, 59, "cite"], [53, 63, "cite"], [53, 64, "cite"], [54, 53, "cite"], [55, 53, "cite"], [55, 59, "cite"], [56, 63, "cite"], [56, 64, "cite"], [56, 67, "cite"], [56, 69, "cite"], [57, 56, "cite"], [58, 56, "cite"], [59, 56, "cite"], [60, 56, "cite"], [60, 61, "cite"], [61, 56, "cite"], [62, 56, "cite"], [63, 64, "cite"], [63, 68, "cite"], [64, 0, "cite"], [64, 69, "cite"], [66, 0, "cite"], [66, 68, "cite"], [67, 66, "cite"], [67, 68, "cite"], [67, 69, "cite"], [68, 0, "cite"], [68, 64, "cite"], [68, 69, "cite"], [69, 0, "cite"]]});
